-## C library, time and random numbers
-
-### Time zone rules, calendar conversion and the network clock
-
-- **Symptom:** saves dated 18:00:02 31/12/1969 (`ToCalendarTime: first conversion 2 → 1969-12-31 18:00`), and Continue, which takes the newest save, loads an older one.
-- **Cause:** the host failed `time::StandardNetworkSystemClock::GetCurrentTime`, and the game kept the PosixTime it had (2). A Switch keeps its network clock from its last time sync, so one that has ever been online reads it offline too. The SDK's `StandardNetworkSystemClock::now()` never calls `GetCurrentTime`: it reads the time service once its own initialized flag is set, which the host's `Initialize` take-over never sets, and returns 0 (1970) otherwise ([Ocarina of Time](../../dm/games/0400486024DEC000/README.md)'s `sdk+0x5d8490`).
-- **Fix:** the network clock is the host's clock, as the user clock (`DM_FIXED_TIME` included), since the host's clock is set from the network: `GetCurrentTime`, `now()`, and the network fields of the host's clock snapshots ([below](#steady-clock-clock-snapshots-and-user-clocks)). `t_time` checks all three. Also hooked: `time::ToPosixTime` (host `mktime`), and `time::ToPosixTime` with an explicit rule (the host's zone, as for `ToCalendarTime`); `time::LoadTimeZoneRule` and the `ToCalendarTime` overload with a rule (the host's zone is used); `time::StandardNetworkSystemClock::IsAccuracySufficient` (no: the host does not know when its clock last synced). The network clock's context and the adjustable clocks follow the same rule ([below](#the-adjustable-network-clock-aborts-in-adjust)). `time::GetDayOfWeek` is a pure computation and needs nothing.
-- **Reach:** `main` calls the network clock in [Breath of the Wild](../../dm/games/01007EF00011E000/README.md), [Tears of the Kingdom](../../dm/games/0100F2C0115B6000/README.md), [Mario Kart World](../../dm/games/0400C3F00006E000/README.md), [Minecraft Dungeons II](../../dm/games/0100A7C01B792000/README.md), [PRAGMATA](../../dm/games/0400C76024710000/README.md), Super Mario Party and [Ocarina of Time](../../dm/games/0400486024DEC000/README.md) (`now()` too); [Pokémon Sword](../../dm/games/0100ABF008968000/README.md) and [Shield](../../dm/games/01008DB008C2C000/README.md), [Brilliant Diamond](../../dm/games/0100000011D90000/README.md) and [Animal Crossing: New Horizons](../../dm/games/01006F8002326000/README.md) import it.
-- **Found on:** [Divinity: Original Sin 2](../../dm/games/010027400CDC6000/README.md) (SDK 8.2.0; first brought up in DroidPlay Core, an Android fork that shares this host, from [PR #2359](https://github.com/seedds/nxnative/pull/2359): the network clock); [13 Sentinels: Aegis Rim](../../dm/games/01003FC01670C000/README.md) (SDK 11, `ToPosixTime`); [Monster Hunter Rise](../../dm/games/0100B04011742000/README.md) (SDK 11, the rest); [Octopath Traveler 0](../../dm/games/01005270232F2000/README.md) (`GetDayOfWeek`); [Pokémon Legends: Z-A](../../dm/games/0100F43008C44000/README.md) (19.3.11, `ToPosixTime` with an explicit rule, found in the imports before it was met).
-
-### The C library's local time is UTC, as on a Switch
-
-- **Symptom:** times a game shows through the C library are off by the host's UTC offset: Hades II's load screen dated a save made at 01:44 KST (UTC+9) 16:44 the day before, its UTC time. The save file itself holds the right time.
-- **Cause:** the host took over `localtime`, `localtime_r` and `mktime` with its own local-time functions, on the belief that the SDK converts by the system time zone. It does not: the SDK's musl time zone helper (`__secs_to_zone`) is a fixed stub (offset 0, no daylight saving, name `"UTC"`) in every `sdk` checked, SDK 3.5.0 to 22.2.10 (Xenoblade Chronicles 2 to EA SPORTS FC 27, the Switch 2 builds included). Local time on a Switch comes only from `nn::time`. Hades II turns its save header's time into a UTC `struct tm` with `gmtime_r`, back into seconds with `mktime`, then into a calendar with `time::ToCalendarTime`. The host's `mktime` read the UTC `tm` as local time, 32,400 s early in KST, and `ToCalendarTime` added the offset back once. The take-over came from the D2R port, whose chronicle times (`localtime` + `strftime`) differed from the wall clock by the zone's offset. They do on a Switch too.
-- **Fix:** the take-overs are gone, so the SDK's own functions run. The pass-through table (`hle_table.c`), used only when no module defines them, maps `mktime` to the host's `timegm` and `localtime_r` to `gmtime_r`. `nn::time` keeps converting in the host's zone ([above](#time-zone-rules-calendar-conversion-and-the-network-clock)). `t_time` checks both with `TZ=Asia/Seoul`.
-- **Found on:** [Hades II](../../dm/games/0100A00019DE0000/README.md) 1.0.141667 (SDK 20.5.15; [issue #2784](https://github.com/seedds/nxnative/issues/2784): `gmtime_r` from `main+0x4301bc`, `mktime` from `main+0x430a4c`, `ToCalendarTime` from `main+0x430d18`).
-
-### Steady clock, clock snapshots and user clocks
-
-- **Symptom:** SDK calls that reach the time service, each aborting in `sm::Initialize` (or found in the imports before they were met).
-- **Fix:**
-  - `time::StandardSteadyClock::GetCurrentTimePoint`: host monotonic seconds, fixed source ID; `GetSpanBetween` runs in `sdk` ([The Legend of Zelda: Breath of the Wild](../../dm/games/01007EF00011E000/README.md) 1.9.0, SDK 20.5.22).
-  - `time::ClockSnapshot::CreateWithStandardSystemClock` and `CreateWithAdjustableSystemClock`: 0xD0 bytes, filled on the host from the steady clock, the user clock and the calendar; the network fields are the user clock's, as the network clock is ([Time zone rules, calendar conversion and the network clock](#time-zone-rules-calendar-conversion-and-the-network-clock)), so two host snapshots take their span from their steady time points and one made on a Switch from the network times. The type byte at `+0xcd` is 1 for standard and 2 for adjustable snapshots (0 means uninitialized), as verified in [Tears of the Kingdom's SDK](../../dm/games/0100F2C0115B6000/README.md#services). The adjustable entry also dereferences the absent time-service session without a host take-over. `t_time` checks both registrations, guarded buffers, the adjustable calendar and location, and snapshot arithmetic. The adjustable crash and boot fix were reported for Tomodachi Life: Living the Dream in [PR #385](https://github.com/seedds/nxnative/pull/385); that gameplay report has not been repeated here.
-  - The adjustable user clock (`time::AdjustableUserSystemClock::GetCurrentTime`, which the game's days follow) reads the time service's shared memory in the SDK (`MonotonicSystemClock::GetCurrentTime`, `sdk+0x4421cc`), which the host never maps, so it is the host's clock, as the user clock; `time::GetStandardSteadyClockCurrentTimePoint`, the free-function form of the steady clock ([Animal Crossing: New Horizons](../../dm/games/01006F8002326000/README.md) SDK 20.5.4, found in `main`'s imports before the first boot).
-  - `time::StandardSteadyClock::now()`, the C++ clock form: a time point of seconds in `x0`. The SDK asks the steady-clock session `time::Initialize` opens and returns 0 without one (SDK 3.7.0, `sdk+0x16edf4` in [Mega Man Legacy Collection](../../dm/games/01002D4007AE0000/README.md), which calls it from six sites in `main`); the host takes over `Initialize` and opens none, so the clock never moved. It is the host's monotonic seconds, as `GetCurrentTimePoint`. Reported in [PR #2729](https://github.com/seedds/nxnative/pull/2729).
-- **Found on:** [The Legend of Zelda: Breath of the Wild](../../dm/games/01007EF00011E000/README.md), [The Legend of Zelda: Tears of the Kingdom](../../dm/games/0100F2C0115B6000/README.md), [Animal Crossing: New Horizons](../../dm/games/01006F8002326000/README.md), [Mega Man Legacy Collection](../../dm/games/01002D4007AE0000/README.md).
-
-### The adjustable network clock aborts in `Adjust`
-
-- **Symptom:** `game called nn::diag abort: Result 0x74, call site sdk+…` within the first frames, the backtrace's first `sdk` frame right past `time::AdjustableNetworkSystemClock::Adjust` (its last instruction is the call of `diag::detail::AbortImpl`, so the return address names the next function, `GetSystemClockContext` of the same class). Any other reader of an adjustable network clock, or the adjustable user clock's `now()`, aborts in `diag::detail::AbortImpl` under `time::detail::MonotonicSystemClock`.
-- **Cause:** `Adjust` copies `StandardNetworkSystemClock::GetSystemClockContext` into the SDK's `detail::MonotonicSystemClock` and aborts on its failure; the SDK asks the time service, which the host never opens, and gets 0x74. Past that, every adjustable reader goes through `MonotonicSystemClock::IsAdjusted`, `GetCurrentTime` or `GetCurrentTimespec`, which turn the stored context into a time through the time service's shared memory, which the host never maps, and abort without it (Nintendo Switch Sports Resort's `sdk+0x5d96e0`..`+0x5d985c`). The adjustable network clock's `GetCurrentTime`, `now()` and `GetSystemClockContext` each call `IsAdjusted` first; the adjustable user clock's `now()` calls `GetCurrentTime`. `GetStandardNetworkSystemClockCurrentTimespec` (SDK 17) reads the shared memory too and returns 0x74.
-- **Fix:** on the host the network clock is the user clock ([Time zone rules, calendar conversion and the network clock](#time-zone-rules-calendar-conversion-and-the-network-clock)) and each adjustable clock is its standard clock, in every reader. `hle_time.c` answers `StandardNetworkSystemClock::GetSystemClockContext`, `AdjustableUserSystemClock::GetSystemClockContext` and `AdjustableNetworkSystemClock::GetSystemClockContext` with the user clock's context (the one the host's snapshots carry at `+0x00` and `+0x20`), `AdjustableNetworkSystemClock::GetCurrentTime` and the `now()` of both adjustable clocks with the host's clock, and both network `…CurrentTimespec` getters with the host's real time (`DM_FIXED_TIME` included). `Adjust` runs as is and only sets the SDK's copy. `t_time` checks each of them against a snapshot, the contexts within their 0x20 bytes.
-- **Found on:** METAL GEAR SOLID: Peace Walker - Master Collection Version (`0100C6F01C4F8000`, 1.3.1, SDK 19, not in the games table), program 1, which its launcher starts: it aborted in `Adjust` (`sdk+0x42c514` on that build) right after its first frames. Found and diagnosed by varyform in [PR #1875](https://github.com/seedds/nxnative/pull/1875), whose first fix (a zero network context) ran it on for 90 seconds (2100 frames) with sound on an M3 Max; the fix as landed has not been run on that game here (no game files).
-
-### A periodic benefit claim without the time service session
-
-- **Symptom:** `Break#1 reason=0 Result=0 svc at sdk+0x370f24` right after the first save at the title, with `bt: sdk+0x175f00 sdk+0x175fc4 sdk+0x176060 sdk+0x375b88 main+0x6ea74 …` (`diag::detail::OnAssertionFailure` called from `time::ClaimPeriodicBenefitWithUserSystemClock`).
-- **Cause:** the public `time::ClaimPeriodicBenefitWithUserSystemClock` (a once-a-day reward) asserts that `time::g_pStaticService`, the time service session `time::Initialize` opens, is set; the host takes over `Initialize` and opens none. The work is in `time::detail::ClaimPeriodicBenefitWithUserSystemClock`, which only compares the snapshots' fields through `CalculateSpanBetween` and `CalculateStandardUserSystemClockDifferenceByUser` (both host take-overs) and asks the service two things: when automatic correction was last switched, if the context's snapshot and this one differ in it (`+0xcc`), and the device's location and when it was set, if they differ in location name. Host snapshots never have automatic correction and always carry the host's zone name, but a claim context saved on a Switch (a location such as `Asia/Tokyo`) or a Mac whose zone changed reaches both, again with no session.
-- **Fix:** an `HLE_FORCE` on the public entry calls the SDK's `detail::` implementation directly (`hle_time.c`), and logs `time: periodic benefit claim → <result>`; past its session check the public entry does only that (SDK 6.5 and 20.5). The two service questions are taken over too: `detail::GetDeviceLocationNameAndUpdatedTime` answers the host's zone name, as its snapshots carry, and both it and `detail::GetStandardUserSystemClockAutomaticCorrectionUpdatedTime` answer a cleared time point. The host does not know either moment, and the claim uses such a time point only when its source ID is the current snapshot's steady clock's, which a cleared one never is.
-- **Found on:** Tetris 99 (010040600C5CE000) 2.5.0, SDK 6.4 (the same `sdk` module as [ASTRAL CHAIN](../../dm/games/01007300020FA000/README.md)), from a provisional profile: the claim returned 0, then 1 on the next check, and the title screen kept running for three minutes at about 56 fps.
-
-### Hooks whose return type did not match the SDK
-
-- **Symptom:** a call the host answers reads as failed when it succeeded, or reads leftover register bits. Super Smash Bros. Ultimate refills the Spirit Board at every visit, since the elapsed time between two snapshots never reads as known.
-- **Cause:** four take-overs declared another return type than the SDK's own function: `time::CalculateSpanBetween` returns bool (true when the span is stored), and the host returned a Result 0; `nifm::HandleNetworkRequestErrorResult` returns bool, and the host returned a Result whose low bit happened to be 0; `audio::AcquireAudioDeviceNotificationForOutput` and `pl::BeginVrMode` return a Result, and the host's were `void`. Checked in the `sdk` of SDK 12 to 20 (Metroid Dread `sdk+0x3ab540`, `+0x336210`, `+0x534a0`; Breath of the Wild `sdk+0x412970`).
-- **Fix:** each returns what the SDK does: the span call 1 or 0; the network call 0 (not resolved, offline); the notification 0 the first time and 0x499 (already acquired) after; `BeginVrMode` 0xd08e, the SDK's own answer when `pctl::CheckStereoVisionPermission` refuses, which the host now does too, having no VR display (Breath of the Wild's options then stay 2D, as MacNativeGames found). `t_time` checks the span call's values.
-- **Found on:** reported by MacNativeGames on [Super Smash Bros. Ultimate](../../dm/games/01006A800016E000/README.md) (the Spirit Board) and Breath of the Wild 1.6.0 / 1.9.0 (the VR option); the Spirit Board was not replayed here.
-
-### `clock_gettime(CLOCK_REALTIME)` through the time source pointer
-
-- **Symptom:** `std::terminate` (`Break` from `sdk+0x5bfa48`) at the first real-time read (right after the first-boot dialog, `main+0x3f3f68` → `system_clock::now()`).
-- **Cause:** `clock_gettime(CLOCK_REALTIME)` calls through a source pointer (`sdk+0xb05010`) that `nntimeStartup` sets once `time::Initialize` succeeds: 1 = `nn::time::GetStandardUserSystemClockCurrentTimespec`, 2 = the adjustable clock's (`sdk+0x8e414`). Both asked the time service.
-- **Fix:** they are host take-overs now (`hle_time.c`), and `system_clock::now()` works on any build.
-- **Found on:** [Ace Attorney Investigations Collection](../../dm/games/010005501E68C000/README.md) (1.0.0.2, SDK 17.5.3).
-
-### Guest clock reads wait while the app is in the background
-
-- **Symptom:** after switching apps and back, one guest thread at 100% in a tight loop (`main+0x9abd0`, `x10 = 0`); the game freezes, picture still, sound on. Mac repro `DM_OE_SCRIPT=bg=1@20,bg=0@25`.
-- **Cause:** the frame pacing measured a frame period of 0 (`main+0x5926a8`: the clock difference between two frames). The host freezes the guest clock in the background but its threads went on, so two frames read the same time.
-- **Fix:** guest reads of the system counter (the `cntpct` gate, `svc GetSystemTick`) now wait while the app is in the background (`hle_os.c` `guest_clock_read_wait`); host work and the keyboard freeze ([The software keyboard blocks only its caller](#the-software-keyboard-blocks-only-its-caller)) keep the frozen clock.
-- **Found on:** [Super Mario Galaxy 2](../../dm/games/0100FD8022DAA000/README.md) (one of two causes; the other is [Focus handling mode decides whether focus changes are notified](#focus-handling-mode-decides-whether-focus-changes-are-notified)).
-
-### Cryptographically random bytes
-
-- **Symptom:** abort in `sm::Initialize` under `nn::crypto::GenerateCryptographicallyRandomBytes` (Unicorn Overlord: `std::random_device` → the `sdk`'s own `getentropy` → `InitializeCsrng`; Z-A: the first call in `nnMain`, `main+0x1734b4`).
-- **Cause:** the bytes come from the csrng service. The `sdk` defines `getentropy`, so the host's `getentropy` hook never sees the call, but the call into `nn::crypto` goes through the `sdk`'s PLT.
-- **Fix:** an `HLE_FORCE` on it fills from the host's entropy stream, the same stream as `getentropy` (`hle_os.c`).
-- **Found on:** [Unicorn Overlord](../../dm/games/010069401ADB8000/README.md) (SDK 16.2.4; one boot), [Pokémon Legends: Z-A](../../dm/games/0100F43008C44000/README.md) (19.3.11; one boot).
-
-### `clock_gettime` is `nn::time`'s steady clock (SDK 22)
-
-- **Symptom:** `libc++abi: terminating due to uncaught exception of type std::__1::system_error` (the arguments came out as rubbish, the format named the failure) once the game's own code ran, with `sdk+0x5f2e60` (`std::terminate`) and `sdk+0x66ad2c`/`sdk+0x66acc8` (`get_terminate`/`terminate`) in the backtrace.
-- **Cause:** SDK 22's exported `clock_gettime` (`sdk+0xa1430`) accepts `CLOCK_REALTIME` and `CLOCK_MONOTONIC` and returns `ENOTSUP` for ids 2–11; both accepted ids jump through musl ifunc slots in the SDK's data (`sdk+0xba5018`, `sdk+0xba5020`) whose `R_AARCH64_GLOB_DAT` relocations bind `nn::time::GetAdjustableUserSystemClockCurrentTimespec`, `_ZN2nn4time41GetStandardUserSystemClockCurrentTimespecEP8timespec` and **`_ZN2nn4time37GetStandardSteadyClockCurrentTimespecEP8timespec`** (`sdk+0xb92298`). The first two were answered from [the CLOCK_REALTIME entry](#clock_gettimeclock_realtime-through-the-time-source-pointer); the steady one was not, so the SDK's shared-memory client ran, could not read the time service's page, and returned an error, which `std::chrono::steady_clock::now()` (`sdk+0x5f2e10`: `mov w0,#1; bl clock_gettime; cbnz w0` → `std::__1::__throw_system_error`) turned into the exception.
-- **Fix:** `hle_time.c` answers `GetStandardSteadyClockCurrentTimespec` with the host's monotonic clock (`struct timespec` from `clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC)`). The network-clock variants answer the host's real time, as the network clock is the user clock ([The adjustable network clock aborts in `Adjust`](#the-adjustable-network-clock-aborts-in-adjust)). To name such a failure, `hle_sdk16.c` takes `nn::diag::detail::VAbortImpl` (an assert, an uncaught C++ exception or an SDK check with a message): it logs `[diag] abort at <function>: <expression> (<file>:<line>) message format <format>` and then aborts as `AbortImpl` does. Only the format is printed: the host cannot expand a `va_list` the guest built.
-- **Check:** with `DM_AUDIO=1`, a run to the first room has no `[diag]` line and the frame counter keeps advancing.
-- **Found on:** [Well Dweller](../../dm/games/0100217023F6C000/README.md) (SDK 22.2.0), in `std::chrono::steady_clock::now()` called from GameMaker's frame-timing helper.
-
-## Modules and symbols
-
-### Host symbol indexes avoid repeated name scans
-
-- **Symptom:** relocation repeatedly scans all HLE and forced-takeover entries, and named-stub reuse becomes quadratic as more imports are missing.
-- **Cause:** module exports already had a name index, but `resolve_import`, `hle_implements`, `force_lookup` and stub reuse still searched their tables linearly.
-- **Fix:** open-addressed name indexes store entry numbers. HLE duplicates retain the first entry; forced-takeover duplicates still abort. Stub insertion grows its index above half capacity and retains existing stub numbers. Resolution is serialized at startup and by the module write lock during runtime NRO loading; module string tables stay mapped across unload.
-- **Found on:** [The Legend of Zelda: Breath of the Wild](../../dm/games/01007EF00011E000/README.md#found-on-this-game), reported in [PR #261](https://github.com/seedds/nxnative/pull/261).
-- **Check:** `make -C dm/host/tests GAME=010015100B514000 symbols nro` covers collisions that wrap around the table, duplicate precedence/rejection, absent sections and names, counting-wrapper selection, all 4,096 stubs through growth and reuse at capacity, and NRO relocation/lifecycle. The symbol test also passes AddressSanitizer and UBSan. A local arm64 Mac synthetic 270-entry HLE table with 25% misses, Apple clang 21 at `-O1`, took 375–442 ns per linear lookup versus 15 ns indexed (25–29x; three runs of 360,000 lookups). This measures lookup cost, not overall game loading time; no new gameplay run here.
-
-### Global symbol resolution in load order
-
-- **Symptom:** a library's `free` rejects a pointer (`CentralHeap::Free` → `NotNMallocPtrError`).
-- **Cause:** the game's `main` defines its own `operator new` / `delete` (Unity's memory manager) and `sdk` has a copy forwarding to `malloc` / `free`.
-- **Fix:** symbols are resolved globally in load order (the first module's definition wins, also for a module's references to its own exports); only local / non-default-visibility symbols bind to the module itself (`loader.c`). The loader logs how many references bind to an earlier module.
-
-### Undefined weak imports bind to 0
-
-- **Symptom:** a weakly imported function the game calls although nothing defines it: an `UNIMPL` line, then an abort.
-- **Cause:** the loader bound every undefined import to a named stub, so the address test the compiler emits for a weak symbol saw a function.
-- **Fix:** an undefined weak symbol that no module defines and the host does not implement is now 0, as Horizon's rtld binds it (`loader.c`, logged as `undefined weak … bound to 0`).
-- **Found on:** [Octopath Traveler 0](../../dm/games/01005270232F2000/README.md) (the `thread_local` init function `_ZTHN13LowLevelTasks5FTask10ActiveTaskE`).
-
-### `nn::ro` and its port
-
-- **Symptom:** crash in `nn::ro::InitializeWithPortName`.
-- **Cause:** `nn::ro` connects to `ldr:ro`.
-- **Fix:** take over `Initialize` and answer `LookupSymbol` from the loaded modules (`hle_ro.c`). Check the mangled names against the new title's imports: SDK 19's `LoadModule` takes one more bool (`_ZN2nn2ro10LoadModuleEPNS0_6ModuleEPKvPvmib`), so the hook for the older form did not catch it and the SDK's own code would have run.
-- **Found on:** [Hades II](../../dm/games/0100A00019DE0000/README.md) (SDK 19's `LoadModule`).
-
-### `ro::ProtectRelro` runs natively
-
-- `nn::ro::ProtectRelro` (called from `main`'s DSO init), a new import of SDK 20, needs nothing: it runs natively on the host's no-op `SetMemoryPermission` / `SetMemoryAttribute`. Found on [The Legend of Zelda: Breath of the Wild](../../dm/games/01007EF00011E000/README.md) (1.9.0, SDK 20.5.22).
-
-### Loading NROs with `ro::LoadModule`
-
-- **Symptom:** the frame loop runs, but the game waits for code it loads itself (`ro::RegisterModuleInfo` / `ro::GetBufferSize` red lines; `main` imports 1,045 `lua2cpp::create_agent_*` functions, global, that no ExeFS module defines, bound to stubs at start).
-- **Cause:** the fighters' code is 98 NROs inside `data.arc`, registered by `.nrr/cross2_*.nrr`. The SDK's own `LoadModule` (SDK 8.2.1 `LoadModuleInternal`, `sdk+0x347460`) uses the service only to map the image, then zeroes bss, relocates through rtld's module lists, calls the module's init and binds the variables of the modules loaded before; the host replaces rtld, so it takes over the whole call.
-- **Fix:** the NROs are collected at pack time by the SHA-256s the NRRs list (`dm/tools/nro_collect.py`: loose files, the current files of an indexed archive, or a scan for stored and zstd-framed NROs; Smash's NRRs of older releases also register 666 superseded copies, which `data.arc` still holds and which are left out), rewritten like any module (`patch_nso.py` reads NROs: the flat image, segments at +0x20, build ID at +0x40) and listed in `<patched dir>/nro.tsv`. The host maps them all at start and keeps them out of symbol lookups; `LoadModule` finds one by the image's SHA-256, restores its data and bss (after the first load), relocates it against the modules loaded then in load order, binds the earlier modules' symbol relocations it defines (main's `lua2cpp` stubs), fills `nn::ro::Module` as the SDK does (+0 module, +8 state 1, +0x10 image, +0x18 buffer) and calls its `DT_INIT`; `UnloadModule` calls `DT_FINI` and binds every relocation that pointed into it again (`loader.c` "NRO modules", `hle_ro.c`). `GetBufferSize` returns the bss size from header +0x38 as the SDK does. The startup init pass must skip NROs (their init ran unrelocated and crashed). Only data is written at a load, so it works with code embedded in the iOS executable: `gen_embed.py` packs the NROs into three sections (`__dm_nro_x0`, `__DM_NRO_W`, `__dm_nro_x1`) and a table of their pieces (`__dm_nro_tab`), since a Mach-O file has at most 255 sections and three per NRO would pass it, and `map_image` maps an NRO from there (`loader.c` `nro_pieces`).
-- **Found on:** [Super Smash Bros. Ultimate](../../dm/games/01006A800016E000/README.md) (13.0.5): it loads six NROs in its first 30 s and calls none of the stubs. Played on an iPhone.
-
-- **`.nrolz`:** NERD's Hagi keeps a recompiled game as an NRO packed as a big-endian u32 size, then one LZ4 block ([Super Mario Sunshine (Super Mario 3D All-Stars)](../../dm/games/010049900F546002/README.md): `data/gekko/MarioSunshine/nx/MarioSunshine.nrolz`), which it unpacks and hands to `ro::LoadModule`, so its SHA-256 is the unpacked image's. `dm/tools/nro_collect.py` unpacks such files too and matches them against the NRRs.
-### NRO tables sized from the manifest and snapshots taken on demand
-
-- **Symptom:** `FATAL .../nro.tsv: more NROs than module slots (MAX_MOD 128)` before `nnMain` (GitHub issue [#372](https://github.com/seedds/nxnative/issues/372)); after increasing the capacity, startup eagerly copies several GB of inactive NRO data and bss.
-- **Cause:** the host used fixed 128-entry module and activation tables. It also allocated and copied every NRO's full writable image at startup so reload could restore it, including initially zero bss.
-- **Fix:** count the manifest once before loading and allocate both tables at their final size, preserving every `module_t` address. Keep each NRO's initialized data only when it is first loaded, before relocation and initialization. Reload restores that data and zeroes bss; unused NROs need no snapshot. The original NRO header supplies the initialized data size.
-- **Found on:** [FINAL FANTASY X/X-2 HD Remaster](../../dm/games/0100BC300CB48000/README.md), 1,432 NROs plus three initial modules; 5,836 MB of writable data and bss versus 767 MB of initialized data across all NROs. Most effect modules stay inactive on a short route.
-- **Check:** `make -C dm/host/tests GAME=01007BB017812000 nro` exercises manifest sizing, the last slot of a 1,435-slot table, load order, initialized-data restoration and zeroed bss after reload.
-
-### Online SDKs in NROs are never loaded
-
-- **Symptom:** `[EOS] Failed to create platform` (the game plays offline); `refused …: an online SDK (Epic Online Services), not loaded`; `refused an NRR that registers only online SDKs (1 hashes)`.
-- **Cause:** Epic Online Services in an NRO (`rom:/nro/EOSSDK-Switch-Shipping.nro`, loaded with `ro::LoadModule`; `main` imports the `EOS_*` functions). Before the host loaded NROs, the imports stayed `UNIMPL` stubs and `EOS_Platform_Create` handed back a garbage handle; the game checks it and plays offline.
-- **Fix:** the host now loads NROs ([Loading NROs with `ro::LoadModule`](#loading-nros-with-roloadmodule)), but never an online SDK, whatever the game: an NRO that exports `EOS_Platform_Create` is left out of the pack (`dm/tools/nro_collect.py` `ONLINE_SDKS`, a `refused` row in `nro.tsv`) and `ro::LoadModule` refuses it with one log line (`refused …: an online SDK (Epic Online Services), not loaded`), also by the export when an older `nro.tsv` has it. An NRR that registers only such modules fails `RegisterModuleInfo`, as every registration did before the host loaded NROs, so the game takes the path it took then. So both games behave as before: offline, local saves.
-- **Found on:** [Absolum](../../dm/games/0100A83022A22000/README.md) (SDK 18.3.2, one boot each); [Hades](../../dm/games/0100535012974000/README.md): it never asks for the module (checked on 1.0.38233: `refused an NRR that registers only online SDKs (1 hashes)`, no `LoadModule`, its frames as before). Hades' `main` imports only EOS Auth, Connect and PlayerDataStorage, Epic-account cross-save the host cannot serve; the real SDK would at best fail its login and at worst wait on time-outs or contact Epic's servers, with an unknown risk to the player's account.
-- **Note:** open question for the maintainer: always load, refuse online SDKs (as now), or decide per profile.
-
-### GPU driver modules that are never loaded
-
-- **Symptom:** `main` imports EGL (SDL3's GL support) and `vkGetInstanceProcAddr` (its Vulkan support), but the game renders with NVN (`FNA3D_Driver_NVN.cpp`).
-- **Cause:** NVIDIA's OpenGL and Vulkan drivers (`subsdk1`, `subsdk0`) program the GPU through NvRm, which the host does not have.
-- **Fix:** they are never loaded (`profile_guess.py` `GPU_DRIVERS`); the EGL imports stay unbound stubs that nothing calls.
-- **Found on:** [Absolum](../../dm/games/0100A83022A22000/README.md) (FNA3D).
-
-### Modules only the NROs import from
-
-- **Symptom:** `UNIMPL _ZN5movie12SetAllocatorEPFPvmmS0_EPFvS0_S0_EPFS0_S0_mS0_ES0_`, `UNIMPL glslcInitialize`, then `UNIMPL _ZN5movie9ExtractorC1ENS_13ContainerTypeE` and a crash reading address 0x18 in an NRO (`pc=nro18+0x97f4`) as the first movie starts.
-- **Cause:** the provisional profile loads a subsdk only when `main` imports something from it that the host does not supply. The survey extracts only the code modules, so the NROs in the RomFS were not looked at. Portal's `main` is a launcher with SDL; its Source engine NROs play the movies through NintendoSDK_movie (`subsdk2`, imported by `video_nx.nro`) and initialize and call GLSLC (`subsdk0`, imported by `materialsystem.nro` and `video_nx.nro`). Left out, their imports were bound to stubs, and the stub constructor of `movie::Extractor` left the object empty.
-- **Fix:** once the rewrite stage has collected the NROs, the NSP script adds to a provisional profile every subsdk whose exports an NRO imports and that no loaded module, other NRO or host hook supplies (`profile_guess.py` `nro_modules`, written under a section `After the NRO collection`). NVIDIA's GPU drivers stay out, as above.
-- **Found on:** [Portal](../../dm/games/01007BB017812000/README.md) (`modules main subsdk0 subsdk2 sdk`; `subsdk1`, NVIDIA's OpenGL driver, stays out).
-
-### A module the NROs import from, missing from the build row
-
-- **Symptom:** a game with a provisional profile stays black where its opening movie plays; the log has `modules of main's build …: main sdk (the profile lists 3)`, and `movie::Extractor`'s constructor is unimplemented.
-- **Cause:** since the host loads the modules of the `build` row for main's build ([A derived profile leaves out a module that moved to another slot](packing.md#a-derived-profile-leaves-out-a-module-that-moved-to-another-slot)), a module in `modules` alone is not loaded. The NRO step above added its subsdks to `modules` only, and the provisional profile's row, written by the survey, named the modules `main` imports from.
-- **Fix:** after the NRO collection, `provisional_modules` (`scripts/build_ipa_from_nsp.py`) writes `modules` and completes the row of this ExeFS's main build in one write: every module of `modules` the ExeFS has, with its build ID. It runs on every build of a provisional profile, so a profile written before is repaired; the rows of other versions packed from the same folder are left as they are. A changed row rebuilds the Mac host, which has the rows compiled in.
-- **Found on:** [Mario Golf: Super Rush](../../dm/games/0100C9C00E25C000/README.md) (4.0.0, issue #814), from the PR author's run ([PR #2215](https://github.com/seedds/nxnative/pull/2215)): `subsdk0` (NintendoSDK_movie) in `modules` and not in the row; with it in the row the opening movie plays (4,921 frames decoded) and the title follows; on the Mac here too (M6, 2026-11-13).
-
-### `ro::LookupModuleSymbol` for a name the module does not define
-
-- **Symptom:** `[RED] ro::LookupModuleSymbol BuiltDebug: not defined in that module` once at boot; nothing goes wrong after it.
-- **Cause:** the Source engine asks each module it loads for `BuiltDebug`, which only its debug builds define. `ldr:ro` fails such a lookup on Horizon too, and the game takes the failure as "release build".
-- **Fix:** the host's answer was already Horizon's (a failure result). The line is no longer red when the module is a loaded one; a lookup in a module the host does not know stays red (`hle_ro.c`).
-- **Found on:** [Portal](../../dm/games/01007BB017812000/README.md).
-
-### IL2CPP common string table intact
-
-- Not needed in the end: the tolerant common string table of pull request #15. [Ace Attorney Investigations Collection](../../dm/games/010005501E68C000/README.md) 1.0.0.2's `main` has the table intact at `main+0x46169ac`, 109 entries ending at `Hash128`.
-
-### Code inherited from another title at fixed addresses
-
-- **Symptom:** harmless on the build it was written for, silent memory corruption on any other.
-- **Cause:** code that calls fixed module addresses. d2r-mobile's 3D render scale read a renderer pointer at `main+0x31e0848` and called `main+0x8582f0`; in Mario v0 that is the middle of a static initializer and only did nothing because the pointer happened to read 0.
-- **Fix:** such code checks `module_t.build_id` now (written into `fixups.tsv` by the rewriter) and stays off on other builds.
-- **Found on:** [Super Mario Bros. Wonder](../../dm/games/010015100B514000/README.md) (v0, code inherited from d2r-mobile).
-
-### Built-in fonts and the audio device notification of SDK 21
-
-- **Symptom:** an abort connecting to `pl:u` at boot; a failed `nn::audio` call at boot.
-- **Cause:** SDK 21 has new names: `nn::pl::GetBuiltInFontData` (a Switch 1 and a Switch 2 overload, each filling type, address and size, and returning whether the font is loaded), `nn::pl::GetBuiltInFontDataSet` (the same for a list of types, a Switch 2 and a Switch 1 overload: at most 6 and 7 types, one byte each, `*count` entries of 0x18 bytes in order, returning whether the fonts are loaded; the Switch 1 overload sets `*count` only when they are) and `nn::audio::AcquireAudioOutputDeviceNotification`. Unhooked, they reach the services the host does not have.
-- **Fix:** all hooked (`hle_sdk16.c`, `hle_audren.c`), with the same stand-in fonts and the same never-signaled event as their older names. Both font-set overloads fill each type as `GetBuiltInFontData` does, in the order asked, and leave out a type with no stand-in font (`t_shared_font`).
-- **Found on:** [Splatoon Raiders](../../dm/games/0400380020056000/README.md); `GetBuiltInFontDataSet` on [Kirby and the Forgotten Land](../../dm/games/01004D300C5AE000/README.md)'s Nintendo Switch 2 Edition (program `04004D300C5AE001`, at boot).
-
-### The Joy-Con mouse pointer
-
-- **Symptom:** an abort in `nn::hid` at boot, in a Switch 2 game that sets up the Joy-Con's mouse mode.
-- **Cause:** `InitializeNmouse` opens the hid service, and the host has neither the service nor the sensor.
-- **Fix:** `InitializeNmouse`, `GetNmouseHandles` (none), the button configuration calls and the merged pointer's calls (a handle with no states) are hooked (`hle_hid.c`). SDK 21's forms too: `InitializeNmouse` with a pointer type, the pointer range, movement area and restriction (kept or dropped), the USB mouse's button configuration and handle (none), and the calls on a handle, which return the SDK's bool (SDK 22.2.0 `sdk+0x6d5510`: its result is success): the merged handle 0 takes the pointer settings and has no state and no controller; any other handle is unknown.
-- **Found on:** [Fire Emblem Fortune's Weave](../../dm/games/04007EF01F8AE000/README.md), [Pokémon Pokopia](../../dm/games/0400A3A01EE4C000/README.md). SDK 21's forms: [Mario Kart World](../../dm/games/0400C3F00006E000/README.md) 1.8.0 at boot, from pull request #1116 (that build is not run here).
-
-### The Joy-Con 2 mouse sensor and pointer
-
-- **Symptom:** a Switch 2 game's mouse mode does nothing with Joy-Con 2s: its pointer stays still, or a game that offers its mouse modes only to Joy-Con 2 controllers finds none.
-- **Cause:** the host answered the sensor's calls as having no sensor (`GetJcmsStates` none, `GetJcmsState` a cleared state, `GetNmouseHandles` none), and a Joy-Con 2 pad had only the plain Joy-Con styles. A Switch 2 game can also support only the Joy-Con 2 forms of the Joy-Con styles: style bits 12, 13 and 14 for JoyDual, JoyLeft and JoyRight. Those bits are inferred: Nintendo Switch 2 Welcome Tour's mouse exhibits support only `0x1000` and test a pair with `0x1004`, a left Joy-Con with `0x2008` and a right one with `0x4000` beside JoyRight.
-- **Fix:**
-  - A front end reports each Joy-Con's sensor with `dm_joy_mouse` (`dm_input.h`): the movement since its last report and whether it is connected and on a surface. A Joy-Con 2 pad says it is one (`dm_joy_attach` with `DM_JOY_2`), and its npad then shows the Joy-Con 2 style beside the plain one to a game that supports it (`hle_hid.c`).
-  - `GetJcmsStates` / `GetJcmsState` read a ring of 16 states per player and Joy-Con. A JcmsState is 32 bytes: 8 bytes the game does not read, the sampling number at 8 (newest first), the movement at 0x10 and 0x14, the attributes at 0x18 (bit 0 connected, bit 1 on a surface). A JcmsType is 0 for the left Joy-Con and 1 for the right.
-  - The pointer (Nmouse): `GetNmouseHandles` gives a handle per Joy-Con whose sensor reports, the one last used on a surface first (it stays first while lifted, to press a button), since Welcome Tour moves its pointer by the first handle only. `GetNmouseState` fills a 48-byte NmouseState: 8 bytes, then the Switch's MouseState fields (sampling number, position on the game's pointer range, movement, wheel), the buttons at 0x28 (left 1: R or ZR on a right Joy-Con, L or ZL on a left one; right 2) and the attributes at 0x2c (bit 1 connected, which the game requires). `SetNmousePointerPosition` moves it. The movement is a quarter of the sensor's counts.
-  - The Switch 2 transport turns the sensor on with its own feature bit, `0x10` (`S2_FEATURE_MOUSE`, `switch2_proto.h`), only once the game has set up the sensors or the pointer (`InitializeJcms` / `InitializeNmouse`, `dm_joy_mouse_wanted`), as the console does for a game that uses them; a controller that connected before is sent the two feature commands again with the bit (not yet checked on hardware), and keeps it on while connected. Measured on a right Joy-Con 2 slid on a desk: report bytes 0x10 and 0x12 are 16-bit position counters that wrap (x right, y toward the player). 0x14 and 0x16 jump from about 1,600 and 150 on a surface to about 4,600 and 3,200 when lifted, and 0x16 under 1,000 counts as on a surface. While lifted, no movement is reported. The Pro Controller 2 has no sensor.
-  - `DM_JCMS_LOG=1` logs the game's sensor and pointer calls, at most once a second each kind.
-- **Found on:** [Nintendo Switch 2 Welcome Tour](../../dm/games/040057C02159A000/README.md): its cursor and its mouse exhibits, with a left and a right Joy-Con 2. `t_hid` checks the states, the handles and their order, and the styles.
-
-### A private application's HID scene
-
-- **Symptom:** a null read at `sdk+0x65c75c` during startup, after `[RED] sf: the game connects to service hid itself, which the host does not serve`.
-- **Cause:** SDK 20.5.29's `nn::hid::SetSceneIdForPrivateApp(int)` calls the HID service's scene setter through its absent session. Welcome Tour's flow NRO calls it before its first frame.
-- **Fix:** `hle_hid.c` owns the scene setting across modules. The setter records the integer and `GetSceneIdForPrivateApp()` returns it; neither opens Horizon IPC. The controller transport does not use this scene label.
-- **Found on:** [Nintendo Switch 2 Welcome Tour](../../dm/games/040057C02159A000/README.md), update v262144 (`sdk+0x65d3b0`, from `nro12+0x3e38`).
-
-### Home button protection on a Switch 2 controller
-
-- **Symptom:** a Switch 2 game crashes as a Joy-Con 2 joins: `[RED] sf: the game connects to service hid itself, which the host does not serve`, then `CRASH sig=11 addr=0` at `sdk+0x63ce10`, called from `nn::hid::EnableUnintendedHomeButtonInputProtection`.
-- **Cause:** `nn::hid::EnableUnintendedHomeButtonInputProtection(const NpadIdType&, bool)` turns on a guard against a Home press made by accident on that controller. It returns nothing; the SDK's own sets the flag through the hid service, which the host does not serve, and calls through the null session.
-- **Fix:** the host answers it (`hle_hid.c`), records the setting per npad (No 1-8, Other, Handheld) and logs it (`hid: EnableUnintendedHomeButtonInputProtection npad 0 on`). The question, `IsUnintendedHomeButtonInputProtectionEnabled(const NpadIdType&)`, whose SDK function asks the service too, answers what the game set, off by default. The host handles the Home button itself ([home_quit.h](../../dm/host/home_quit.h)), so nothing else changes.
-- **Found on:** Drag x Drive (040041601D83A000, v196608, SDK 20; no profile in the repository yet): 43 s in, as player 1's Joy-Con 2 pair joined (`main+0x2c80c4`). [Nintendo Switch Sports Resort](../../dm/games/04008E001F098000/README.md) (`04008E001F098000`, v65536) asks the question at boot: the SDK's own aborted on its error (`Result 0x58cca` at `sdk+0x6ca40c`).
-- **Check:** `make -C dm/host/tests GAME=<id> run` (`t_hid`).
-
-### GameShare guest displays
-
-- **Symptom:** a crash at boot in `nn::vi` under `nn::streamplay::CreateIndividualDisplays`.
-- **Cause:** a game can create its guest displays at boot, before any session, and the call starts a second display service the host does not have.
-- **Fix:** `CreateIndividualDisplays`, `DestroyIndividualDisplays` and `GetNativeWindowHandle` answer that there is no guest (`hle_misc.c`).
-- **Found on:** [Pokémon Pokopia](../../dm/games/0400A3A01EE4C000/README.md).
-
-### GameChat at boot
-
-- **Symptom:** `[RED] sf: the game connects to service chat:u itself, which the host does not serve`, then `game called nn::diag abort: Result 0xe15` under `nn::chat::Initialize`.
-- **Cause:** a Switch 2 game can initialize GameChat at boot. The SDK's `Initialize` opens `chat:u`, and its other calls go through the service object that `Initialize` makes.
-- **Fix:** `nn::chat::Initialize` (ready, no chat), `IsChatting` (no) and `SetScreenSharingMode` (nobody to share with) are hooked (`hle_misc.c`). Another GameChat call a game imports needs a hook of its own: each of the SDK's calls checks a count only its own `Initialize` raises, and aborts otherwise. Also hooked: `AttachToRoomInfoChangedEvent` (bound, never signaled), and the camera video storage, local and remote. SDK 22.2.0 (`sdk+0x6b43c0`) checks 32 to 1920 by 32 to 1080 pixels, 1 to 4 images and a page-aligned buffer, and zeroes the 0xe0-byte storage type. With no camera each image is a blank native image of that size, zeroed memory with room for 16 bytes a pixel (`hle_codec_video.c` `dm_native_image_blank`), so the game's NVN textures on them (`nvnTextureInitializeFromNativeImage`) are made as on a decoded picture ([AV1 movies through SDK 22's decoder](movies.md#av1-movies-through-sdk-22s-decoder)). The storage type keeps their handles, and the game's buffer is one page that stays unused.
-- **Found on:** [Hyrule Warriors: Age of Imprisonment](../../dm/games/04003F40232DE000/README.md). The room event and the camera storage: [Mario Kart World](../../dm/games/0400C3F00006E000/README.md) 1.8.0 (three local images of 384x216 at boot), from pull request #1116 (that build is not run here).
-
-### GameChat queries from the title screen (SDK 21)
-
-- **Symptom:** `CRASH sig=11 addr=0 pc=sdk+0x6be4d4` in `nn::chat::GetChattingUid()`, from the game's title screen code.
-- **Cause:** SDK 21 games ask the chat library who is chatting, for the room, the sharing key, the virtual camera, the members' audio levels and the camera streams; the SDK's versions read the service object `Initialize` would have made.
-- **Fix:** hooked as "no chat" (`hle_misc.c`): a zero Uid, `GetRoomInfo` fails (with `RES_FAIL`, the chat library's real result not being known), the sharing and virtual camera calls succeed and keep nothing, the camera streaming calls succeed and stream nothing, the next-image requests fail, no audio levels.
-- **Found on:** [Star Fox](../../dm/games/0400E88025B1A000/README.md): 33 `nn::chat` imports.
-
-### The camera through the graphics system
-
-- **Symptom:** `nn::diag abort` in `gs::sdk::CheckGraphicsSystemInitialized`, or a failed `camera::sdk::CreateCameraContext` (0x3af3), at boot.
-- **Cause:** the Switch 2's camera (a USB camera, for GameChat) is a session the SDK asks the graphics system's user session (`gsv`) for. The host never opens that session, and the SDK's check that its graphics system is booted reads a flag only the SDK's own graphics boot sets.
-- **Fix:** `CheckGraphicsSystemInitialized` passes (`hle_nv.c`): the host's graphics layer is up from the start. `CreateCameraContext` gives a host session (`hle_sf.c`), a 32-bit handle (SDK 22.2.0 `sdk+0x2c42c0` writes it), that answers as a console with no camera. Every command fails with 0xdc4, except command 21, the camera's event, which copies the read end of a kernel event that never fires (`hle_thread.c` `hle_new_event`) in the response's special header. The game is said to take 0xdc4 from command 3, opening the camera, as "no camera"; any other error aborts.
-- **Found on:** [Mario Kart World](../../dm/games/0400C3F00006E000/README.md) 1.8.0, from pull request #1116, not checked here (that build is not on this Mac): the meaning of 0xdc4 and the commands are inferred there from the game's code. `t_sf` checks the session's answers.
-
-### The camera commands of SDK 21's own camera module
-
-- **Symptom:** with the camera session answering 0xdc4 to every command, Star Fox logs `This platform does not support the camera function.` (its `vwebcam.switch2.cpp:997`) and dereferences a null on the way out (`main+0xba54e4c`); with 0x7c4 to every command its module aborts in `Initialize` (subsdk3+0x1ef4) or `Finalize` (subsdk3+0x200c).
-- **Cause:** the game ships its own camera module (`subsdk3`, `nn::camera`) and sorts the results itself. `Initialize` sends command 3 with the device name `Default` and takes success or 0xdc4, the latter passed to the game as "no camera support", a path a console with a camera port never takes; `QueryDeviceConnection` (command 29, a DeviceRequest) reads 0x7c4 as `No camera device detected.`; `Finalize` sends command 4 and aborts on any failure; the connection event is command 21.
-- **Fix:** `camera_answer` (`hle_sf.c`) answers commands 3 and 4 with success, 21 with an event that never fires, and the others, 29 among them, with 0x7c4, so the game sees a console whose camera port has nothing plugged in. `nn::applet::BeginUsingCameraResource` / `EndUsingCameraResource` (the module takes the applet's camera resource) and `nn::camera::sdk::DestroyCameraContext` succeed too; the SDK's versions read service objects the host never made. The client also ends the session with an HIPC Close (message type 2), answered with the closed-session result 0xf601 (`hle_sf.c`); before that it logged `[RED] sf: camera: HIPC message type 2 not served`. Mario Kart World 1.8.0, whose reading of 0xdc4 the old answer followed, is not re-tested here.
-- **Found on:** [Star Fox](../../dm/games/0400E88025B1A000/README.md).
-
-### Whether the language selection is limited
-
-- **Symptom:** a null read at boot in `nn::oe::IsLanguageSelectionLimited`, right after the game asks for its language.
-- **Cause:** the SDK asks the `am` session at vtable +0x238, and the host never opens that session (`oe::Initialize` is taken over).
-- **Fix:** hooked: no limit; the host's language is `DM_LANG` alone (`hle_oe.c`).
-- **Found on:** [Hyrule Warriors: Age of Imprisonment](../../dm/games/04003F40232DE000/README.md).
-
-### Application GPU scheduling time
-
-- **Symptom:** a null read in `nn::os::SdkMutexType::Lock`, at `sdk+0x151fb0`, called from `nn::gs::sdk::QueryApplicationGpuTimeSpan` after the first rendered frame.
-- **Cause:** the query asks the graphics server's session (command 27), which the host never opens, how much of an interval the GPU was given to the application rather than to another of the console GPU's clients (the system's own rendering). Its two times are nanoseconds on the system tick clock; callers pass intervals bracketed by their own GPU counter timestamps.
-- **Fix:** `hle_nv.c` answers the whole interval (`end - begin`, 0 if `end` is not later) with success. The host's counter timestamps are its model of the console GPU's timeline ([GPU timestamps per submission segment](frame-rate.md#gpu-timestamps-per-submission-segment)), on which the application is the only client. It is not the GPU's busy time: Welcome Tour's Bezel profiler queries each render pass's GPU region (`main+0x30a8f4`) and the whole frame's (`main+0x30a808`), and the frame load its dynamic resolution reads is the frame's scheduled time, plus the time the application was not scheduled since the previous frame, plus 0.36 ms. With busy time the GPU's idle time between frames would count as taken, and every frame would read as full. The earlier stub returned `0xaf3` (not even the SDK's own unavailable-session result, `0x30af3` at `sdk+0x41200c`); Bezel then took each region's length and left out the 0.36 ms margin.
-- **Found on:** [Nintendo Switch 2 Welcome Tour](../../dm/games/040057C02159A000/README.md), update v262144 (`sdk+0x411dc0`, called from `main+0x30a840`).
-
-### The target frame rate (graphics frame length)
-
-- **Symptom:** a null read at boot in `nn::os::SdkMutexType` under `nn::gs::sdk::SetGraphicsFrameLengthMode`, called from `nn::oe::SetGraphicsTargetFrameRate`.
-- **Cause:** `SetGraphicsTargetFrameRate` maps up to 59 fps to mode 1, up to 119 to mode 2 and above to mode 3 (`SetGraphicsTargetFrameRateAuto` to mode 0) and calls `SetGraphicsFrameLengthMode`, which locks the gs graphics server's context and sends it the mode. The host has no graphics server, so the context is null.
-- **Fix:** `SetGraphicsFrameLengthMode` is hooked and logs the mode once per change; both `oe` paths reach it. The host paces by the window's present interval (`hle_oe.c`).
-- **Found on:** [Hyrule Warriors: Age of Imprisonment](../../dm/games/04003F40232DE000/README.md): 60 / its present interval at boot.
-
-### The display's largest buffer
-
-- **Symptom:** a Switch 2 game presents at 720p, with its upscaler at the matching low size.
-- **Cause:** the game chooses its output from `nn::vi::QueryDisplayBufferMaxResolution` (its tables: 0 is 720p, 1 is 1080p, 2 is 1440p, 3 is 4K), and what the game got before the hook gave it 720p.
-- **Fix:** `hle_vi.c` answers as a Switch 2 does: in handheld mode its own 1080p screen; in console mode the largest of the four that the display the game is shown on holds, as a docked Switch 2 does with its TV. The display's size comes from the front end (`front_display_pixels`: on the Mac the window's screen in its current mode, an iPhone's or iPad's screen), 1080p when it is not known. The host logs the answer once.
-- **Found on:** [Fire Emblem Fortune's Weave](../../dm/games/04007EF01F8AE000/README.md): its DLSS output is 2048x1152 for 1440p and for 4K, from 1024x576; for 1080p it renders 960x540 into 1920x1080, a fifth fewer pixels at each stage, and the output matches the screen.
-
-### A game that decodes its code at run time
-
-- **Symptom:** `registered NROs not found` from `nro_collect.py` in a game whose RomFS has an NRR, or `[RED] ro::LoadModule: no rewritten NRO with SHA-256 …`. `main` is small (about 600 KB of code) beside a data segment of random-looking bytes.
-- **Cause:** `main` is a boot stub. [Mario Tennis Fever](../../dm/games/0400D4201C6EA000/README.md)'s (`nxboot`) starts six threads, one per stream, which unscramble and unpack the game's code out of `main`'s data segment into one buffer; it then registers the RomFS's NRR and calls `nn::ro::LoadModule` on that buffer. No file holds the NRO, so nothing can rewrite it offline, while iOS runs no code loaded at run time and the host only loads rewritten copies.
-- **Fix:** the host's run of the stub is the decoder. `DM_NRO_CAPTURE=<folder>` writes the image of a `LoadModule` it has no rewritten copy of to `<folder>/<SHA-256>.nro` and ends the run (`hle_ro.c`). The NSP script (`capture_nro`) boots the rewritten `main` and `sdk` so when `nro_collect.py --missing-ok` lists a registered hash no file holds, about 2 s on the Mac, checks the image against the hash the NRR registers, adds it to the collected NROs and rewrites. A build then runs like any game with NROs, and the shader split takes the folder (`DM_NRO_DIR`) as well as `main`. Decisions that follow what the game imports count the NROs too (`dm_game_imports`): this `main` imports no `ScalableFontEngineHelper::Decode`, its NRO does.
-- **Check:** `scripts/build_ipa_from_nsp.py <folder> --platform mac` after removing `patched_*` and `nro_*` from the build folder: rewrite, capture, rewrite, split.
-- **Found on:** [Mario Tennis Fever](../../dm/games/0400D4201C6EA000/README.md).
-
-### NRR version 1
-
-- **Symptom:** a crash in `ro::RegisterModuleInfo`, or an NRR that seems to register millions of hashes: the words at +0x340 and +0x344 are random.
-- **Cause:** an NRR with 1 in the byte at +5 (the Switch 2's) has a header 0x180 bytes longer. The same fields follow it: program ID +0x4b0, size +0x4b8, hash table +0x4c0, its count +0x4c4.
-- **Fix:** `hle_ro.c` and `nro_collect.py` read the table by that byte.
-- **Found on:** [Mario Tennis Fever](../../dm/games/0400D4201C6EA000/README.md).
+# Services and SDK calls
+
+SDK calls and system services the host answers (`dm/host/hle_*.c`). Each entry: symptom, cause, fix, the games it was found on. The [symptom index](../agents/symptoms.md) lists every symptom.
+
+## Service connections
+
+### SDK calls that open a system service through sm
+
+- **Symptom:** `sm::Initialize` aborts right after `ConnectToNamedPort` (SVC 0x1F) and `SendSyncRequest` (0x21); abort inside `nn::sm::Initialize`, the log showing `SVC 0x1f` (ConnectToNamedPort, port name `sm:`) and `SVC 0x21` answered by placeholders, then an `nn::diag` abort; abort with no message at start-up in a service call the host has no hook for (`Break` from `sdk+…` with an `nn::diag` backtrace).
+- **Cause:** an SDK path reached a system service the host doesn't replace: the SDK's IPC to the service fails and it calls `AbortImpl`. Often it is an SDK call the host does not hook, for example a newer overload of a hooked call.
+- **Fix:** hook the public SDK entry point named in the backtrace (`HLE_FORCE` in the relevant `hle_*.c`). Symbolize the abort backtrace against the SDK's exports to find the caller (resolve the frames with `sdis.py --sym` on the decompressed module) and hook that name. List the game's imports of that service the host does not hook ([Listing a game's unhooked imports before they are met](#listing-a-games-unhooked-imports-before-they-are-met)) and add the ones that talk to a service; library helpers that only fill the game's own buffers need nothing. Look at how the game uses the result before choosing it (a `bcat` mount failure is the game's own "nothing delivered" path).
+- **Found on:** [Super Mario Galaxy](../../dm/games/010099C022B96000/README.md) (SDK 18): `nn::hid::InitializeTouchScreen(int, int)`, the SDK 18 overload of the hooked `InitializeTouchScreen()`; [Super Mario Odyssey](../../dm/games/0100000000010000/README.md) (SDK 7): `account::OpenPreselectedUser`, then `bcat::Initialize`, then `audio::AcquireAudioDeviceSwitchNotification`, and a null read in `account::GetLastOpenedUser`.
+- **Note:** the same abort from other services: `ec::InitializeForShopServiceAccessors` ([Shop service accessors](#shop-service-accessors)), `pctl::IsFreeCommunicationAvailable` ([Parental controls and free communication](#parental-controls-and-free-communication)), `oe::FinishStartupLogo` ([The startup logo](#the-startup-logo)), `hid::VibrationNode::Update` ([Vibration values flushed by the SDK's vibration graph](#vibration-values-flushed-by-the-sdks-vibration-graph)), `audio::GetAudioDeviceOutputVolume` ([Audio device output volume and names](audio.md#audio-device-output-volume-and-names)), `capsrv::InitializeForApplication` ([Album calls fail with no album](#album-calls-fail-with-no-album)), `settings::LanguageCode` ([Language codes](#language-codes)), and the rest of this file.
+
+### SDK 3.6.0 answered as SDK 4
+
+- SDK 3.6.0 booted to its frame loop with the provisional profile as generated and no host change: the services, the NVN API version (53, as for every SDK up to 10), the audio output and the heap all answered as for SDK 4.
+- **Found on:** [Bayonetta 2](../../dm/games/01007960049A0000/README.md) (PlatinumGames' engine in its Wii U form): what it needed was graphics ([Clears of attachments larger than the render pass](rendering.md#clears-of-attachments-larger-than-the-render-pass), [`Downsample` of a single-sample texture](rendering.md#downsample-of-a-single-sample-texture)) and a reader for its CRI CPK archives ([BNSH files in PlatinumGames DAT archives inside CRI CPK archives](shaders.md#bnsh-files-in-platinumgames-dat-archives-inside-cri-cpk-archives)); [Bayonetta](../../dm/games/010076F0049A2000/README.md), on the same `sdk` and `subsdk0` builds, then needed no change at all (its profile is Bayonetta 2's with its own build row and tables, and the route from the boot to the first fight logged no red line). [OCTOPATH TRAVELER](../../dm/games/010057D006492000/README.md)'s SDK 3.5.1 answered as SDK 4 as well (NVN 53.313); its audio renderer needed the update lock found without `SdkMutexType` ([No `SdkMutexType` in SDK 3](audio.md#no-sdkmutextype-in-sdk-3)).
+
+### Listing a game's unhooked imports before they are met
+
+Most service calls can be found before they crash, before the first boot or the next one: the calls that would abort in `sm::Initialize` (or read a null service object) are in the import list.
+
+- **Procedure:** list `main`'s `nn::` imports (its undefined imports) that no host hook names, by namespace: the survey's import table (`nso_syms.py`), then `main`'s undefined `nn::` symbols that no hook (`HLE` / `HLE_FORCE`) names ([02](../agents/02-survey.md#imports-vs-host-hooks)), and read each one's SDK code. This gives the calls a game will make before they are met. The import list itself says where to look: diff the undefined symbols against the closest title the host already runs (and every other game's imports), drop what the host hooks, and the rest are the candidates. To find which `sdk` function aborted, map the backtrace offsets to the nearest exported symbol ([The Legend of Zelda: Tears of the Kingdom](../../dm/games/0100F2C0115B6000/README.md) 1.4.3).
+- **What needs nothing:** most `nn::os` / `nn::mem` imports run natively in `sdk`. Calls that are pure computations need nothing, nor do socket calls behind a `Socket()` that already fails offline, nor library helpers that only fill the game's own structures.
+- **Hook names:** hooks are `HLE_FORCE`, `SSF`, `SKF`, `IKB_IGNORE` across `dm/host` and `dm/gfx`. `strings` misses names the symbol table stores as the tail of a longer one, so list them with `nso_syms.dynsyms`.
+- **The survey table's blind spots:** it counts only names given to `HLE` / `HLE_FORCE` directly: hooks made through macros (`SKF` and `SSF` in `hle_net.c`, `TOUCH(N)` in `hle_hid.c`) show as missing. The touch-screen states (`GetTouchScreenState<16>`) were already hooked by such a macro, which the import table does not see. It once listed every vi call as unhooked: `nso_syms.py host_hooks` read only `dm/host`, and the vi hooks are in `dm/gfx` (it reads `dm/gfx` and `dm/gfx/nvn` now; found on [Absolum](../../dm/games/0100A83022A22000/README.md)).
+- **What it found** (the calls themselves are in the entries of their service below):
+  - [Pokémon Sword](../../dm/games/0100ABF008968000/README.md) (SDK 7, one boot each): the import list against the hooks and every other game's imports named all of them beforehand: 33 `nn::` imports no host hook names and no other game imports.
+  - [13 Sentinels: Aegis Rim](../../dm/games/01003FC01670C000/README.md) (SDK 11).
+  - [Monster Hunter Rise](../../dm/games/0100B04011742000/README.md) (SDK 11, one boot each): the import list against the hooks and the other SDK 11 games found most of them before the first boot.
+  - [DRAGON QUEST XI S](../../dm/games/01006C300E9F0000/README.md).
+  - [Octopath Traveler 0](../../dm/games/01005270232F2000/README.md): the pure computations were the swkbd presets, the web argument constructors, `time::GetDayOfWeek` and the audio resampler.
+  - [DRAGON QUEST VII Reimagined](../../dm/games/0100A9D01C446000/README.md) (SDK 19.3.1): the import list against the hooks gave the rest before they were met; the rest fill the game's own structures or sit behind a socket that fails offline.
+  - [Pokémon Legends: Z-A](../../dm/games/0100F43008C44000/README.md) (19.3.11): the import list against the hooks (the survey's `nso_syms.py` table, then `main`'s undefined `nn::` symbols that no `HLE_FORCE` names) left 86 candidates; most only fill the game's own structures (error and web argument builders, `prepo` report buffers, `swkbd` configuration, the audio resampler, `nn::async`). The ones that reach a service were hooked before they were met where the answer is clear: `time::ToPosixTime` with an explicit rule, `album::SaveCurrentScreenshot`, `err::ShowApplicationError` and the `ShowShopAddOnContentList(UserHandle)` overload.
+  - [STEINS;GATE RE:BOOT](../../dm/games/01008BC024FDE000/README.md) (SDK 20.5.17): `nn::audio::SetAudioOutVolume` ([AudioOut volume kept per output](audio.md#audioout-volume-kept-per-output)) was among `main`'s imports that no host hook names; the others there fill the game's own structures or run in `sdk` (the CPU audio renderer, the swkbd and hid argument builders).
+  - [Paper Mario: The Thousand-Year Door](../../dm/games/0100ECD018EBE000/README.md) (SDK 15.3.2): `nn::settings::GetAvailableLanguageCodes` was the one import of `main` that no host hook names and that reaches a service; `fs::MountRamDisk` and the swkbd and hid helpers it also imports run in `sdk` or were never called.
+  - [Catherine: Full Body](../../dm/games/0100BF00112C0000/README.md) (SDK 8.2.1): besides `GetAvailableLanguageCodeCount`, the other imports no hook names run in `sdk` (the `LanguageCode` comparison, `ClockSnapshot`'s constructor and getters, fibers, `NumberLineAllocator`).
+  - [Animal Crossing: New Horizons](../../dm/games/01006F8002326000/README.md) (SDK 20.5.4): the clock, region and album calls under their own entries below were found in `main`'s imports before the first boot.
+  - [The Great Ace Attorney Chronicles](../../dm/games/010036E00FB20000/README.md) (SDK 10.4.1): listed after its first boot crashed in `oe::DisableAlbumImageTakenNotification`; the calls found are in the entries under `## Display, window and operation mode`, `## File system and save data` and `## Accounts, network, shop and parental controls`.
+  - [Big Brain Academy: Brain vs. Brain](../../dm/games/0100620012D6E000/README.md) (SDK 11.4.3).
+
+### A game update imports SDK calls the base game did not
+
+- **Symptom:** a game update aborts at boot where the base game ran.
+- **Cause:** the update imports SDK functions the base game didn't. Mario 1.2.1 added 28, among them `nn::nfp` (amiibo, initialized at boot), `pl::IsRunningOnOunce` (is this a Switch 2), `ec::ShowShopApplicationInformationForUpgrade`, `account::PopOpenUsers` and `aoc::CountAddOnContent`.
+- **Fix:** diff the undefined dynamic symbols of the two `main` modules before the first run, and read the call sites before choosing answers: Mario only reads the count from `PopOpenUsers` and falls back to `TryOpenPreselectedUser` on 0, and only counts add-on content when running on a Switch 2. The host answers as a Switch 1 with no amiibo and no add-on content (`hle_sdk16.c`, `hle_account.c`, `hle_misc.c`).
+- **Found on:** [Super Mario Bros. Wonder](../../dm/games/010015100B514000/README.md) (update 1.2.1).
+
+### Game-linked HIPC clients of system services
+
+- **Symptom:** abort in `sm::Initialize` under `sf::hipc::InitializeHipcServiceResolution`, right after the first frames (from `main+0x5b8198`); since that fix, `[RED] sf: the game connects to service <name> itself, which the host does not serve`, then an abort with `Result=0xe15` (`caps:su` in Super Smash Bros. Ultimate's single-player mode, `notif:a` when Fit Boxing 3 opens its Settings).
+- **Cause:** `main` links its own clients of `olsc:u` (cloud save data backup settings) and `npns` (push notifications), which talk HIPC through `sm` and `svcSendSyncRequest`. For each user the game asks whether backup is on and switches it off where it is (`main+0x2809c8c`); a failed connect aborts as well.
+- **Fix:** `hle_sf.c` takes over the SDK's HIPC client entry points (`InitializeHipcServiceResolution`, `ConnectToHipcService`, `SendSyncRequest`, `CloseClientSessionHandle` and the control requests `QueryPointerBufferSize`, `ConvertSessionToDomain`, `DuplicateSession(Ex)`): a service it serves (`olsc:u`, the capture services below) gets a host session, and every request on it succeeds with zeroed output, which reads as backup off for every user. Any other name is refused with a red line, as `sm` refuses a service nobody registered (`npns` was never connected). `DM_SF_TRACE=1` dumps each request. A service with real answers registers itself with `SF_SERVICE` (`hle.h`) from its own file: it gets the request's raw input, the client's handles and its A / B / X buffers, writes raw output, and may copy an event's read end or move a new session (an object outside a domain) back; a session keeps its own state and ends it on close (`audren:u`: [SDK 1: the renderer is the `audren:u` service](audio.md#sdk-1-the-renderer-is-the-audrenu-service)).
+- **Capture services:** Super Smash Bros. Ultimate links clients of `caps:su` and `caps:u` (screenshots and clips) and aborts with `Result=0xe15` when `sm` refuses them at the start of its single-player mode. The host has no album, so `caps:su`, `caps:u`, `caps:a`, `caps:c`, `caps:sc` and `caps:ss` get host sessions too: a list reads as empty and a save does nothing.
+- **Reminder alarms:** `nn::notification`'s alarm calls (`ListAlarmSettings`, `RegisterAlarmSetting`, `UpdateAlarmSetting`, `DeleteAlarmSetting`, `LoadApplicationParameter`), which most SDKs link, connect to `notif:a` on their first call and abort on any failure: Fit Boxing 3 2.0.0 (SDK 21.4.4) lists them when its Settings open. The client sends only commands 1000 (Initialize, with the process ID), 500, 510, 520, 530 and 540 (SDK 21: `sdk+0x58ac80`..`0x58ad10` in Splatoon Raiders 1.1's `sdk`); the notification event comes from `am`, not from this service. The host schedules no alarms, so `notif:a` is served: a list is empty and leaves the caller's array as it is, a registration succeeds with ID 0 and never fires, a parameter loads 0 bytes.
+- **Found on:** [Animal Crossing: New Horizons](../../dm/games/01006F8002326000/README.md) (SDK 20.5.4); [Super Smash Bros. Ultimate](../../dm/games/01006A800016E000/README.md) (capture services); Fit Boxing 3 2.0.0 (`0100AC101BFA2000`, a provisional profile, no note: `notif:a`, PR #1974).
+
+### Mission events through `mnpp:app`
+
+- **Symptom:** `[RED] sf: the game connects to service mnpp:app itself, which the host does not serve`, then an `nn::diag` abort with Result 0xe15 (`main+0x37080`) when a game is started.
+- **Cause:** `main` links its own `mnpp:app` client, outside the host's BCAT SDK hooks, and aborts when `sm` refuses the connection ([Game-linked HIPC clients of system services](#game-linked-hipc-clients-of-system-services)). The service (13.0.0+) initializes with a PID descriptor and reserved input `u64` (command 0), then accepts a `Uid` and type-0x5 input buffer (command 1). Neither command has output; the real service creates events for later upload to Nintendo ([service contract](https://switchbrew.org/wiki/BCAT_services#mnpp:app)).
+- **Fix:** `hle_sf.c` serves `mnpp:app` through the existing success/zeroed-output response path. It is a deliberate offline stand-in: events are acknowledged and discarded, with no retained queue or upload. This fixes the missing host service at the protocol level (`host` binding), without a game or build gate. It does not implement Nintendo's event backend.
+- **Found on:** Super Nintendo Entertainment System – Nintendo Classics (`01008D300C50C000`, 6.0.0, SDK 20.5.21: starting a game from the list; its profile is not in this repository). [PR #2164](https://github.com/seedds/nxnative/pull/2164) reports the original abort and Mac gameplay with the separate friends hook and game profile; this refresh's checks use synthetic requests.
+- **Check:** `make -C dm/host/tests GAME=<id> build/t_sf`, then `dm/host/tests/build/t_sf`, checks initialization with the PID/reserved input, event submission with the `Uid` and input buffer, success without output data or handles, untouched input, and refusal of the other `mnpp` service names. The same test retains its existing offline-service, alarm and camera checks.
+
+## SDK names that changed between versions
+
+### Older SDK names for the same calls
+
+- **Symptom:** a call the host hooks under its newer name reaches the SDK's own code on an older game.
+- **Cause:** older SDKs export other names for the same calls.
+- **Fix:** hook both generations. SDK 12 names: `ListAudioDeviceName` / `GetActiveAudioDeviceName`, singular `hid::GetNpadState`, `OpenDefaultAudioOut`. SDK 11 names: `fs::CommitSaveData(mount)` is the older name of `fs::Commit`.
+- **Found on:** [Metroid Dread](../../dm/games/010093801237C000/README.md) (SDK 11 names).
+- **Note:** overloads change too: SDK 18's `nn::hid::InitializeTouchScreen(int, int)` ([Super Mario Galaxy](../../dm/games/010099C022B96000/README.md), [SDK calls that open a system service through sm](#sdk-calls-that-open-a-system-service-through-sm)), SDK 19's `LoadModule` with one more bool ([`nn::ro` and its port](#nnro-and-its-port)), and `vi::CreateLayer` with the layer's size ([Absolum](../../dm/games/0100A83022A22000/README.md), [`vi::CreateLayer` with the layer's size](#vicreatelayer-with-the-layers-size)). SDK 19's `fs::MountRom(const char *, int, void *, size_t)` and `fs::QueryMountRomCacheSize(size_t *, int)` take a program index (Metroid Prime 4: Beyond's boot, `[RED] sf: the game connects to service fsp-srv itself`): an index mounts that program's RomFS image, `romfs_programs/<index>.img` beside the RomFS, which the NUP packer writes for the other programs of an application, and an index with none mounts this program's own (a red line for an index other than 0) ([A Switch 2 Edition NUP](packing.md#a-switch-2-edition-nup-stops-at-2-base-packages-or-is-not-a-base-title-id)).
+
+### SDK 4's `friends::GetBlockedUserList` without a `Uid`
+
+- **Symptom:** abort in `sm::Initialize` under `nn::friends::GetBlockedUserList` after the opening movie.
+- **Cause:** SDK 4's overload without the `Uid` (`EPiPNS_7account23NetworkServiceAccountIdEii`), which the host did not take over.
+- **Fix:** it reports an empty list with success: the game turns a failure into an error for the error applet (`main+0x8ff9f0`). The overload with a `Uid` keeps reporting a failure, which [DRAGON QUEST XI S](../../dm/games/01006C300E9F0000/README.md) takes as it is ([Account, friends, play data and Nintendo Account calls](#account-friends-play-data-and-nintendo-account-calls)).
+- **Found on:** [DARK SOULS: REMASTERED](../../dm/games/01004AB00A260000/README.md) (SDK 4.5.0, the oldest so far; one boot).
+
+### SDK 20's `friends::GetPlayHistoryRegistrationKey` without a `Uid`
+
+- **Symptom:** an `nn::diag` abort with Result 0xe15 in `friends::detail::ipc::Initialize` (`sdk+0x3b45a8`), first thing in `nnMain`, after `[RED] sf: the game connects to service friend:u itself`.
+- **Cause:** the form without a `Uid` (`GetPlayHistoryRegistrationKey(PlayHistoryRegistrationKey*, bool)`) was not taken over, and the SDK's own opens the friends service.
+- **Fix:** taken over as the `Uid` form is: the key is unavailable (`hle_friends.c`). Both return `RES_FAIL` without writing the output; the host has no friends service. The hook is bound to the SDK symbol, with no title or build gate.
+- **Check:** `make -C dm/host/tests GAME=<id> build/t_friends`, then `dm/host/tests/build/t_friends`, resolves both overloads by their actual registered names and checks the failure result and untouched output for both local flags, including guest bool arguments with upper bits set.
+- **Found on:** Super Nintendo Entertainment System – Nintendo Classics (`01008D300C50C000`, 6.0.0, SDK 20.5.21, `main+0xc608`; its profile is not in this repository). [PR #2162](https://github.com/seedds/nxnative/pull/2162) reports the original abort and a Mac boot past it with separate mission-events and profile changes; this hook does not add those changes.
+
+### SDK 6's `account::OpenPreselectedUser` returns a Result
+
+- **Symptom:** the first loading screen never ends; `DM_FS=1` shows `save:/system` "not found" and no `fs mount save`.
+- **Cause:** in SDK 6 `account::OpenPreselectedUser` returns a Result, and the game skips its save mount when it is not 0 (`main+0x53b7e0`). The host's hook returned void and left the handle pointer in w0.
+- **Fix:** the hook returns 0 now; SDK 7 and later callers ignore it.
+- **Found on:** [Fire Emblem: Three Houses](../../dm/games/010055D009F78000/README.md) (SDK 6.5.0).
+
+## File system and save data
+
+### Directory handle allocation failure
+
+- **Symptom:** the host crashes in `h_fs_OpenDirectory` when its folder-backed directory handle allocation fails.
+- **Cause:** `opendir` succeeded, but the following `calloc` of `dirh_t` was dereferenced without a null check. Returning early without cleanup would also leak the already-open `DIR`.
+- **Fix:** close the directory on allocation failure, preserve `errno` across cleanup, and return the existing filesystem failure through `err_res` before publishing a handle. The caller's output stays untouched, as on the other failed-open paths. `t_fs_directory.c` injects allocation failure in the real handler, checks the descriptor is closed and the output unchanged, then exercises successful open, count, read and close.
+- **Found on:** the [GCC audit in issue #313](https://github.com/seedds/nxnative/issues/313#issuecomment-6523656102), reproduced with a synthetic directory and allocation fault injection; no game-specific failure was reported.
+
+### The SDK's own `fs::IsMounted` reads an empty mount table
+
+- **Symptom:** IL2CPP "Could not open …/global-metadata.dat" (then a crash in its logger).
+- **Cause:** IL2CPP checks `nn::fs::IsMounted("rom")` before opening; the SDK's own `IsMounted` reads a mount table the host never fills.
+- **Fix:** answer from the host's mount table (`hle_fs.c`).
+- **Note:** the C library's path checks read the same empty table through `IsPathMounted` ([C library path checks ask the file system's limits](#c-library-path-checks-ask-the-file-systems-limits)).
+
+### A mount in the SDK's own table stays until the SDK releases it
+
+- **Symptom:** `game called nn::diag abort: Result 0x7802` (`MountNameAlreadyExists`) when a game mounts a RAM disk under a name it has just unmounted.
+- **Cause:** the host's mount table is permanent and `nn::fs::Unmount` did nothing. `nn::fs::MountRamDisk` has no host version, so it runs in `sdk` and registers in the SDK's own table, and the no-op left that entry there while the game freed the disk's buffer.
+- **Fix:** `h_fs_Unmount` leaves a host mount alone and sends any other name to the SDK: its `IsMounted`, then its `Unmount` if it holds the name (the SDK's `Unmount` aborts on a name it does not hold). `guest_sym` gives the module's own definitions, not the host's take-overs. A name in neither table stays a no-op. `t_fs.c` covers each case.
+- **Note:** the file calls on such a mount now reach the SDK too: [File calls on a mount in the SDK's own table](#file-calls-on-a-mount-in-the-sdks-own-table).
+- **Found on:** [Paper Mario: The Thousand-Year Door](../../dm/games/0100ECD018EBE000/README.md) ([GitHub issue #1012](https://github.com/seedds/nxnative/issues/1012)).
+
+### File calls on a mount in the SDK's own table
+
+- **Symptom:** `game called nn::diag abort: Result 0x202` (path not found) right after `fs path not found: <name>:/...` lines (`DM_FS=1`) for a mount name no `fs mount` line ever named.
+- **Cause:** `nn::fs::MountRamDisk` runs in `sdk` and registers in the SDK's own mount table ([A mount in the SDK's own table stays until the SDK releases it](#a-mount-in-the-sdks-own-table-stays-until-the-sdk-releases-it)), while every file call the host takes over looked in the host's table only. Nintendo Switch Sports Resort mounts its VoiceText speech data as the RAM disk `vtapi`, copies `VoiceText/` from `content:` into it at boot and aborted on its first `GetEntryType` (`main+0x414dbc`).
+- **Fix:** a path whose mount name the host does not hold but the SDK's `IsMounted` does goes to the SDK's own call (`sdk_path_call` in `hle_fs.c`; `guest_sym` gives the module's definition, not the host's take-over), with no host lock held. The file and directory handles that call opens are listed (`sdk_handle_add`, up to 256 open at once; past that the open is closed in the SDK and fails), and the calls that take a handle go to the SDK when it is listed; a close forgets the handle before the SDK frees it. `IsMounted` and `IsPathMounted` answer from both tables. A rename between the SDK's mount and another fails without reaching either. A path on a host mount never asks the SDK. `t_fs.c` (⑩) covers routing, handle tracking and isolation.
+- **Note:** Paper Mario: The Thousand-Year Door's file cache (the RAM disk `cache`) takes this path as well: its `CreateFile` on `cache:/<hash>` was "path not found" and the game skipped the cache ([its note](../../dm/games/0100ECD018EBE000/README.md)); not checked on that game.
+- **Found on:** [Nintendo Switch Sports Resort](../../dm/games/04008E001F098000/README.md) (`04008E001F098000`).
+
+### File handles with low bits set, and backslash paths
+
+- **Symptom:** a 10-byte read of the wrong RomFS file, then a null pointer at boot; files named `rom:\StartupUI.pak` are not found.
+- **Cause:** on Horizon a `FileHandle` is a pointer to the SDK's `FileAccessor`, a heap object, so its low bits are clear, and a game's file device may mask them off (`handle & ~7`) before `ReadFile`. The host's RomFS-image handle kept the entry index in its low bits, so entry 7 was read as entry 0. Separately, Horizon's file system accessor turns every backslash in a path into a slash before it normalizes the path (`FileSystemAccessor::SetUpPath`, on every mount except the PC host's); the host treated a backslash as part of a name.
+- **Fix:** the guest gets the host's handle shifted left by 4, 16-byte aligned like an AArch64 heap block (`FH_OUT` / `FH_IN`). Each guest path resolver (`img_path`, `host_path_ex`, `set_guest_name`, `dir_snapshot`) turns backslashes into slashes first, so overlays, `..` and guest names see the same path (`hle_fs.c`; `dm/host/tests/t_fs.c`).
+- **Found on:** Metroid Prime 4: Beyond (SDK 19; bring-up in [issue #229](https://github.com/seedds/nxnative/issues/229), from [PR #513](https://github.com/seedds/nxnative/pull/513)); not in the games table yet.
+
+### Saves named before backslash paths
+
+- **Symptom:** after a rebuild, a game shows every file of its file select as new, while its save folder holds names that start with a backslash (`\savedata0.bin`, `\screenshot0.bin`).
+- **Cause:** a host from before [backslashes separated paths](#file-handles-with-low-bits-set-and-backslash-paths) took the backslash in `save:\savedata0.bin` as part of the file's name. Today's host looks for `savedata0.bin`, and the old file cannot be reached by any path a game can ask for.
+- **Fix:** each mount of a committed save, before its work directory is cloned, renames every entry whose name has a backslash to the name the game asks for today (`save_old_names`, `hle_fs.c`): `\dir\f` becomes `dir/f`, the folder made on the way, and `\dir` and `dir` merge. It goes by guest names, so `\Foo` beside `foo` stays a file of its own. Each rename is atomic and keeps the contents, and a kill partway is finished by the next mount, so it needs no transaction and no marker, and a file the game erases later stays erased. It holds the locks the cloud sender's clone takes, and comes after the cloud's save came in, so that one is renamed too.
+- **Limits:** an entry whose new name is taken stays under its old name, with a log line at each mount (`save: … stays as it is`): the game wrote a new save since, and that is the one it reads. To go back to the old save, delete the new file and start again. A build from before the change can no longer read a renamed save. With cloud saves, a rename counts as a change to this device's save and goes out at the next mount or Commit; a device still on such a build then sees an empty save.
+- **Check:** `dm/host/tests/t_fs.c` section 9. On Link's Awakening on the Mac, one save renamed to the old form: every file shows New Game without the fix, and File 1 with it, the file unchanged. Through a cloud folder, old names that come in are renamed, the renamed save goes out, and the next mount is in step.
+- **Found on:** [The Legend of Zelda: Link's Awakening](../../dm/games/01006BB00C6F0000/README.md) ([GitHub issue #905](https://github.com/seedds/nxnative/issues/905), diagnosed in [LNPANDY/NX2Apple#461](https://github.com/LNPANDY/NX2Apple/pull/461)).
+
+### Both saves changed is the player's choice, not the newest file's
+
+- **Symptom:** after playing on two devices, a save started fresh on one of them a minute after a real save on the other replaces the real one in the cloud; it survives only in that device's `save-backups/<date> cloud`.
+- **Cause:** when both saves changed since the last sync (or a device that has never synced has a save of its own), the host keeps the newer one, and "newer" is the newest file date in the save. A new game's first save written 45 s after a 54-minute save is the newer one. The host could ask instead (`ss_decide` returns `SS_ASK` when a front end sets `DM_SAVE_SYNC_CHOICE`, documented in [The cloud saves folder](../frontends.md#the-cloud-saves-folder)), but neither launcher set it, so the player was never asked.
+- **Fix:** `save_sync_report()` (`dm/host/save_sync.c`) answers, before the start, one line about the two saves as the game's first mount will see them: their hashes, dates, file counts and sizes, the cloud's device, and whether both changed. It finds the cloud's file with the mount's own rules (`cloud_path`: `DM_SAVE_SYNC`, `icloud`, a folder named after the game) and fetches it through the same front-end call. Both launchers call it when Play is pressed (`SaveSyncReport.swift`, shared): on a conflict they show both saves with their dates and sizes (the size tells a new game from one with progress; no button is the default on the Mac) and pass `local:<hash>` or `cloud:<hash>`, both with the hash of the cloud's save the player chose against (`local:none` when the cloud has none); with nothing to choose they pass `ask`. The Mac launcher sets `DM_SAVE` before its window so the report can read the save, and checks with the window off too.
+- **Limits:** with no launcher (`DM_LAUNCHER=0`, or an iPhone app without `launcher.json`) the newer save still wins. A front end that sets `ask` without comparing keeps a save that changed on both sides on neither side's until a start that asks.
+- **Check:** `dm/host/tests/t_save_sync.c` section 15: nothing anywhere, a save only here, in step, the game's own folder chosen, only in the cloud, both changed with the new save the newer and the smaller, the cloud unreadable, sync off, `DM_SAVE_SYNC` read when no folder is given, nothing left behind. The Mac launcher and the iPhone sources build; neither dialog has been run here.
+- **Found on:** Cadence of Hyrule (`01000B900D8B0000`, 1.5.0) on an iPhone (iPhone19,4) and a Mac mini M2 Pro ([PR #2180](https://github.com/seedds/nxnative/pull/2180)).
+
+### The cloud's save is listed but not downloaded
+
+- **Symptom:** the first start after the other device saved plays this device's save; start it, quit, start again, and the cloud's save comes in. With no connection, the game's first save mount waits out its whole time limit.
+- **Cause:** iCloud lists a file it has not downloaded as `.save.dmsync.icloud`, and the mount gives the download `FETCH_MS` (10 s) before it plays on with this device's save; a later start finds the file there. The report says the coordinated read could also come back without the file before the download landed; that is not checked here.
+- **Fix:** the download happens in the launcher, before the game: its report waits up to a minute (`SaveSyncReport.waitMs`) with "Checking the cloud save…" and **Play Without It** on screen. `front_save_sync_fetch` (`dm/ios/Sources/SaveSync.m`) asks for the download and waits for the file until it is there or iCloud reports a download error (`NSURLUbiquitousItemDownloadingErrorKey`), so with no connection it ends at once. A cloud save that did not come is said so, with this device's save, another try or Cancel; playing this device's passes `offline`, so the mount does not wait again, and that save is not sent until it has been compared with the cloud's.
+- **Limits:** with no launcher the mount's 10 s apply as before. Whether iCloud sets the download error at once when offline is from Apple's documentation, not a run.
+- **Check:** not run on an iPhone here: neither a listed file nor an offline start. The PR author's runs (its own 25 s wait): a planted `.save.dmsync.icloud` in a scratch folder timed out with nothing written, and a real iCloud fetch logged `downloaded in N s`.
+- **Found on:** Cadence of Hyrule on an iPhone (iPhone19,4), after a save from a Mac mini M2 Pro, macOS 26.6 ([PR #2180](https://github.com/seedds/nxnative/pull/2180)).
+
+### A failed pull deleted this device's save
+
+- **Symptom:** `[RED] save sync: bringing the cloud's save in failed (errno=…): playing on with this device's`, and the game starts with the cloud's save; this device's is in neither its save folder nor `<save>-backups`.
+- **Cause:** `pull` (`dm/host/save_sync.c`) unpacked the cloud's save into `<save>.sync-tmp/in`, swapped each folder with the live one, and then moved the swapped-out folder, this device's, from the staging folder into the backups. Between the swap and the move the staging folder held the only copy of this device's save. When the move failed, `mount_once` logged the line above and cleared `<save>.sync-tmp`, deleting it; an app killed between the two steps lost it the same way, since every start clears that folder. A failure at the second folder (`save_device`) after the first had swapped left a save that was half the cloud's.
+- **Fix:** each of the cloud's folders is moved into this device's backup folder first, and swapped from there with the live one, so one atomic step puts the cloud's folder in place and leaves this device's in the backups; a folder the cloud's save does not have is moved to the backups. The staging folder never holds this device's save. `pull` records what it did to each folder, and on a failure puts them back in reverse order: the save is this device's again, whole, and the empty backup folder goes. If a step of putting back fails, nothing is deleted and the red line names the backup folder that holds what is not in place; since [A pull cut short left a half-replaced save](#a-pull-cut-short-left-a-half-replaced-save) that save is not mounted, and the putting back is done by the journal's recovery.
+- **Check:** `dm/host/tests/t_save_sync_metadata.c`, the pull cases: nothing fails, with and without the cloud's device save; the move into the backups fails; the device save's swap fails and the save is swapped back; the device save's move to the backups fails; the swap back fails too. After every rename and swap the staging folder is checked for this device's save. On the old `pull`, 22 of these checks fail, among them the reported loss. `t_save_sync` 101 checks.
+- **Found on:** no game; a report with an injected EIO on the move into the backups (a pull of the cloud's save chosen by the player).
+
+### A pull cut short left a half-replaced save
+
+- **Symptom:** after the app was killed while it brought the cloud's save in, or after `[RED] save sync: bringing the cloud's save in failed (errno=…), and this device's could not all be put back`, the game started on a save that was half the cloud's (its `save` folder the cloud's, `save_device` this device's, or the other way round), and the sender could send it over the cloud's. This device's folders were in `<save>-backups`, but nothing put them back.
+- **Cause:** `pull` (`dm/host/save_sync.c`) undid a failed step only within the same call, from a list of what it had done; an app killed between the two folders' swaps left no record, and the next start, finding no pull in progress, compared and played on what was in place. A step of putting back that failed was logged, and the mount went on: `save_sync_mount` returned nothing its callers could refuse on. The backups of this device's older saves were pruned when the new backup folder was made, before the pull was known to succeed. Reproduced against the old `pull`: killed after its first folder's swap, the next start (sync off) played on the cloud's `save` with this device's `save_device`.
+- **Fix:** a journal, `<save>.pull`, is written and synced before the first of this device's folders moves, and removed once the cloud's save is wholly in place (the commit point). It names the backup folder and, for each folder, what this device had there (nothing, an empty folder or a save, with the folder's device and inode: a rename or a swap keeps a folder's inode, so it is found wherever the pull stopped). One recovery, `recover`, puts this device's save back from any point, removing only the cloud's folders: a failed step calls it, and so does the first save mount of every start, before the staging folder is cleared and before a sender can run, whatever `DM_SAVE_SYNC` says. A journal that cannot be read, or a recovery that fails, leaves everything where it is and refuses the save mount: `save_sync_mount` returns -1 (a second mount gets the first's answer) and `hle_fs.c` stops the game with the reason (`die`), and the next start tries again. The Mac launcher's send after the host (`save_sync_after_exit`) sends nothing while a journal is pending, and the launchers' report (`save_sync_report`) says `pull=1` and offers no conflict, as what is in place may be half the cloud's. The older local backups are pruned only after a pull completes. An app kill is covered; a power loss is not claimed (the folder renames are not synced to the disk).
+- **Check:** `dm/host/tests/t_save_sync_metadata.c` (204 checks): a kill after each rename and swap of a pull, with this device's device folder holding a save, empty or absent and the cloud's save with and without one, then the next start's mount: this device's save is back as it was, nothing of the cloud's left, the journal gone; a kill in the middle of that recovery, finished by the next start; a pull whose putting back fails keeps its journal and the next start recovers it; a recovery that fails refuses the mount, keeps the journal, and the start after it recovers; an unreadable journal refuses the mount; a second mount gets the first's answer; a pull that fails prunes nothing and one that completes leaves the newest 10; nothing is sent after the host while a journal is pending. `t_save_sync` (104): a journal the start cannot put back stops the game's own `MountSaveData`, leaving the save and the journal as they were, and with the journal gone the mount goes on. Four deliberate breaks (no journal, a mount that ignores it, pruning at the backup's creation, a recovery that leaves the cloud's folder in the backups) each fail the test.
+- **Found on:** no game; the audit of the review's save fix (step 15), with the reproduction above.
+
+### Automated runs synced their scratch save with the player's cloud save
+
+- **Symptom:** none in a log. With `DM_SAVE_SYNC` exported in the shell, a `scripts/regress.py` route with `save=new` starts with the player's cloud save for that game, and the run's own progress then replaces it in the cloud.
+- **Cause:** `regress.py` (`run_env`), `bench.py` (`run_one`) and the NSP script's host runs (`tool_env`: the captures, checks and the pipeline prebuild) copied the caller's environment and replaced only `DM_SAVE` with a scratch folder. The host syncs whenever `DM_SAVE_SYNC` names a folder, and names the cloud file by title ID (`dm/host/save_sync.c`, `cloud_path`), so the scratch save was synced with the player's: with no `DM_SAVE_SYNC_CHOICE` the newer save wins, a save only in the cloud is pulled in, and the first commit after it is sent over it. The cloud save it replaced was kept in `<DM_SAVE>-backups`, inside the run folder, which the next run deletes.
+- **Fix:** every automated run (`regress.py`, `bench.py`, the NSP script's tool runs and prebuild) goes through one policy, `dm/tools/run_saves.py` `cloud_off`, applied after the overrides it takes: `DM_SAVE_SYNC=0`, and the launchers' `DM_SAVE_SYNC_CHOICE` removed (the player's answer, meaningless with sync off); `regress.py --env` or a `bench.py` variant or `--env` that turns sync on is refused (`CloudSync`). `regress.py` leaves `DM_SAVE_SYNC*` out of a baseline's context, since no run reads them. The command the NSP script prints for the player's own run is unchanged. Cloud saves are checked by `dm/host/tests/t_save_sync.c` and `t_save_sync_metadata.c` on temporary folders.
+- **Found on:** no game; a report with a temporary cloud folder holding a synthetic save.
+
+### An unhooked `fs::ReadFile` overload falls through to the SDK's own
+
+- **Symptom:** a fault inside `sdk` at boot, right after the save data is mounted: `ldr w8, [x0, #0x24]` with `x0` holding a host file handle (SD GUNDAM G GENERATION CROSS RAYS: `sdk+0x62a78`, `x0 = 0x4000000000000390`), while `GetFileSize` on the same handle answered just before.
+- **Cause:** the game imports `nn::fs::ReadFile(FileHandle, s64, void*, size_t, const ReadOption&)`, the overload with read options and no bytes-read output, which the host did not hook, so the call bound to the SDK's own. That one reads the handle as a `FileAccessor*`, and the host's handle is an image-entry id ([as above](#file-handles-with-low-bits-set-and-backslash-paths)). The general case: [SDK calls that open a system service through sm](#sdk-calls-that-open-a-system-service-through-sm); the import list finds such overloads beforehand ([Listing a game's unhooked imports before they are met](#listing-a-games-unhooked-imports-before-they-are-met)).
+- **Fix:** a hook with the contract of the overload without read options (`h_fs_ReadFileExactOpt` calls `h_fs_ReadFileExact`): the options are ignored and a short read is a failure, as on Horizon, since the caller has no byte count to see it by (`hle_fs.c`; `dm/host/tests/t_fs.c`).
+- **Found on:** SD GUNDAM G GENERATION CROSS RAYS (`010055700CEA8000`, SDK 9.4.1, from [PR #947](https://github.com/seedds/nxnative/pull/947)): the fault was all that stood between the boot and its frame loop; not in the games table yet.
+
+### C library path checks ask the file system's limits
+
+- **Symptom:** Addressables never start (no `StreamingAssets/aa/settings.json` in the `DM_FS=1` output at all), then a null asset crash (`main+0x31100ec`).
+- **Cause:** Unity 2022's C# file API reaches the SDK's C library, not `nn::fs`: `File.Exists` → `SystemNative_Stat` → `stat` = `open` + `fstat` + `close` (`sdk+0x948b0`). nnmusl's path check (`sdk+0x904e0`, then the converter `sdk+0x8ef50`) asks `nn::fs::IsPathMounted`, `GetPathLengthMax`, `GetUtf16PathLengthMax`, `GetEntryNameLengthMax` and `GetUtf16EntryNameLengthMax` before it calls `nn::fs::GetEntryType` / `OpenFile`, and the SDK's own versions read the empty mount table (`IsPathMounted` false; the next one aborted with Result 0x35f202).
+- **Fix:** the host answers them from its mount table, with Horizon's 0x300-byte limits and no UTF-16 limit (`hle_fs.c`). The path .NET builds is `/rom:/…`; nnmusl's converter turns it back into `rom:/…`.
+- **Found on:** [Ace Attorney Investigations Collection](../../dm/games/010005501E68C000/README.md) (1.0.0.2, SDK 17.5.3, Unity 2022.3 IL2CPP).
+- **Note:** armyja's fork (GitHub issue #18) patched the managed stat wrappers by address instead; these hooks need no per-build table.
+
+### Paths with `.` and `..` components
+
+- **Symptom:** an Atk task aborts in a `switch` default (`UnexpectedDefaultImpl`, then `AbortImpl`) while a stream sound loads.
+- **Cause:** the stream path was `rom:/system/snd/../../sounds/streams/…`, and the host answered "not found" for any `..` component. Horizon's path normalizer resolves `.` and `..` lexically and fails only above the mount root.
+- **Fix:** `host_path_ex` now does the same (`norm_rest` in `hle_fs.c`).
+- **Found on:** [Metroid Dread](../../dm/games/010093801237C000/README.md) (`main+0x1225a8c`).
+
+### RomFS lookups on large directories
+
+- **Symptom:** a long black screen at boot with nothing in the log (Engage: about 17 s on the Mac, 32 s on an iPhone); sampling the process (`sample <pid> 3`) shows `getxattr` under `path_component` at the top of stack.
+- **Cause:** the game is walking RomFS, and every path component scanned its whole parent directory. That scan maps the game's case-sensitive names onto APFS, so it calls `getxattr` on each entry, and Engage has directories of 38,000 files. The rest of the time was the listing itself: `ReadDirectory` called `stat`, `getxattr` and a failing `fopen("<file>.holes")` for every entry.
+- **Fix:** read-only mounts now use a per-directory name index built at the first lookup (`dir_index_get` in `hle_fs.c`), which also speeds up every file Wwise opens during play. In listings, now only `.dm-case-*` names (the only ones that carry the xattr) read it, directories take their type from `d_type`, and files use `fstatat` / `openat` relative to the directory.
+- **Found on:** [Fire Emblem Engage](../../dm/games/0100A6301214E000/README.md): with the name index the first frame came at 5.7 s on the Mac; with the listing fix too it comes at 1.8 s on the Mac (9.9 s → expected about 3 s on an iPhone), and the opening movie starts at 7 s instead of 49 s.
+
+### ReadFile into write-protected texture pool pages
+
+- **Symptom:** `[RED] fs ReadFile into … failed at +0 (EFAULT): VM region … protection 1`, now and then.
+- **Cause:** the destination was texture pool memory, which the dirty-page tracker write-protects after each upload (`nvn_dirty.c`). `ReadFile` lifts the protection first (`dm_guest_write_prepare`), but the graphics layer can protect the pages again before `pread` writes them, and a system call gets EFAULT where a plain write would fault into the tracker's handler; at 60 fps uploads come twice as often.
+- **Fix:** on EFAULT the host now prepares the rest of the buffer again (unprotect, mark dirty, so the texture is uploaded again) and reads on, up to eight times (`h_fs_ReadFile`; the first case is logged, `[fs] ReadFile … prepared again`).
+- **Found on:** [Monster Train 2](../../dm/games/010051701FB46000/README.md) at 60 fps: two runs in three as the route map loads, never at 30 fps.
+- **Check:** four runs at 60 fps: one retry, no failed read.
+
+### File-not-found noise from optional paths
+
+- **Symptom:** `fs … not found` lines with `DM_FS=1`.
+- **Cause:** games probe many optional paths (debug parameters, other-language voice files).
+- **Note:** only a missing file followed by a failure matters.
+- **Found on:** every game (general).
+
+### Directory, file data cache and save-thumbnail calls
+
+- **Symptom:** a missing `fs::DeleteDirectory`; undefined `nn::fs::` / `nn::account::` imports with no hook, found from the import list ([Listing a game's unhooked imports before they are met](#listing-a-games-unhooked-imports-before-they-are-met)) before they were met.
+- **Fix:** hooked: `fs::DeleteDirectory` (an empty directory), `fs::CleanDirectoryRecursively`, `fs::RenameDirectory` (done as `RenameFile`); `fs::EnableGlobalFileDataCache`, `fs::DisableGlobalFileDataCache`, `fs::EnableIndividualFileDataCache` / `DisableIndividualFileDataCache`; `account::StoreSaveDataThumbnailImage` / `DeleteSaveDataThumbnailImage` (nothing kept) (`hle_fs.c`, `hle_account.c`).
+- **Found on:** [Metroid Dread](../../dm/games/010093801237C000/README.md) (SDK 11: `fs::DeleteDirectory` was missing); [13 Sentinels: Aegis Rim](../../dm/games/01003FC01670C000/README.md) (SDK 11: `CleanDirectoryRecursively`, `EnableGlobalFileDataCache`); [DRAGON QUEST XI S](../../dm/games/01006C300E9F0000/README.md) (SDK 7.3.2: `DisableGlobalFileDataCache`); [The Great Ace Attorney Chronicles](../../dm/games/010036E00FB20000/README.md) (SDK 10.4.1: `RenameDirectory`, the thumbnail calls), [Big Brain Academy: Brain vs. Brain](../../dm/games/0100620012D6E000/README.md) (SDK 11.4.3: the individual file data cache).
+
+### `fs::Commit` with a list of mounts
+
+- **Symptom:** the SDK's own code opened fsp-srv and aborted, leaving the save writes uncommitted.
+- **Cause:** `fs::Commit(const char* const*, int)`, the mount-list overload, had no hook.
+- **Fix:** the host commits the list as one transaction when every name is the same host save; cache and temporary storage in the list are skipped (their writes are already on disk, as for the single-name `Commit`), and a list of two separate saves is refused without committing either.
+- **Found on:** [Animal Crossing: New Horizons](../../dm/games/01006F8002326000/README.md) (PR #25).
+- **Note:** SDK 11's `fs::CommitSaveData(mount)` is the older name of `fs::Commit` ([Older SDK names for the same calls](#older-sdk-names-for-the-same-calls)); `fs::Commit` with a `CommitOption` is under [Device save data on the account save's directory](#device-save-data-on-the-account-saves-directory).
+
+### Save-write failures stay with their backing transaction
+
+- **Symptom:** a failed write in one independent save can make another save's next `Commit` fail and discard its successful changes; the damaged save may then commit its truncated working file.
+- **Cause:** the host used one process-wide failure marker, consumed by whichever save committed first.
+- **Fix:** each canonical backing save owns its failure state. Open file handles retain that transaction identity, aliases share it, and each commit waits for in-flight writes or size changes to its backing save before consuming or restoring its failure marker (`hle_fs.c`). Concurrent first writes initialize the shared failure-injection settings with `pthread_once`.
+- **Check:** `t_fs_commit_list` injects write failures after truncation in either of two independent saves, verifies that the other save commits its changes, and checks failure propagation through aliases, rename failure handling, and a failed recovery clone. It blocks writes and size changes through an alias while another save commits, then verifies that both Commit ABIs wait for the affected I/O and refuse its failure. `make -C dm/host/tests GAME=<title ID> fs-tsan` also checks concurrent first writes and failed file I/O concurrent with mounting another alias.
+- **Found on:** the [two-save host regression](../../dm/host/tests/t_fs_commit_list.c). [Mario Kart 8 Deluxe](../../dm/games/0100152000022000/README.md) uses independent device and account saves.
+
+### A failed save path operation still allowed the save to commit
+
+- **Symptom:** after an unexpected host error while creating, opening for writing, deleting or renaming a save entry, `Commit` could publish the other changes in the working directory without the failed change.
+- **Cause:** writes and size changes through file handles marked the backing transaction failed, but path lookup and most path mutations did not. A recursive delete also continued after a child failed, letting the next directory read replace its error.
+- **Fix:** unexpected mutation failures mark the transaction by its exact guest mount name, even when lookup failed before a physical path existed. Aliases share the backing transaction's marker; read-only and non-save mounts do not mark it. Ordinary missing, existing, type, not-empty and invalid-name results (what Horizon answers too) remain unmarked; a rename whose name metadata and rollback both fail marks it whatever the first error, since the entry stays at the new path. Recursive deletion and cleaning check the child result as well as the saved directory-read error before returning, restoring that error after closing the directory; deletion calls `rmdir` only after every child succeeded. This also keeps the analyzer from treating a failed child followed by a successful `rmdir` as a failure with undefined `errno`. The existing commit refusal restores the committed version and notifies the front end; the next complete save can succeed.
+- **Found on:** the [synthetic save-mutation regression](../../dm/host/tests/t_fs_mutation.c), following the filesystem lead in [issue #1692](https://github.com/seedds/nxnative/issues/1692) for [Ocarina of Time](../../dm/games/0400486024DEC000/README.md). The cause of that game's area-transition crash and subsequent black screen remains unconfirmed; this correction does not establish that connection or repair an already damaged save.
+
+### A save folder that cannot clone directories
+
+- **Symptom:** `[RED] save transaction: cloning the work directory failed (…, errno=45)` when the save folder (`DM_SAVE`) is on an external drive, then a read-only save mount and every save write failing (South Park: `profile.sys`); now the line reads `the save folder's filesystem cannot clone directories (save transactions need APFS …)`.
+- **Cause:** a save transaction clones the committed folder into its work folder with `clonefile` and publishes a commit with `renamex_np(RENAME_SWAP)`. Both need APFS: on HFS+ and exFAT disk images both fail with `ENOTSUP` (45), and neither format advertises `VOL_CAP_INT_CLONE` or `VOL_CAP_INT_RENAME_SWAP`, so copying the files instead of cloning them would still leave no atomic commit. The host already failed safely (the committed save mounted read-only, the iOS front end told); the line did not say why. The apps' own save folders are on APFS (the Mac app's `~/Library/Application Support/nxnative/<title ID>/save`, the iOS app's `Documents/save`); only a `DM_SAVE` set by hand or a development run's work folder can be elsewhere.
+- **Fix:** `hle_fs.c` names the cause when `clonefile` returns `ENOTSUP`: set `DM_SAVE` to a folder on an APFS volume (the RomFS and shader libraries may stay on the other drive). Recursive deletes (`rm_tree`, `CleanDirectoryRecursively`) now take each entry's type from `lstat`, not `d_type` (which a filesystem may report as unknown), unlink a symlink without entering it, and refuse a symlink given as the tree itself; a commit whose leftover `.new` cannot be cleared is refused with its own red line instead of failing in the clone.
+- **Check:** `dm/host/tests/t_fs.c` section 8 makes `clonefile` fail with `ENOTSUP` (read-only mount, one front-end notice, committed save unchanged, writable again once cloning works) and deletes save folders holding a symlink to a folder outside the save (the target keeps its files).
+- **Found on:** South Park: The Fractured But Whole 1.05, whose port is [PR #662](https://github.com/seedds/nxnative/pull/662) (not yet in the games table): a development Mac run with its save folder on a Journaled HFS+ external drive.
+
+### Save data size queries
+
+- **Symptom:** SIGSEGV at address 0 in `nn::fs::GetSaveDataSizeMax`, about 13 minutes into a new game.
+- **Cause:** Unreal's save system asks before its first save; the SDK's version reads the fsp-srv object the host never made.
+- **Fix:** it reports 256 MB of data and journal, as `GetSaveDataSize` does (`hle_fs.c`). `fs::GetFreeSpaceSize` gives the same answer; `fs::GetCacheStorageSize` reports the size the storage was created with ([Cache storage limits and the cache storage list](#cache-storage-limits-and-the-cache-storage-list)). Also hooked: `fs::GetSaveDataSize` / `ExtendSaveData` and the one-argument `fs::MountCacheStorage`.
+- **Found on:** [Octopath Traveler 0](../../dm/games/01005270232F2000/README.md) (`GetSaveDataSizeMax`, `GetCacheStorageSize`, `GetFreeSpaceSize`, found by the import list); [Monster Hunter Rise](../../dm/games/0100B04011742000/README.md) (SDK 11: `GetSaveDataSize`, `ExtendSaveData`, `MountCacheStorage`).
+
+### Cache storage and other titles' save data
+
+- **Symptom:** each aborted in `sm::Initialize` or read a null applet proxy, one per boot.
+- **Cause:** SDK 20 file system calls that reach a service.
+- **Fix:** cache storage: `OpenCacheStorageList` / `ReadCacheStorageList` list the cache folders that exist, none on a fresh run ([Cache storage limits and the cache storage list](#cache-storage-limits-and-the-cache-storage-list)), `CreateCacheStorage` / `MountCacheStorage` use a host folder beside the temporary storage. `fs::IsSaveDataExisting(ApplicationId, Uid)` for another title (it asks for Breath of the Wild's save: none) and `MountSaveDataReadOnly`.
+- **Found on:** [The Legend of Zelda: Tears of the Kingdom](../../dm/games/0100F2C0115B6000/README.md) (1.4.3, the same `sdk` build as Breath of the Wild 1.9.0).
+
+### A read-only mount of the game's own save data
+
+- **Symptom:** `game called nn::diag abort: Result 0, call site main+0x3917270` about 48 s into every start after the game first saved, right after `fs mount save0`; the first start, with no account save yet, went on. Earlier the log has `[RED] fs read-only mount of another program's save data SaveReadOnly: program <the game's own ID>, the host has none`.
+- **Cause:** the game mounts its own account save read-only as `SaveReadOnly` at boot (`fs::MountSaveDataReadOnly` with its own application ID; `fs::IsSaveDataExisting` had told it the save exists). The host answered every such mount TargetNotFound. Once an account save existed, the game read through that mount and aborted where it checks the mount's name against `SaveReadOnly` (main+0x349d880 → main+0x3917260).
+- **Fix:** `hle_fs.c` mounts the game's own save data read-only on its committed save directory (`DM_SAVE`, not the transaction's work directory), creating it empty on a first start, as the console creates the save data; another program's save data is still TargetNotFound, and so is the game's own when `DM_SAVE` is unset or its folder cannot be made (a red line, no exit).
+- **Check:** `make -C dm/host/tests GAME=<id> run` (`t_fs_case`): the own save mounts read-only, reads the committed files and not the uncommitted ones, refuses writes; another program's gets TargetNotFound; a first start mounts an empty save; no `DM_SAVE`, or a file in its place, gets TargetNotFound. In the game, a start with the save the abort happened on runs past it.
+- **Found on:** [Nintendo Switch Sports Resort](../../dm/games/04008E001F098000/README.md) (Switch 2, `04008E001F098000`, v65536).
+
+### Device save data on the account save's directory
+
+- **Symptom:** the island lives in device save data, which the host did not mount.
+- **Cause:** 3.0.3's `main` mounts no account save data; the island is in device save data (`MountDeviceSaveData`, `GetDeviceSaveDataSize`, `ExtendDeviceSaveData`).
+- **Fix:** the host gives device save data the account save's host directory and transaction (one player). A second mount name over a save already mounted now shares that mount's transaction (`mount_ex`); it used to clone the committed directory again, over the first name's pending writes. Also `fs::Commit` with a `CommitOption` (its one flag clears the restore flag) and `GetSaveDataRestoreFlag` (never restored from a backup).
+- **Found on:** [Animal Crossing: New Horizons](../../dm/games/01006F8002326000/README.md) (3.0.3, SDK 20.5.4).
+- **Note:** a game whose `main` imports `MountSaveData` too gets its device save as a save of its own ([Device and account save data as two saves](#device-and-account-save-data-as-two-saves)).
+
+### Device and account save data as two saves
+
+- **Symptom:** every boot asks to pick a Mii again (`DM_FS=1`: `UserSave:/userdata.dat` not found although the host save has it).
+- **Cause:** the game mounts `CommonSave` with `MountDeviceSaveData` and `UserSave` with `MountSaveData(uid)` (`main+0x8b0c84`), two saves on Horizon; the host made device save data the account save (for Animal Crossing: New Horizons, which mounts only device save data), so both names shared one directory. At boot the save manager deletes `CommonSave:/userdata.dat` (`main+0x8b1910`, through its file device's `DeleteFile`, `main+0x633afc`; a probe there with `@*x1+8` shows the name) and commits, and in the shared directory that was the account save's own file.
+- **Fix:** a game whose `main` imports `MountSaveData` too gets its device save as `<DM_SAVE>_device`, a transaction of its own (`hle_fs.c`); of the games on hand only this one imports both, so Animal Crossing's islands stay where they are.
+- **Found on:** [Mario Kart 8 Deluxe](../../dm/games/0100152000022000/README.md) (4.0.0, SDK 4.4.0; one boot).
+
+### A rename between two mounts
+
+- **Symptom:** `RenameFile("save:/a", "rom:/a")` succeeded and wrote into the read-only RomFS folder; a rename between two writable mounts, or between two names of one host save, moved the file.
+- **Cause:** `rename_entry` in `hle_fs.c` checked that the source's mount is writable and resolved the destination without looking at its mount. Horizon's `fs::RenameFile` and `fs::RenameDirectory` find each path's file system by its mount name and refuse two before touching either (Atmosphère's `fs_user_filesystem.cpp`: the two accessors must be one, else `ResultRenameToOtherFileSystem`, module 2 description 6034); only a rename between the SDK's own mount and the host's was refused, with `RES_FAIL`.
+- **Fix:** `rename_any` compares the two mount names (the part before `:`, as `FindFileSystem` takes it) and answers `0x2F2402` for two, before the SDK or the host is asked; one mount's rename goes on as before, and a read-only source is refused by its own check. Account and device saves that share a host folder and transaction are two mounts on Horizon too, so a rename between them is refused as well. `make -C dm/host/tests GAME=<id> run` (`t_fs`, ⑦b) renames into a read-only mount, into another writable one and between two names of one save, and checks both paths afterwards.
+- **Found on:** a code audit's reproduction (2026-11-13); no game has been seen to rename between mounts.
+
+### A folder that cannot be read is listed short
+
+- **Symptom:** after a read error on a folder (`EIO`, an external drive going to sleep), files of a read-only mount are not found for the rest of the run; `CleanDirectoryRecursively` succeeds with files left; a listing or count is short with no error.
+- **Cause:** `readdir` returns NULL both at a folder's end and on a failure, and only `errno` tells them apart. Every loop in `hle_fs.c` took NULL for the end: the name index of a read-only folder (`dir_index_get`) was kept for the whole run without the names after the failure, the path walk answered "not found", and the clean, the listing (`ReadDirectory`), the count and the overlay snapshot went on with what they had.
+- **Fix:** `dir_next` in `hle_fs.c` gives the next entry without `.` and `..`, and NULL with `errno` 0 at the end or set on a failure; the name index, the path walk, the recursive delete and clean, `ReadDirectory`, `GetDirectoryEntryCount` and the overlay snapshot (`OpenDirectory`) use it and fail on a failure. A name index that could not be read is not kept, so the next lookup reads the folder again. `make -C dm/host/tests GAME=<id> fs-directory` (`t_fs_directory`) fails a folder's reading at once and after one entry in each of them.
+- **Found on:** a code audit's reproduction with an injected `EIO` (2026-11-13); no game log has shown it.
+
+### Opening a large folder with an overlay set takes seconds
+
+- **Symptom:** with `DM_ROMFS_OVERLAY` set, `OpenDirectory` on a RomFS folder of tens of thousands of entries takes seconds, during which every other path call waits for the path lock.
+- **Cause:** the overlay snapshot (`dir_snapshot` in `hle_fs.c`) compared each name it gathered with every name gathered before it, the base folder's own too, to drop a name a layer repeats: n(n−1)/2 comparisons, 722 million for 38,000 names.
+- **Fix:** the snapshot gathers every layer's names, then `names_first` sorts (name, position) once and keeps each name's first, in the order gathered, so the listing is the same as before (the last overlay folder first, then the base, an image base in its table order). Measured on an M4 Pro, a read-only folder of 4-byte files under an empty overlay folder: 10,000 entries 0.19 s → 0.03 s, 38,000 entries 3.23 s → 0.20 s; what is left grows linearly (each name's layer is looked up for its type and size). `t_fs_directory` checks a name in several layers, names differing in case and the layers' order.
+- **Found on:** a code audit's synthetic measurement (2026-11-13). No game runs with an overlay by default.
+
+### System data stand-in for the MiiModel archive
+
+- **Symptom:** abort in `fs::detail::GetFileSystemProxyServiceObject` under `fs::QueryMountSystemDataCacheSize` (`main+0x8f23bc`).
+- **Cause:** the game mounts system data 0100000000000802, MiiModel, as `MiiResource:` and reads `NXTexture{Low,Mid}{Linear,SRGB}.dat` and `Shape{Mid,High}.dat` whole; the SDK's Mii rendering is linked into `main` (`main+0x8f1400`–`+0x8fc000`).
+- **Fix:** firmware content is not the host's to ship, so the host writes a stand-in of the same layout with every part blank (`dm_mii_standin` in `hle_mii.c`, mounted by `hle_fs.c` `MountSystemData`; any other system data answers "target not found"). A player's own archive takes its place ([A console's own Mii database and system data](#a-consoles-own-mii-database-and-system-data)). Heads build and draw nothing; bodies and favourite colours show.
+- **Found on:** [Mario Kart 8 Deluxe](../../dm/games/0100152000022000/README.md) (4.0.0, SDK 4.4.0; one boot).
+- **Note:** the layout as the code reads it: texture file +0xc the largest decompressed size of each of 11 part kinds, +0x38 their alignments, from +0x64 one 0x1c-byte entry per part (offset, size, stored size, 4 zlib parameters, then width, height, format, mips); shape file +0xc / +0x3c the same for 12 kinds, from +0x6c one 0x58-byte entry per part (offset, size, stored size, parameters, five vertex attribute offsets and sizes, index offset and byte size, bounding box), then hair (132 × 0x48, +0x12704) and faceline (12 × 0x24, +0x14c24) transforms. A texture of size 0 is skipped (`main+0x8faaf4`), but a game may still ask for its texture, so every texture entry points at one blank 8x4 texture ([A Mii part the stand-in leaves out](#a-mii-part-the-stand-in-leaves-out); Mario Kart 8 Deluxe has not been run with it); shapes cannot all be absent, because the faceline is drawn without a check (a null buffer read at `main+0xa690d0` under `main+0x8286c4`), so every shape entry points at one shared mesh of one vertex and the triangle (0, 0, 0), 32 bytes of zeros in a stored zlib block. The largest sizes must not be 0: they size the model's memory pool (`main+0x8f2e6c`), whose alignment is the first one. The Mii database itself: [Mii database answered by the host](#mii-database-answered-by-the-host).
+
+### The Switch 2's Mii model archive
+
+- **Symptom:** at boot `[RED] fs mount system data MiiResource: the host has no 0400000000000849`; the game goes on, and minutes later, where it first builds a Mii model, a guest crash reading address 0xc (`[dm] CRASH sig=11 addr=0xc`). Nintendo Switch Sports Resort: `pc=main+0x1cdcfb4`, `lr=main+0x1cd2e68`, 3.7 minutes into a session, in two-player Avioneta.
+- **Cause:** the Switch 2's Mii model data is system data 0400000000000849, which the game mounts as `MiiResource:`; the host served only the Switch's MiiModel, 0100000000000802. With the mount refused the game's Mii resource kept null file pointers, and the function at `main+0x1cd2e30`, which sizes a model's memory with `nn::mii::detail::CalculateSizeAndAlignment`, read the texture file's largest part size (`+0xc` of the file, `main+0x1cdcfb0`) through the null one.
+- **Fix:** the same stand-in serves both archives from one directory ([System data stand-in for the MiiModel archive](#system-data-stand-in-for-the-miimodel-archive)) and holds the Switch 2's texture sets too (`OunceTexture{Low,Mid,High}{Linear,SRGB}.dat`). The files have the Switch's layout: the game's accessors (`main+0x1cdcd80`–`+0x1cdd0c4`) read 11 texture part kinds from +0x64 in 0x1c-byte entries with the same section offsets, 12 shape kinds from +0x6c in 0x58-byte entries, the hair transforms at +0x12704 and the facelines at +0x14c24, and skip a texture entry of size 0 (`main+0x1cdead0`), which the stand-in no longer has ([A Mii part the stand-in leaves out](#a-mii-part-the-stand-in-leaves-out)). Mii heads build and draw nothing, as with the Switch's stand-in.
+- **Check:** `t_mii_sysdata` (host tests): both archives mount from the one stand-in directory, every file the game names reads in that layout, another archive is still "target not found". Switch Sports Resort on the Mac: the mount succeeds at boot, and the menus, table tennis and one-player Avioneta play as before. The crash itself needs two players, which a scripted run cannot be: a player's 11-minute session with the stand-in (2026-11-12), two-player Avioneta among it, did not crash.
+- **Found on:** [Nintendo Switch Sports Resort](../../dm/games/04008E001F098000/README.md).
+
+### A Mii part the stand-in leaves out
+
+- **Symptom:** a crash in an NVN texture call with a null texture where the game builds or draws a Mii model: `[dm] CRASH sig=11 addr=0xc pc=h_nvnTextureGetFlags+0`, `x0=0`. Nintendo Switch Sports Resort: two-player skate, on leaving the practice zone (a player's session, 2026-11-12, 193 s in), guest backtrace `main+0x49c58 main+0x3fbc9b0 main+0x3fbbb24 main+0x3fbb474`.
+- **Cause:** the [Mii model stand-in](#system-data-stand-in-for-the-miimodel-archive) left every texture part out (size 0). The Mii code skips such a part when it builds a model (`main+0x1cdead0` in `CharModel` initialization, `main+0x1cd33e0`), but its texture getter (`main+0x1cd5490`) answers for a part by the model's create flags alone: texture 1 (the part of kind 0 that `GetHairType` picks, held at the model +0x150) exists whenever the normal or the hat flag was given. The firmware's files have every part, so on a console the getter's answer always holds a texture. Switch Sports Resort sets up a renderer for its Mii models there (`main+0x3fbb420` → `main+0x3fbc8d0`), which takes textures 0 and 1 and registers each in its texture pool (`main+0x49b30`: `nvnTexturePoolRegisterTexture`, then `nvnTextureGetFlags`); texture 1's holder was the empty one (`main+0x1cdd0f0`), so both calls got a null texture and the second read its flags through it.
+- **Fix:** in `hle_mii.c`, for every game that uses the stand-in: every texture part is one shared blank texture, as the firmware's files have one for every part: 8x4 texels of format index 0 (nn::gfx `R8_Unorm`, the table at `main+0x42d2688`), one level, optimal tiling, 0x20 zero bytes stored as one zlib block (the mesh's), which the reader inflates into the texture's own storage (`main+0x1cdcbe8`, 512 bytes on NVN). Mii heads still draw nothing.
+- **Check:** `t_mii_sysdata` (host tests): every part of every texture file has a texture whose data inflates to its size, no more than its texels (one check fails without the change). The crash itself needs a second player, which a scripted run cannot be: a player's 11-minute session with the blank texture (2026-11-12), two-player skate among it (the practice zone, then leaving it), did not crash ([the game note](../../dm/games/04008E001F098000/README.md#miis)).
+- **Found on:** [Nintendo Switch Sports Resort](../../dm/games/04008E001F098000/README.md).
+
+### Save-sync metadata errors close the temporary file
+
+- **Symptom:** a failed write of `<DM_SAVE>.sync.tmp` leaks its stream, and the diagnostic can report a cleanup error instead of the write error.
+- **Cause:** `write_base` short-circuited `fprintf`, `fclose` and `rename` in one condition. A failed write skipped the close; `unlink` could then replace `errno` before logging.
+- **Fix:** always close an opened stream, preserve the first write/close/rename error, and rename the temporary file only after both writing and closing succeed. Cleanup leaves the previous sync metadata in place.
+- **Found on:** static analysis reported in [issue #313](https://github.com/seedds/nxnative/issues/313#issuecomment-6543080671); reproduced without game files.
+- **Check:** `t_save_sync_metadata` injects open, write, close and rename failures into the production writer, including simultaneous write/close failures and cleanup changing `errno`. It checks descriptor closure, preservation of existing metadata, diagnostics and a successful replacement. No cloud service or gameplay validation is claimed.
+
+## Add-on content
+
+### Add-on content from `DM_AOC_DIR`
+
+- **Symptom:** DLC the player owns is not unlocked.
+- **Fix:** the host reports add-on content from `DM_AOC_DIR`, one numbered folder per DLC index (title ID − base − 0x1000) holding that DLC's RomFS. `build_ipa_from_nsp.py` extracts the DLC NSPs in the folder with `nx_extract --aoc`, `build_ipa.sh` copies them to `<App>/aoc/`, and the app sets `DM_AOC_DIR`. `aoc::CountAddOnContent` / `ListAddOnContent` report those indices (`hle_misc.c`), and `fs::MountAddOnContent` mounts the folder read-only (`hle_fs.c`). D2R keeps its fixed `{1}` without `DM_AOC_DIR`; `DM_AOC_OWNED=0` reports none.
+- **Found on:** [Fire Emblem Engage](../../dm/games/0100A6301214E000/README.md): its two DLC packages each hold one empty marker file (`e0`, `h0`), and the content is already in update 2.0.0. `DLCManager.RefreshHasList` marks a content owned when its mount succeeded (probe `main+0x29f3c88`).
+
+### Owned add-on content lists beyond 64 entries
+
+- **Symptom:** some owned DLC is missing, and `aoc: reporting` stops at 64 add-on contents.
+- **Cause:** `hle_misc.c` stored only 64 indices and stopped reading `DM_AOC_DIR` when that array filled, before sorting. The omitted indices depended on directory order; its 256-byte log buffer also truncated longer lists.
+- **Fix:** the shared array holds 2000 indices, covering the scanner's existing accepted range (1..2000), and a static buffer holds the complete index log without increasing guest-stack use. Folder and `.img` discovery, sorting, deduplication, pagination and `DM_AOC_OWNED=0` retain their behavior. This applies to every game using the add-on content hooks.
+- **Open:** the existing 1..2000 acceptance range is retained; it is not established here as Horizon's full legal range. More than 2000 accepted folder/image entries can still fill the array before deduplication, even when their unique indices fit.
+- **Found on:** [Super Smash Bros. Ultimate](../../dm/games/01006A800016E000/README.md) (13.0.5, 99 DLC licences; PR #441).
+
+### Cache storage limits and the cache storage list
+
+- **Symptom:** SIGSEGV at address 0 in `sdk+0x34fb44` at start-up, in `nn::fs::GetCacheStorageMax(int*, long*)` (SDK 14), which reads the SDK's service object and dereferences it. Separately, cache storage the game made on an earlier run (or a player copied in) is not found: the list reported none.
+- **Cause:** no host hook for `GetCacheStorageMax`, whose answer is the application's own control data; `OpenCacheStorageList` / `ReadCacheStorageList` reported none on every run, a fresh console's answer.
+- **Fix:** `GetCacheStorageMax` reads the game's control data (`control_read` in `hle_fs.c`): `CacheStorageDataAndJournalSizeMax` (s64 at 0x3180) and `CacheStorageIndexMax` (u16 at 0x3188) of `control.nacp`, from `DM_CONTROL_NACP`, else beside the RomFS: the NSP script's extraction folder (`<version>/control.nacp`, so `regress.py` and Mac runs need nothing), the Mac app's `Resources` (`pack_app.py --control-nacp`), the iOS app's `dm/control.nacp` (`build_ipa.sh`, which `main.m` names). No control data is a `[RED]` line and an error, not a guessed limit. The list is the `<DM_TEMP>_cache<N>` folders that exist: `OpenCacheStorageList` takes a snapshot (one `readdir` of their parent), `ReadCacheStorageList` hands it out in index order as 0x20-byte `CacheStorageInfo` records (the index, the rest zero), and the handle is a list number, never a host pointer. A storage's size is the one it was created with, as on Horizon: `CreateCacheStorage` records it beside the folder (`<folder>.size`, which the list skips; creating it again keeps it, `DeleteCacheStorage` removes it), and `GetCacheStorageSize` reports it. A folder with no record (made by `MountCacheStorage`, an older host or the player) gets the control data's default (`CacheStorageSize` / `CacheStorageJournalSize` at 0x3170 / 0x3178), else 256 MB of each; a storage that does not exist is `ResultTargetNotFound`.
+- **Found on:** Just Dance 25.0.3 (0100BEE017FC0000, SDK 14; no profile in this repository, [issue #1049](https://github.com/seedds/nxnative/issues/1049)): a diagnostic host passed the crash, mounted nine cache storages and read the offline save. [Tears of the Kingdom](../../dm/games/0100F2C0115B6000/README.md) created cache storage 0 (54,198,272 bytes, journal 180,224) and mounted it as `savct`, then `savc`, at every boot because the list was empty; from its second run on it now finds it.
+- **Open:** the record layout was read from SDK 14's `ReadCacheStorageList` only. Tears of the Kingdom's second boot with the list is not run yet (no build of it on the test Mac); no other local game makes cache storage on its route (OCTOPATH TRAVELER 0 and II, Princess Peach: Showtime!, Yoshi and the Mysterious Book and Orbitals list it and find none).
+
+### Add-on content mount cache sized as RomFS working memory
+
+- **Symptom:** SIGSEGV in `crypto::detail::CbcModeAes128Helper::DecryptBlocks` with a null destination (`main+0x8737e8`, right after `aoc:` is mounted).
+- **Cause:** the add-on content (0100152000023001: `contents.dat` and `version.txt`, the courses are in the update) is decrypted in `AocHeap`, a 0x3130-byte heap that also holds the mount cache. The host answered `QueryMountAddOnContentCacheSize` with 4 KB, and the 0x1400-byte decryption buffer then did not fit.
+- **Fix:** on Horizon the cache is the RomFS's file table as `RomFsFileSystem::GetRequiredWorkingMemorySize` sizes it: both hash tables (4 bytes a bucket, the builder's bucket count) and all directory and file entries (0x18 / 0x20 bytes plus the name, padded to 4), 0x88 bytes for these two files. The host computes that from the folder it mounts (`romfs_work_size` in `hle_fs.c`); `rom` keeps its minimum answer, which is too small rather than too large.
+- **Found on:** [Mario Kart 8 Deluxe](../../dm/games/0100152000022000/README.md) (4.0.0, SDK 4.4.0; one boot).
+
+### Add-on content queries on a small guest stack
+
+- **Symptom:** SIGBUS in `__vfprintf` under `aoc_load`, from `h_aoc_Count` (a store to `0x…67ff0`, right below a page of the guest thread's stack).
+- **Cause:** the first add-on content query lists `DM_AOC_DIR` and logs, a few KB of stack, and the game asks from a thread with little left.
+- **Fix:** `aoc::CountAddOnContent` and `ListAddOnContent` run on the big host stack (`hle_misc.c`; since 2026-11-13 every take-over does: [Host calls on small guest stacks](memory.md#host-calls-on-small-guest-stacks)).
+- **Found on:** [Digimon Story Cyber Sleuth: Complete Edition](../../dm/games/010014E00DB56000/README.md) (SDK 6.4.0; one boot).
+
+### An add-on content mount name used again after Unmount
+
+- **Symptom:** the game shows the add-on content version error (`game asks to show an error: add-on content version mismatch`) after its title screen, with every add-on in `DM_AOC_DIR`; with `DM_FS=1` the first add-on's file opens and the second's is not found under the same mount name (Pokémon Friends: `AVCtitle:/AocVersion/AocVersion_DLC01.json` opens, `AocVersion_DLC02.json` is `fs stat not found`).
+- **Cause:** the game checks each add-on in turn under one mount name: `MountAddOnContent("AVCtitle", index)`, read its version file, `Unmount`. On Horizon Unmount releases the name. The host's mount table is permanent and its Unmount a no-op, and a mount under a name in use answers success with the old target, so the second add-on's mount still showed the first one's files.
+- **Fix:** an add-on content mount releases its name at Unmount (`hle_fs.c` `aoc_release`): the entry is marked released, which no name lookup matches (`mnt_is`). The entry and its image stay, as an open image file names its entry by index, and a later mount of the same image under any name takes that entry back (`mount_aoc`), so cycling through the add-ons does not grow the table. Save, RomFS and other mounts keep the permanent table.
+- **Found on:** Pokémon Friends (`010008C01E742000`, update 1.1.5, two add-ons; no profile in the repository yet). Its expected versions are one string, `"3"`, compared for equality (`AocVersionCheker.Check`), which both add-ons hold.
+
+## Input
+
+### Controller disconnect between style and player lookup
+
+- **Symptom:** clang reports a negative shift in `fill` or `vib_targets` (`hle_hid.c`).
+- **Cause:** a style check looked up the connected player, then input or vibration routing looked it up again. A disconnect between the reads made the second result -1, used in `1u << player`.
+- **Fix:** look up the player once and derive the style from that same result before constructing the player mask.
+- **Found on:** clang static analysis ([issue #424](https://github.com/seedds/nxnative/issues/424)); no gameplay failure reported.
+
+### Steam Controller hotplug interrupts other players
+
+- **Symptom:** connecting a Steam Controller clears another controller's held input or rumble and makes it press a button to rejoin; a composite controller can appear as two players or lose native fallback after a partial exclusive open.
+- **Cause:** resetting every native profile covered the native/HID notification race, but discarded unrelated players. Independent opens of native, generic gamepad and vendor siblings could leave half of one controller seized. A generic reader with a separate slot table or an auto-opening HID manager could also allocate a duplicate player or prevent the exclusive claim. Dropping a deferred matching callback lost that generic controller until reconnect.
+- **Fix:** `controllers_mac.m` completes synchronous HID discovery before admitting a fresh, non-neutral native event. It gates only unadmitted snapshots and preserves established slots and the merged rumble owner. USB device ancestry, per-port Puck interface ancestry, or the OS physical-device ID groups sibling claims; report metadata selects the Triton endpoint. Opens commit together, suppression handles have no decoder, and failure releases only that group. Generic HID uses the same registry, slot allocation, shared mapping and focus gate, with a separate transport that never receives Triton output reports. Both managers use independent devices and open only after admission. Standard nonprotocol collections without physical identity or Puck interface metadata retain generic fallback when no conflicting raw route exists; ambiguous new raw claims wait instead of guessing a port. Deferred generic matches are retained and reconsidered when discovery changes, then released on unplug. See [Mac controller behavior and fallback](../mac-controllers.md#steam-controller-2026).
+- **Found on:** synthetic native/HID race, ownership-failure and mixed-controller regressions in `dm/host/tests/t_controllers_mac.m`; physical controller/game validation remains pending.
+
+### Steam Controller bypasses the rumble cutoff
+
+- **Symptom:** `DM_RUMBLE_MIN` silences quiet effects on native controllers but has no effect on the Steam Controller's direct HID output.
+- **Cause:** the direct adapter converted the game's vibration bands and gain into motor reports without applying the cutoff used by the Core Haptics and DualShock 4 routes.
+- **Fix:** all three routes share `dm_haptics_apple_controller_intensity`. The Steam adapter applies it independently to each motor after band conversion and gain, before encoding the output report. Guest vibration values are unchanged. Replacing a DualShock route with a direct output also releases that player's old HID claim, preserving other players' claims.
+- **Found on:** synthetic regressions in `dm/host/tests/t_controllers_mac.m` and `t_haptics_apple.m`, covering packet mapping, gain before cutoff, threshold/rescaling and route replacement. No new hardware or game validation.
+
+### Steam Controller stick drift suppresses gyro aiming
+
+- **Symptom:** six-axis values reach the game but gyro aiming does nothing, or stops after using a stick.
+- **Cause:** the raw Triton reports contain small nonzero stick values at rest. Passing them through as intentional input prevents a game that prioritizes stick input from using motion aiming.
+- **Fix (resolved in the decoder):** apply a radial deadzone to both sticks in `steam_input.h`, default 10%, rescaling the remaining travel to full range. `DM_STEAM_DEADZONE` accepts 0 to below 0.9; invalid values retain the default. Trackpads and motion remain unchanged. Decoder regressions cover all three report formats, centre drift, radial boundaries, travel, diagonals and settings.
+- **Found on:** [Tears of the Kingdom](../../dm/games/0100F2C0115B6000/README.md#services); the contributor's USB-C test confirmed gyro aiming with the proposed deadzone. No new local gameplay validation.
+
+### History reads fill every requested entry
+
+- **Symptom:** buttons and menus work, the sticks do nothing in play (Super Mario Odyssey: its first hint, "Look Around", waited forever); an `nn::diag` abort with Result 0x2aca in the first frames of `nnMain` (Hollow Knight: Silksong, Unity 6000.0.50f1: `bt: sdk+0x514580 main+0x1296c64 …`).
+- **Cause:** games read whole histories and ignore the returned count. Odyssey reads all 16 entries of `GetNpadStates`; the host filled only the newest. It polls every controller slot each frame, which moves the shared sampling number on by about 16, so the stick samples it combined were mostly empty, while buttons and the stick's direction bits (which menus use) still came through. Silksong's `main+0x1296c60` calls `hid::GetDebugPadStates(states, 16)` every frame, and the SDK's own reads a debug pad resource nothing initialized (module 202, hid); the host had only the single-state `GetDebugPadState`.
+- **Fix:** the host now fills every requested `GetNpadStates` entry with the current state under consecutive sampling numbers; the `GetDebugPadStates` history form answers every requested entry as not connected, newest first with consecutive sampling numbers (`hle_hid.c`). `DM_KEYS` takes `RLeft` / `RUp` / `RRight` / `RDown` for the right stick.
+- **Found on:** [Super Mario Odyssey](../../dm/games/0100000000010000/README.md); [Hollow Knight: Silksong](../../dm/games/010013C00E930000/README.md).
+- **Note:** Odyssey's six-axis history is read the same way ([Six-axis sensors](#six-axis-sensors)).
+
+### Npad style set update event
+
+- **Symptom:** the game never reads the controller (the title's "Press L+R" never sees a press; no `GetNpadState` call at all).
+- **Cause:** it waits for the event bound by `hid::BindNpadStyleSetUpdateEvent` before reading.
+- **Fix:** the host now signals it once when it is bound for a connected controller, and again when a player's controller connects or goes away.
+- **Found on:** [The Legend of Zelda: Link's Awakening](../../dm/games/01006BB00C6F0000/README.md) (SDK 8.2.0).
+
+### No input after the console mode changes at run time
+
+- **Symptom:** the game runs on but takes no input, from the touch gamepad or a controller, after the console mode is switched at run time (to handheld and back to docked), until it is restarted.
+- **Cause:** a mode change moves player 1 between the Handheld npad and No1, as taking the Joy-Cons off the console's rails or putting them back does, and the hid service signals the style update event of every npad whose controller came or went. The host pushed notification 30 and signalled none, and it kept no event bound to Handheld (0x20) at all. A game that waits for those events stays on the npad it has: Hades II, sent to Handheld by the controller applet in handheld mode, kept reading it once docked, where it is empty.
+- **Fix:** one function, `styles_sync` (`hle_hid.c`), signals the bound event of each npad (No1..No8 and Handheld) whose style is no longer the one the game was last told, and everything a style depends on calls it after its change: the console mode (`dm_set_opmode`, before the notification), the players and their Joy-Cons, the ids and styles the game supports, its Joy-Con assignment. A split pair's second npad, which handheld mode takes back, is signalled with the rest. `t_hid.c` (`t_opmode`) and `t_oe.c` cover it.
+- **Found on:** [Hades II](../../dm/games/0100A00019DE0000/README.md) ([issue #2143](https://github.com/seedds/nxnative/issues/2143)); on the Mac, `DM_OE_SCRIPT="opmode=0@15,opmode=1@21.5"` on the note's route reproduces it (the applet answers at the press between the two).
+
+### Six-axis sensors
+
+- **Symptom:** motion moves never happen; shaking does nothing (Super Mario Odyssey: cap throws by shaking).
+- **Cause:** the host handed out six-axis handles but reported no sensor.
+- **Fix:** it reports one combined sensor per FullKey or Handheld controller, two for dual Joy-Cons and one for a single Joy-Con, with the SDK style index, npad id and device in the handle ([handle encoding](#six-axis-handle-encoding)). At rest the sensor lies flat: acceleration (0, 0, −1) G, no rotation, identity direction. Samples are 5 ms apart on the guest clock and numbered by time, so a history read returns the samples at those times. `DM_BTN_SHAKE` (`dm_input.h`: the iPhone shaken, Space on the Mac, `DM_KEYS` `Shake`) plays a side-to-side shake in whole cycles.
+- **Found on:** [Super Mario Odyssey](../../dm/games/0100000000010000/README.md): it reads all 16 history entries per sensor and ignores the count, like its npad history ([History reads fill every requested entry](#history-reads-fill-every-requested-entry)).
+- **Note:** find a game's swing detector by probing the accelerometer value it reads (`DM_PROBE=main:626248@x8+10` in Odyssey 1.4.1).
+
+### Switch 2 controllers over Bluetooth LE
+
+- **Symptom:** a Joy-Con 2 or Pro Controller 2 does not appear in the Mac's Bluetooth menu, or as a GameController or HID device; the game sees no controller.
+- **Cause:** these controllers are Bluetooth LE devices with Nintendo's own GATT service, not HID gamepads (Apple's controller driver list on macOS 27.2 has no Switch 2 products), and a Joy-Con 2 forgets a connection the moment it drops: one that is let go stays silent until Sync is held again, unless it is bonded to the host.
+- **Fix:** `switch2_proto.h` (pure C: the advertisement and what the host does with it, command framing, bonding, the input report, stick calibration, the motion maps) and `pad_switch2_apple.m` (a CoreBluetooth central the Mac and iOS front ends share, behind the off-by-default `DM_SW2`). A controller held in Sync advertises no host: on the Mac the host bonds it with the console's `0x15` commands (this Mac's Bluetooth address, then two keys, which may be fresh random ones), and from then on a button press makes it advertise toward this Mac and the host connects. A controller advertising another host is never written to. After connecting, the host runs the handshake (identity read, stick calibration, player lights, features) and takes the 63-byte reports; a player light write once a second keeps the link up.
+- **Report layout (a left and a right Joy-Con 2, macOS 27):** the controller's own clock in milliseconds at offset 0 (four bytes; +30 a report on a Mac, +60 or +90 when one was lost), buttons at offset 4 (the same bits as the Pro Controller 2; the right Joy-Con's rail buttons are `0x10` / `0x20`, the left's `0x100000` / `0x200000`), the left Joy-Con's stick in the first stick field and the right one's in the second, both calibrated from the first slot (`0x130A8`); accelerometer at `0x30`, gyro at `0x36`. A Joy-Con held upright reports raw x right, y forward, z up, and its gyro channels are right-handed rotations about the same axes, which is the Switch frame, so its map is the identity for rotation and the negated reading for gravity. The gyro scale is 0.0607 degrees per second per count, measured: 14 tilts from lying on an edge to standing against a wall (90 degrees by the accelerometer) integrated to 1.153 times as much at the Switch family's nominal 0.070, on both Joy-Cons. Each sample is timed by the controller's clock, not by when its notification arrived (they come in bursts, and a lost report leaves a gap only the clock shows). At rest the gyro reads up to about 1.5 degrees per second off, a fixed offset per controller: it is there from the first sample, the one learned for that controller before (kept under its serial number) or, for a right Joy-Con 2, its factory offset (16 bytes at `0x13040`: a temperature and the offset in radians per second, which matches its rest offset); about a second of nearly constant samples refines it.
+- **Rumble:** each controller lists its own vibration channel (the right Joy-Con 2's `FA19B0FB-CD1F-46A7-84A1-BBB09E00C149`, the left one's `289326CB-A471-485D-A8F4-240C14F18241`). The report is a zero byte and a 16-byte block per actuator: `0x40 | samples << 4 | sequence`, then up to three five-byte samples, played in turn. A sample is two bands, low then high, each a 10-bit frequency code and a 10-bit amplitude. The frequency code is logarithmic, 96 steps an octave, with 333 at 110 Hz (about 9.9 Hz to 16 kHz); the amplitude is linear, 1023 at full; both bands together are limited to 0.98. Hertz and amplitude are rounded as the console rounds them: truncate, then the nearer of that code and the next. Checked against the Switch 2's own encoder on 200,000 random values: no byte differed. An earlier reading (hertz as the code, a single sample per block) played effects 3 to 5 times too low in pitch and dropped two samples of three. A game sets its vibration about every 5 ms, and each value plays once in its own 5 ms sample, three to a packet every 15 ms (`switch2_proto.h`, Rumble timing), as the console plays them: a one-step pulse stays one sample long. Before that, a value played until the next packet, so short pulses lasted two to three times as long; replayed against a recording of Nintendo Switch 2 Welcome Tour's rumble exhibits, their energy came to 1.8 to 2.2 times the console's, and now matches it. The game's two full-key motors reach the pad through `haptics_apple.m` (`dm_haptics_apple_set_sink`, with Rumble strength and cutoff applied): each Joy-Con of a pair plays its side, a Joy-Con alone the stronger of the two, a Pro Controller 2 one per grip (rumble [checked in Echoes of Wisdom](../../dm/games/01008CF01BAAC000/README.md#controller-rumble)). The console also corrects the amplitude by frequency with tables of its own per controller model; that is not done here.
+- **Bonding (a left and a right Joy-Con 2):** each bonded with fresh random keys, then woke toward the Mac on a button press and reconnected with no Sync. The answer to the first key was the same bytes on both Joy-Cons and with any key; the answer to the second changes with the key.
+- **Permission:** macOS asks once for each game app, at the first scan. A rebuilt app with the same bundle ID asked again (a fresh ad-hoc signature); a second start of the same app did not.
+- **Found on:** a left and a right Joy-Con 2 on an M1 Pro Mac, macOS 27.2: [Metroid Dread](../../dm/games/010093801237C000/README.md) (both woke and played as one pair, pairing by Sync during the game, the Home quit prompt; a Joy-Con paired back to a Switch 2 woke for it and was left alone, `woke for another host`, until Sync paired it with the Mac again) and [Super Mario Odyssey](../../dm/games/0100000000010000/README.md) (motion: the cap throw by shaking, from the pair's right Joy-Con; rumble on both Joy-Cons).
+- **Note:** protocol credit: the community's reverse engineering of these controllers ([Finally the Controller Works](https://github.com/Peterksharma/switch2mac), ndeadly/switch2_controller_research, trevlars/switch2-controllers-linux, Nadeflore/switch2-controllers); the code was written from those notes. A Pro Controller 2 (an M4 Pro Mac, Super Mario Bros. Wonder 1.2.0, [issue #1267](https://github.com/seedds/nxnative/issues/1267)) paired by Sync on the first try and sends the Joy-Con 2's report layout (GR `0x1000000`, GL `0x2000000`, C `0x4000`), with full stick travel from its factory calibration and Home's quit prompt; its rumble channel came from the notes and is now [checked in Echoes of Wisdom](../../dm/games/01008CF01BAAC000/README.md#controller-rumble), and its gyro scale is the Joy-Con 2's, not measured on it. Its motion map is measured: [A Pro Controller 2's motion turned a quarter turn](#a-pro-controller-2s-motion-turned-a-quarter-turn). This Mac's own address comes from `IOBluetoothHostController`, read once the app may use Bluetooth. On iOS there is no API for the device's own address, so a controller in pairing mode is used there without bonding. The Joy-Con 2's mouse sensor: [The Joy-Con 2 mouse sensor and pointer](#the-joy-con-2-mouse-sensor-and-pointer).
+
+### A Switch 2 controller's stick at rest reads as in use
+
+- **Symptom:** a game that turns a feature off while a stick is in use keeps it off after the stick is let go, some of the time. Breath of the Wild with a Pro Controller 2: motion aiming did nothing on about half the aims, and from the first use of the right stick on.
+- **Cause:** each stick axis had a dead zone of 40 raw counts and no scale past it. A Pro Controller 2's right stick comes to rest 0 to 60 counts off its calibrated centre, so after a release it read 0 or a constant 3 to 5 % (41 counts was already 3.4 % of a 1200-count travel). Breath of the Wild decides at the start of an aim whether the right stick is in use.
+- **Fix:** `s2_cal_apply` (`switch2_proto.h`) uses a round dead zone, and past it scales the deflection back to start at 0, as `pad_hid_stick` does, so there is no jump at the edge and a small diagonal movement keeps both axes. Its size is the stick's own. Each stick's factory block, which the host now reads whole (0x31 bytes at `0x13080` / `0x130C0`), holds 0x28 bytes of model constants before its calibration. These match Switch 1's "stick parameters", whose third value is the dead zone the console applies. The third value here is 160 counts on a Pro Controller 2 (10 % of its nominal travel, the last pair, 1583) and 147 on a Joy-Con 2 (12.6 % of 1167), read from dumps in ndeadly/switch2_controller_research and GP2040-CE. A value outside 3 to 25 % of the stick's travel, or a blank block, falls back to 10 %. The log names the dead zone at connect (`[sw2] ... dead zone`).
+- **Open:** that the third value is the dead zone is inferred from the Switch 1 layout and the two models' values, not read from the Switch 2's hid code. The right stick's user calibration is read at `0x1FC062` (ndeadly's layout, past the 2-byte magic); SDL reads it at `0x1FC082`. This matters only after a stick calibration on a console.
+- **Found on:** [The Legend of Zelda: Breath of the Wild](../../dm/games/01007EF00011E000/README.md), a Pro Controller 2 on a Mac, 1.9.0 ([issue #2245](https://github.com/seedds/nxnative/issues/2245)).
+
+### A Pro Controller 2's motion turned a quarter turn
+
+- **Symptom:** with a Pro Controller 2, tilting the controller forward turns the game's view sideways, as a roll.
+- **Cause:** the Pro Controller 2 took the Pro Controller 1's motion map. Its raw frame is the Joy-Con 2 pair's: flat and still its accelerometer read `-164, -584, 4193` (z up), and held with its top tipped up `-73, 3227, 2597` (+0.79 G on y, forward). The old map turned that tilt into about 50 degrees of roll.
+- **Fix:** `s2_map_pro` is the pair's map (`switch2_proto.h`). y and z are measured, which fixes x in a right-handed frame, and the gyro shares the accelerometer's axes (one ICM-42670-P chip). `t_switch2` checks both recorded poses.
+- **Found on:** [The Legend of Zelda: Breath of the Wild](../../dm/games/01007EF00011E000/README.md), a Pro Controller 2 on a Mac, 1.9.0 ([issue #2245](https://github.com/seedds/nxnative/issues/2245)).
+
+### Joy-Cons as dual or single npads
+
+- **Symptom:** a pair of Joy-Cons is one Pro-style controller: the game cannot see a dual controller with two sensors, one Joy-Con cannot be a sideways player with its rail buttons, and a game's single / dual assignment calls (`SetNpadJoyAssignmentModeSingle` / `Dual`) change nothing; a game that wants two players from one pair (its controller screen) gets one.
+- **Cause:** every controller was a full-key (or handheld) npad: `style_for_player` knew only those two styles, the styles the game supports were recorded but never consulted, `fill` kept the low 16 button bits (no SL / SR), the vibration handles knew types 3 and 4 only, and the assignment calls were ignored.
+- **Fix:** a front end that reads Joy-Cons one by one reports them to the npad layer (`dm_joy_attach`, `dm_joy_set`, `dm_motion_set_joy` in `dm_input.h`, no controller-specific names); nothing changes until one does. Docked, a pad that holds both Joy-Cons is one JoyDual npad (both Joy-Cons' buttons, rail buttons included, each stick on its side, a six-axis handle per Joy-Con) when the game supports JoyDual; when the game asks for single assignment, the left Joy-Con stays on the npad (JoyLeft, or the one the game named) and the other takes the lowest free supported npad (JoyRight); one Joy-Con alone is JoyLeft or JoyRight. The controller applet splits a pair when the game wants more players than there are controllers. A game that supports no Joy-Con style gets the pad's merged state as a whole controller: full-key, or JoyDual when the game supports JoyDual but not full-key ([A controller in a style the game does not support](#a-controller-in-a-style-the-game-does-not-support)). Vibration handles exist for the Joy-Con styles (type 5, 6, 7) and reach the motor of their Joy-Con, on whichever npad it is. Handheld mode: player 1's Joy-Cons count as attached to the console (the Handheld npad) and are not split; a player 2 Joy-Con pair is as docked.
+- **Choosing on the controllers:** the Switch 2 transport pairs a left and a right Joy-Con that connect; holding SL + SR on one of a pair for three seconds gives it a player of its own, and holding L on a left one alone and R on a right one alone for three seconds pairs them (`s2_gesture`), as a Switch's Change Grip/Order screen does, without its screen.
+- **Found on:** a left and a right Joy-Con 2 on a Mac:
+
+  | Game | Before (every controller full-key) | After |
+  |---|---|---|
+  | [Super Mario Odyssey](../../dm/games/0100000000010000/README.md) 1.3.0 | a pair was one Pro-style pad with one sensor; two-player mode could not take a Joy-Con each | a JoyDual npad with a sensor per Joy-Con: the two-handed throws (both flicked up, both shaken sideways); two-player mode splits the pair through the controller applet (JoyLeft for Mario, JoyRight for Cappy); rumble on both, also through the full-key handles the game keeps sending to |
+  | [Super Mario Bros. Wonder](../../dm/games/010015100B514000/README.md) 1.2.1 | a pair was one controller: its two-player screen found one and was canceled | choosing two players splits the pair (single assignment): one sideways Joy-Con each |
+  | [Super Smash Bros. Ultimate](../../dm/games/01006A800016E000/README.md) 13.0.5 | a pair was one Pro-style pad; one Joy-Con could never be a fighter of its own | holding SL + SR splits the pair into two sideways players; a 1v1 with a Joy-Con each, the rail buttons as shoulders, rumble on each |
+
+### A controller in a style the game does not support
+
+- **Symptom:** docked, the game opens the controller applet again every frame and never leaves the screen behind it. The log repeats `oe library applet: controller support`, `hid controller applet: 1 to 1 players wanted, 1 connected` and focus 2, then 1, thousands of times (7,162 in 3 minutes), after `hid supported controller styles 0x6`.
+- **Cause:** the host showed every whole controller (the keyboard, a Pro-like pad, a Joy-Con pair the game supports no Joy-Con style for) as full-key on No1..No8, whatever styles the game supports. The hid service connects a controller only in a supported style, so a game may take any connected npad as one of its own. Nintendo Switch Sports Resort sets Handheld | JoyDual (0x6) for its Tennis lobby (`main+0xa9e940`); its player check (`main+0x58d0f0`) found no usable player 1 on a full-key No1, so its caller (`main+0x3f9d120`) opened `ShowControllerSupport` again, and the applet's answer (one player, No1) could not change that.
+- **Fix:** on No1..No8, a whole controller is shown as a JoyDual pair when the game supports JoyDual but not full-key (`pad_style`, `hle_hid.c`): JoyDual is the only other style with all its buttons and both sticks. Its JoyDual state, two six-axis sensors, type 5 vibration handles and the applet's selected npad follow; full-key handles taken earlier still reach its motors. A change of supported styles that changes an npad's style signals that npad's style update event. This holds in both modes: in handheld mode players 2..8 (No2..No8) are shown as JoyDual too, and only player 1's Handheld npad stays Handheld. A game that supports full-key, or neither style, is unchanged.
+- **Found on:** [Nintendo Switch Sports Resort](../../dm/games/04008E001F098000/README.md) (`04008E001F098000`, v65536), on the Mac with the keyboard and `DM_KEYS`: after the fix the applet is not opened once in 220 s, and Play leads to its one-controller screen ("press the button") and into a doubles Tennis match at its serve prompt.
+- **Check:** `t_hid` (`t_unsupported_style`).
+
+### Live controller motion
+
+- **Symptom:** motion controls do nothing with a controller that has a gyroscope (Breath of the Wild: aiming with its Motion Controls option on); the six-axis sensor always lies flat.
+- **Cause:** the host's six-axis sensor had no source of motion: flat at rest, apart from the `DM_BTN_SHAKE` shake ([Six-axis sensors](#six-axis-sensors)).
+- **Fix:** a controller with a gyroscope drives its player's sensor on the Mac and iOS (`GCMotion`, `motion_apple.h` → `dm_motion_set`, `hle_hid.c`); on iOS the phone's own device motion drives player 1's while no controller supplies fresh motion (`DmGamepad.m`). The orientation is integrated from the gyro with tilt correction toward the measured gravity (`motion_fusion.h`), in `SixAxisSensorState`'s units (G, revolutions/s, revolutions). After 300 ms without a sample the source expires; without another source the sensor lies flat again. A shake still adds on top. The switches are in [development.md, How it works](../development.md#how-it-works) (Input).
+- **Source routing (resolved, review of PR #351):** sharing one integrator let phone and controller callbacks mix orientations, including with `DM_PHONE_MOTION=1`; checking only `hasRotationRate` also let zero-only controller reports suppress the phone. Each source now has its own orientation and timestamp: auto prefers fresh controller data, forced mode selects only the phone, and the Apple adapter waits for actual data before publishing. One-player games merge motion from the same controller slots as buttons. Motion settings initialize once with `pthread_once`; per-source locks also protect diagnostic timestamps. `t_hid` covers routing, expiry and concurrent startup under ThreadSanitizer; `t_motion_apple` covers activation, zero-only reports and detachment.
+- **A gyroscope advertised but not there (iOS, PR #1877):** the GameSir X5 Lite has no gyroscope, but GameController advertises rotation data; a few seconds after attachment, the phone's gyro stopped driving player 1 ([NX2Apple issue #971](https://github.com/LNPANDY/NX2Apple/issues/971)). The log contains 26 identical printed controller reports, including a nonzero rotation rate. The Apple adapter (`motion_apple.h`) accepted any report that was not all zeroes, keeping the controller fresh and preferred over the phone. `DM_PHONE_MOTION=1` already forces the phone, including when a genuine controller gyro is attached.
+- **Controller liveness heuristic (platform workaround):** each attachment withholds controller samples until a raw rotation component differs exactly from its first report; acceleration alone does not qualify it. Once qualified, all later reports, including constant or zero values, pass until detachment. This compensates for incorrect platform/device capability reporting without a controller-model or game rule. Motion or sensor noise is expected to vary a working gyro, but constant output does not prove a sensor is missing, and varying fabricated output could pass. `t_motion_apple` covers constant zero/nonzero rotation, nonzero acceleration, qualification, later zero samples and reset on reattachment. **Pending:** an iPhone retest in Breath of the Wild with GameSir attachment/reconnection, continued phone aiming and a genuine gyro controller's takeover. The original log rounds rotation to three decimal places in revolutions/s, so it does not establish the exact raw equality this heuristic needs.
+- **Upright initialization (resolved, review of PR #351):** the heading reference was parallel to gravity when the phone started upright, so projection produced a zero vector and silently left the orientation flat. A nonparallel reference now primes the orientation on the first sample; `t_motion` covers all six axis-aligned starting poses.
+- **Back from the background (iOS):** iOS turns a manual-activation controller's sensors (DualSense, DualShock 4) off while the app is in the background and leaves them off, so the gyro stayed dead until the controller reconnected (reported by MacNativeGames on the iPad). Becoming active again sets `sensorsActive` once more for every attached controller (`dm_motion_rearm`, `front_ios.m`); not tested on a device here.
+- **Found on:** [The Legend of Zelda: Breath of the Wild](../../dm/games/01007EF00011E000/README.md) (1.9.0): motion aiming on a Mac with a DualSense. The iOS paths have not been tested on a device.
+- **History (resolved):** history entries and Switch 2 IMU answers used to repeat the newest sample, so the SDK's sensor fusion saw a staircase instead of a 200 Hz stream. Each live stream now keeps its last 128 samples stamped on the guest clock (`live_motion.ring`, `hle_hid.c`); every 5 ms tick takes the sample at its own time, interpolated between its neighbours (the direction as a normalized blend of the two rotations), the newest sample held past its time. The ring holds host times, turned into guest time with the clock's lag when read, so a freeze or `DM_DETCLOCK` keeps them in order. An answer ends at the clock's tick and never below the last one answered for that handle (`answer_tick`, a high-water mark per handle), so the sampling numbers never go back and keep up with the clock: ending at the newest sample's tick (the first version) took them back when a first sample came late or a player's GameController and HID streams took turns, and stalled them for a game polling faster than its controller. `t_hid.c` feeds 100, 125, 200 and 833 Hz with jittered sample times and checks consecutive ticks, the clock's tick at the end, the signal at each tick and the six-axis history, and keeps the numbers monotone through a late first sample, two streams with different latencies, fast polling, a stale source, a freeze and equal or out-of-order stamps; `t_hid_tsan` runs a writer against a reader.
+- **Playout delay:** a source sends a sample every 15 to 30 ms (a DualSense about 67 a second, a Joy-Con 2 33) while a Switch 2 game reads 5 ms ticks up to the present, so read at the present most ticks lie past the newest sample and can only hold it (76 to 79% of a Joy-Con 2's ticks in one session, in runs of 4 to 9). The IMU samples now play each live stream back late by about one sample interval (`DM_IMU_DELAY`, default `auto`: the 90th percentile of how long after a sample the next one arrives, over the last 32, capped at 40 ms; `motion_lag`, `motion_fusion.h`), a change of delay slewed at 5% of the guest time so the data never goes back or jumps, none on a frozen or `DM_DETCLOCK` clock. In the host's tests at 33 Hz (a Joy-Con 2's clock, arrivals up to 4 ms late) and 67 Hz (jittered arrivals), no tick of the steady state held a sample, against 75% and 52% read at the present. The six-axis states (`GetSixAxisSensorStates`) keep no delay by default (`DM_SIX_DELAY=0`): a Switch 1 game reads the newest state each frame, aiming or steering, and a delay would only add latency.
+- **Joy-Cons shown as a pair:** a game that supports JoyDual but no single Joy-Con style is shown a single Joy-Con as a pair ([A controller in a style the game does not support](#a-controller-in-a-style-the-game-does-not-support)); both of its handles read that Joy-Con's own stream, in the upright frame a pair's sensor has, not the pad's merged stream (a sideways Joy-Con's frame, a quarter turn away). A real L + R pair gives each handle its own half, also in another player's slot of a one-player game; a pair that loses a half leaves that half's handle flat instead of switching it to the other Joy-Con mid-game, and a Joy-Con that has reported its own motion and goes quiet while attached gives its handle the flat sensor, not the merged stream.
+- **Joy-Con 2 sample times:** the front end times a sample by the controller's clock plus an anchor, about the least latency seen (`s2_clock_sample`, `switch2_proto.h`): the anchor rises 1 ms a second, so a controller clock running slow no longer drifts its samples toward 60 ms behind their arrival, and falls only on two reports in a row that came earlier than it predicts, to the later of the two, so one unusually quick report does not move it; a sample keeps at least half a report's step after the last where its arrival allows. `DM_SW2_VERBOSE` also logs the report bytes the parser does not read (0x2A..0x2D read like a microsecond counter, maybe the sensor's own timestamp: unconfirmed).
+- **Diagnostics:** `DM_IMU_LOG=<n>` logs every n-th raw sample per stream as the host receives it and every n-th `GetNpadImuSamples` answer.
+- **Note:** yaw drifts as on the console, with no recenter. The console's own sensor still reports no states ([SDK 20 six-axis sensor and amiibo (`nfp`) calls](#sdk-20-six-axis-sensor-and-amiibo-nfp-calls)), so motion follows the controller in docked mode.
+- **Open:** [PR #286](https://github.com/seedds/nxnative/pull/286) has iPad aiming measurements and calibration controls to compare on a device before choosing shared defaults. Related reports: [#153](https://github.com/seedds/nxnative/issues/153), [#389](https://github.com/seedds/nxnative/issues/389).
+
+### Non-finite motion samples
+
+- **Symptom:** a non-finite accelerometer or gyro sample corrupts the six-axis fields; later valid samples do not restore the previous fusion state.
+- **Cause:** `motion_update` published raw NaN/Infinity and integrated it into persistent angle/orientation. Finite but extreme rates can also overflow the float rotation norm or accumulated angle.
+- **Fix:** reject a non-finite sensor sample before priming or changing any state. A non-finite time step skips integration, and a non-finite gain disables tilt correction. Do not commit an overflowing rotation step or angle; normal finite samples keep the same arithmetic and coordinate conventions.
+- **Found on:** synthetic `t_motion` samples, not a reproduced game or controller fault. Tests cover each sensor axis before/after priming, subsequent valid input, invalid timing/gain, finite overflow, all 48 signed frame maps and small-angle accumulation. No device or gameplay result is claimed for this change.
+
+### The phone's motion in the picture's frame
+
+- **Symptom:** on iPhone Duo's inner display the phone's gyro is turned a quarter turn: tilting up and down moves the pointer left and right, and the other way round ([GitHub issue #569](https://github.com/seedds/nxnative/issues/569)); the outer display is right.
+- **Cause:** Core Motion delivered the phone's samples in the hardware frame, and `DmGamepad.m` turned them into the Switch frame by the interface orientation (`motion_phone_to_switch`, `motion_fusion.h`). That holds only where the hardware frame is the screen's portrait frame, as on a phone with one display. The Duo's inner display is a quarter turn from it, and it ignores the app's supported orientations.
+- **Fix:** on iOS 27 and later the pad view, full screen over the picture, is the motion manager's `deviceMotionBody`: Core Motion then delivers rotation rate, gravity and user acceleration in that view's frame, for whichever display shows it and however it is turned, which is the Switch frame as it is (x to the right of the picture, y to its top, z toward the player), and the orientation map is skipped. iOS 26 keeps the map. No device model or fold state is checked.
+- **Found on:** [Super Mario Galaxy](../../dm/games/010099C022B96000/README.md): the gyro pointer on the inner display, reported by klave7. Not yet tested on a device: on the Duo's inner and outer displays and on a phone with one display running iOS 27, `DM_MOTION_LOG=1` should read acc `0 0 -1` with the phone flat and still.
+- **Open:** while the scene is not full screen (Split View on the inner display) Core Motion falls back to the hardware frame without a flag that says so, and the samples are still taken as the view's frame.
+
+### Switch Pro Controller advertises motion but sends zeroes
+
+- **Symptom:** a Pro Controller's buttons work on the Mac, but its gyro pointer never moves; `GCMotion` callbacks arrive with acceleration and rotation all zero.
+- **Cause:** the macOS driver can expose empty motion through GameController while its underlying Nintendo HID `0x30` reports contain IMU data ([PR #296](https://github.com/seedds/nxnative/pull/296)). The OS driver lies outside this repository; the passive HID adapter is a workaround for that platform defect.
+- **Fix:** `motion_pro_mac.h` listens nonexclusively to vendor `057E`, product `2009`, without sending commands or changing the driver's configuration. `motion_pro.h` converts the three signed little-endian samples to G and rad/s in the Switch frame; their nominal 5 ms spacing reaches the shared integrator through `dm_motion_set_hid`. Each HID device owns its buffer and decoder. HID, GameController and phone orientations stay separate; usable GameController data wins, otherwise fresh HID data supplies that player's controller motion. Removal, focus loss or ambiguous routing clears only the HID source; 300 ms of silence expires it. `DM_MOTION_HID=0` disables the reader; `DM_MOTION=0` disables all motion.
+- **Routing limit:** GameController exposes no public per-controller HID identity. Raw motion is enabled only with one physical Pro and one GameController Pro, using that controller's existing player slot (including `DM_KBD_PLAYER=own` / `DM_PAD_MERGE`). Multiple Pros keep their GameController paths; raw data is never assigned to the first controller by guesswork. Each model the reader knows (a table in `motion_pro_mac.h`, with the [DualShock 4](#dualshock-4-over-bluetooth-advertises-motion-but-sends-zeroes)) is paired on its own, so another model connected beside the Pro does not make it ambiguous; under `DM_PAD_MERGE`, where two models share player 1, the first live model in the table keeps it. Every motion report rechecks routing, so a continuously streaming pad takes over when the preferred model has been silent for one second, even without a removal callback; the preferred model takes the player back on its first report. `t_motion_pro_mac` covers both transitions. Synthetic GameController HID devices are excluded with Apple's `GCSyntheticDevice` property.
+- **Open:** passive reading cannot obtain factory calibration without issuing SPI commands alongside the OS driver (identical pads read from their reports do issue that read, and it works: [Identical controllers that GameController lists as one](#identical-controllers-that-gamecontroller-lists-as-one)). Nominal scales are 4096 counts/G and 70 mdps/count ([protocol research](https://github.com/dekuNukem/Nintendo_Switch_Reverse_Engineering/blob/master/imu_sensor_notes.md)); no automatic bias learning removes slow intentional turns. Sample order follows that research and [Linux hid-nintendo](https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/master/drivers/hid/hid-nintendo.c); SDL reverses it. Confirm order, sensitivity, drift and reconnects on hardware before calling the reworked reader device-tested. `t_motion_pro`, `t_motion_pro_mac` and `t_hid` cover decoding, timestamps, ownership, removal, ambiguity and source fallback with synthetic inputs.
+- **Found on:** [Super Mario Galaxy](../../dm/games/010099C022B96000/README.md), docked pointer on a Pro Controller.
+
+### DualShock 4 over Bluetooth advertises motion but sends zeroes
+
+- **Symptom:** a DualShock 4 over Bluetooth is logged `with motion (gyro)` and the game reads the six-axis sensor every frame, but motion aiming does nothing; over USB it works ([issue #1770](https://github.com/seedds/nxnative/issues/1770)). With `DM_MOTION_LOG=1` no `[hid] motion player 1` line appears.
+- **Cause:** as with the [Pro Controller](#switch-pro-controller-advertises-motion-but-sends-zeroes), GameController's `GCMotion` for the pad sends only zeroes over Bluetooth, which `dm_motion_attach` rightly ignores, while the pad's own Bluetooth input report `0x11` carries the sensor. Over USB the pad sends report `0x01` and `GCMotion` works. The OS driver lies outside this repository.
+- **Fix:** the DualShock 4 is a row of the passive reader's model table (`motion_pro_mac.h`): vendor `054C`, products `05C4` (v1) and `09CC` (v2), report `0x11`, paired with GameController's `DualShock 4` controller. `ds4_motion_decode` (`motion_pro.h`) reads six signed little-endian words from byte 15 (USB's report `0x01` fields two bytes later, as Linux hid-playstation and SDL read them): rotation x, y, z, then acceleration, at the nominal 8192 counts/G and 2000 deg/s over 32767, into the Switch frame with the axis signs measured on a pad (MacNativeGames PR #1306). One sample per report, stamped on arrival; a stream that falls silent for 300 ms must show data again. A pad over USB never sends `0x11`, so its entry never counts and GameController's motion stays. The routing is the Pro Controller's, per model: one DualShock 4 that sends `0x11` and one GameController DualShock 4, whatever other models are connected.
+- **Open:** the factory calibration (a feature report the OS driver owns) is not read, and HID sources get no rest-offset learning, so a gyro bias would show as a slow drift at rest: not measured yet. `09CC` is matched by the same report and not yet tested on a pad. With one pad over USB and another over Bluetooth, GameController lists two and the Bluetooth one keeps its zero `GCMotion`. A controller that presents itself as a DualShock 4 (a GameSir Cyclone uses `054C:05C4`) and sends `0x11` with zeroes never becomes a source. `t_motion_pro` covers decoding, `t_motion_pro_mac` a DualShock 4 paired beside a Pro Controller.
+- **Found on:** [The Legend of Zelda: Ocarina of Time](../../dm/games/0400486024DEC000/README.md), slingshot aiming with a DualShock 4 v1 (`05C4`) on macOS 27.2 (M2 Pro); reported on Tears of the Kingdom with a v2 (`09CC`).
+
+### An original Joy-Con's sensors are read by the host
+
+- **Symptom:** on the Mac, an original (Switch 1) Joy-Con's buttons and sticks work through GameController but its motion does not: the six-axis sensor lies flat, so a game that steers or aims with it (Nintendo Switch Sports Resort: Archery, Boxing; Splatoon Raiders: motion aiming) does not follow the controller. A lone Joy-Con does not play at all, and a pair plays as one full-key controller whatever the game asks for.
+- **Cause:** macOS's GameController presents a paired L + R as one `GCController` ("Nintendo Switch Joy-Con (L/R)") with no `GCMotion` object at all, a lone Joy-Con with no extended gamepad profile, and gives a controller no HID identity, so a sensor read from IOKit cannot be tied to one of its controllers ([PR #2594](https://github.com/seedds/nxnative/pull/2594) routed them by a button press seen on both, a heuristic). The HID report its driver keeps sending (0x30, about 60 Hz) has the IMU fields zeroed: the sensor is off until the host asks for it, and the OS driver never does. The Pro Controller's passive reader ([Switch Pro Controller advertises motion but sends zeroes](#switch-pro-controller-advertises-motion-but-sends-zeroes)) does not fit: it only listens, while a Joy-Con has to be told to send its samples and to give its factory calibration over SPI.
+- **Fix:** the host reads each Joy-Con wholly from its own HID report, on the Mac only (`pad_jc1.h`, pure C, and `pad_jc1_mac.h`, IOKit), and `front_mac.m` leaves GameController's Joy-Con controllers unread (`jc1_gc_unread`, as identical pads' `hidpad_gc_unread`), keeping only Home and Capture away from macOS (`pad_keep_system_buttons`; Home held still opens the quit prompt, read from the report). It matches vendor 057E, products 2006 (left) and 2007 (right), opens the device nonexclusively and sends the console's handshake: device info (0x02), standard full mode (0x03 with 0x30), EnableIMU (0x40 with 0x01), the vibration on (0x48 with 0x01), SPI reads of the factory IMU calibration at 0x6020 and of the stick calibrations (factory 0x603D, user 0x8010, decoded by `pad_pro.h`: a left Joy-Con fills the left stick's block, a right one the right's), then the player lights (0x30), 60 ms apart as the console does, each step its own main-queue block. Output reports go out on a serial queue of their own, so a Bluetooth write never holds up the run loop. A report whose sensor block is all zero is the sensor off, never a reading (a real gyro is never 0 on all three axes three times running, even in free fall): it gives no samples, and a watchdog repeats the handshake for a Joy-Con with no sensor data since its last one, whether its reports stopped or come zeroed (a wake resets the mode), after 1 s and then twice as long each time up to 8 s. A report shorter than 0x30's 49 bytes is refused before any of it is read.
+- **Pads and players:** a Joy-Con that connects joins a pad that holds the other side alone, else it is a pad of its own (`jc1_pair_pick`); holding SL + SR on one of a pair for 3 s makes it a pad of its own, holding L on a left one alone and R on a right one alone together for 3 s pairs them (`jc1_gesture`, the Switch 2 transport's gestures). A pad is a player from its first button press, as a raw HID pad, and its lights show the player (all four flashing while it waits). Each Joy-Con is a device of its own to the npad layer (`dm_joy_attach`, `dm_joy_set` with its rail buttons, `dm_motion_set_joy`), as the Switch 2 transport's Joy-Con 2s, so the game decides whether a pair is one dual npad or two single ones (`hle_hid.c`), not macOS's combine setting. For a game that supports no Joy-Con style the pad is one whole controller (`jc1_pad_input`): a pair as a Pro Controller, a Joy-Con alone held sideways, its face buttons, stick and merged six-axis stream turned a quarter (`jc1_sideways_*`: the left one counterclockwise, the right one clockwise, the rail away from the player). The merged stream (`dm_motion_set_hid`) comes from the right Joy-Con of a pair (`DM_JC1_SIDE=L` for the left), the left one when the right one is not streaming. `DM_PAD_MERGE` makes every pad player 1 and holds no Joy-Cons apart. `DM_JC1=0` gives them back to GameController (buttons, no motion).
+- **Report:** 49 bytes, of which three 12-byte samples sit at offset 13 (acceleration XYZ then gyro XYZ, int16 little endian, 5 ms apart, the third the newest). The chip's axes: +X runs along the controller toward its top, +Y is the short axis, and +Z leaves the face on the left Joy-Con but the back on the right one, so the map into the Switch frame is (-Y, +X, +Z) for the left and (+Y, +X, -Z) for the right, negated, because the host wants the gravity direction and not the accelerometer's specific force; the gyro takes the same rotation, in rad/s.
+- **Gyro scale:** the calibration's coefficient is stored in either of two schemes (816 or 936 over coeff - origin), and the units in the wild use both: one of a pair here had 0x3BE7 (15335) where the other had the default 0x343B (13371), so with one constant the two integrate at rates 13% apart and the orientation drifts during a round. Each axis takes whichever constant puts it nearest the part's measured sensitivity (0.061 deg/s per count), which keeps a real per-unit calibration and drops the scheme. The gyro's origin is the still offset.
+- **Still offset (the cause of Boxing's phantom punches):** the factory's gyro origin is close but not the unit's own: the left one here read 5/25/2 counts against a rest offset of tens of counts, and what is left over (a degree or two per second) integrates into a drift the game reads as the player's hands moving. The host learns the controller's own offset from a second of samples in which the gyro and the accelerometer hardly vary, from fresh reports with sensor data only, and takes the two together (the factory origin is kept from the calibration reply whatever the logging; a rest learned before the reply, against origin 0, starts again, and the same calibration read again after a wake keeps it), as the Switch 2 transport does for its Joy-Con 2 (`s2_rest_update`, `pad_jc1.h`'s `jc1_rest_update`): measured, the left's residual was 11/-3/-7 counts and the right's 4/2/4. On a 22 minute session the learned offsets settled at -1/-2/46 counts on the left and 9/-8/-15 on the right, the left's residual at rest was 0.06 deg/s, and Boxing, which threw punches with the player still on both builds before this one, was clean.
+- **Sample times:** the newest sample is the earlier of the clock and the arrival, the clock advances 15 ms a report and slews a quarter toward the arrival, and the samples within a report are 5 ms apart; a sample at or before the last one given is skipped, so an early report brings fewer samples rather than out-of-order ones.
+- **Rumble:** the game's vibration for a Joy-Con pad's player goes to the Joy-Cons (`dm_jc1_rumble`, set as the player's haptics sink), not to GameController. Every 15 ms while it buzzes, each Joy-Con gets an output report 0x10 with one HD rumble sample (`jc1_rumble_encode`, dekuNukem's rumble data table as Linux hid-nintendo and SDL encode it: a high band of 81.7..1252 Hz and a low band of 40.9..626 Hz, each frequency and amplitude on its log scale; nothing at the default frequencies is the neutral 00 01 40 40): its own side's motor in a pair, the stronger of the two in each band when it is alone. A report carries one sample where a game sets values every 5 ms, so each report plays the strongest value set since the last one (`jc1_rumble_hold`), and a buzz ends with three reports of silence. The tick runs only while there is a Joy-Con pad.
+- **Found on:** [Nintendo Switch Sports Resort](../../dm/games/04008E001F098000/README.md) (v65536), on a Mac (macOS 27.2) with a left and a right Joy-Con: both accept the handshake, the 49-byte reports arrive at about 60 Hz with three samples 5 ms apart, the factory calibration reads, and flat on the desk both halves report the same gravity (left +0.01 -0.06 -1.04, right +0.02 -0.06 -1.01). The normalized gyro scale reads 0.0611/0.0611/0.0610 deg/s per count on the left and 0.0610/0.0609/0.0610 on the right: the same 0.061 the part's sensitivity is, where the left's own coefficient with the 816 constant would give 0.0532, 13% slow. Archery starts and shoots, and the game's own orientation gate (the buttons facing inward) passes. A real punch reached 9.9 G and 18 rad/s. On [Splatoon Raiders](../../dm/games/0400380020056000/README.md) (v65536, a Switch 2 game, SDK 21.4.4) the same pair aimed cleanly and 1 to 1: the game reads the six-axis API every frame (30,400 `GetSixAxisSensorStates` calls to frame 26,100 in a 7 minute session) and never the npad IMU API, and the stream carried the motion (444 deg/s peak from the left half, 484 from the right, 1.27 rev/s handed to player 1).
+- **Open:** reading a Joy-Con wholly from HID (buttons, sticks, rumble, lights, pairs, gestures, the sideways mapping) is checked by `t_jc1` only and has not run on hardware; until it has, buttons, sticks and rumble that worked through GameController depend on it. To check on a Mac with Joy-Cons: a pair and a lone one join at a press and light their player; sticks centred and reaching full after the SPI calibration (`DM_JC1_LOG=1` logs it); a game that asks for single Joy-Cons splits the pair; the rumble strength and feel, and whether a 15 ms report rate keeps up over Bluetooth with two pads; Home held quits; whether GameController's player lights on the Joy-Con fight the host's. The sideways frame derived here (left (-y, x, z), right (y, -x, z)) is the opposite of the Switch 2 transport's `s2_map_left_alone` / `s2_map_right_alone`; one of the two is wrong and only hardware can say which. A pair dropped out once mid-session (the right one's RSSI was -77; a battery is suspected). Whether a pair's two sensor frames should be common or per device is open: a flat pose agrees on both, and mirrored hand poses read mirrored x, which is physically expected and passed Archery. iOS is not supported (IOKit). Boxing's phantom punches were the still offset and are fixed (above); the three samples a report (200 Hz, where the Switch 2 transport sends one) are not implicated, since Splatoon Raiders aims 1 to 1 from the same stream and a round of Boxing is clean.
+- **Check:** `t_jc1` (the output report's bytes, the SPI reply in both encodings, the left and right frame signs, a short report refused unread, the repeated packet counter, a zeroed stream after a live one and the watchdog's timing, the jitter monotonicity with early and late arrivals, the calibration's origin and the rest learned on it; each Joy-Con's buttons and the charging-grip bit, the stick through its SPI calibration, the sideways stick, sensor and buttons, a pad's whole-controller state, the pairing pick, the merged-stream side, each Joy-Con's motors, the gestures, the light patterns, the rumble codes against the table and the peak hold).
