+### Touch sampling numbers across docked and handheld
+
+- **Symptom:** a game started docked takes no touch after it is switched to handheld (the in-game menu on the iPad, `DM_OE_SCRIPT=opmode=0@…` on the Mac), though `DM_TOUCH_LOG=1` logs its reads with the touch points. Started handheld, the same game takes taps.
+- **Cause:** docked reads of `GetTouchScreenState(s)` were numbered from the npad sampling counter (from 65536) and handheld reads from `hle_touch.c`'s own count (from 1). Unity's touch reader takes a state only if its sampling number is above the last one it took, and it takes the docked reads' empty states too (Aokana `main+0xd2bde0`; the last number is cleared at init, `main+0xd2b588`, and stored at `main+0xd2bdec`). After the switch every handheld state fell below the docked numbers and was dropped. Started handheld and then docked and back, the same happened.
+- **Fix:** `hle_touch.c` numbers the states of both modes from its one count: every state `touch_read` (`hle_hid.c`) returns comes from a `touch_fill_n` sample, made with `points` 0 in console mode, which gives an empty state with the next number and leaves the fingers (their Start and End) for the next handheld sample. `DM_TOUCH_LOG` prints the number the game sees. For every game.
+- **Check:** `dm/host/tests/t_touch.c` reads in console mode between handheld reads (an empty state with the next number, the Start of a finger pressed meanwhile kept), and `dm/host/tests/t_hid.c` (`t_touch_sampling`) reads docked, handheld, docked and handheld again through both `GetTouchScreenState` and `GetTouchScreenStates` (points asked for in handheld mode only; since [Touch samples on a 4 ms grid, with a history](#touch-samples-on-a-4-ms-grid-with-a-history), a read reaches `touch_fill_n` at most once per 4 ms in either mode, or on a mode change). In Aokana on the Mac (the PR author's runs, headless, a tap every 1.5 s from 20 s, with the first form of the fix, which numbered both modes from the npad counter): a tap during the opening movie stops it and the title comes up, before the fix only when started handheld; with it, also started docked and switched with `DM_OE_SCRIPT=opmode=0@12`, and started handheld, docked at 12 s and back at 24 s. Not run on the iPad.
+- **Found on:** Aokana - Four Rhythms Across the Blue (`0100990011866000`, Unity 2018.4.22f1 IL2CPP; profile in PR #1372), on the iPad: started docked, then handheld and Tap in the in-game menu, and no tap reached the game.
+
+### Touch samples on a 4 ms grid, with a history
+
+- **Symptom:** a game's own on-screen keyboard highlights the tapped key but enters no character; keys that also have a button (space, delete) work. `DM_TOUCH_LOG=1` shows a full Start / held / End sequence at the right place, but with consecutive sample numbers for reads in the same frame ([Animal Crossing: New Horizons](../../dm/games/01006F8002326000/README.md)). Or a touch drag is dropped: the object is selected and does not follow the finger, while taps work (Pokémon Friends, `010008C01E742000`, update 1.1.5, Unity 2022.3; no profile in the repository yet).
+- **Cause:** two readers, one rule of the hardware that the host broke each way. On the Switch, the touch resource writes the screen's LIFO every 4 ms (yuzu: `GestureUpdatePeriod`), every SDK read returns those samples, and reading uses up no edges. (1) `touch_read` (`hle_hid.c`) once called `touch_fill_n` at every read, and each call advances `hle_touch.c`'s finger state (the Start goes out once, then End once). Animal Crossing reads the touch screen twice per frame (its input manager at `main+0x1b65260`, then its keyboard's reader), so the first reader took the Start and the End and the keyboard only saw a held point. (2) Publishing one sample per presented frame fixed that, but `GetTouchScreenStates` then returned one state. Unity 2022.3 polls on its own thread every 16.7 ms whatever the frame rate (Pokémon Friends `main+0xb00670`, `GetTouchScreenStates<16>(buf, 16)`), takes each returned state whose sampling number is above the last it took, and when a poll takes no new state with touch points while a finger is down it ends every live touch as cancelled (`main+0x60d6a0`). At 30 fps the second poll of a frame found the same number and cancelled the drag. Giving every read a new number instead makes Unity take the same Start again on every state, so taps stop working.
+- **Fix:** a read makes a sample only when 4 ms have passed since the newest one (or the mode changed) and otherwise copies the newest, with its number and edges. The last 16 samples are kept (`g_touch_hist`); `GetTouchScreenStates<N>(s, n)` returns the newest min(n, 16) of them, newest first, each at the reader's own stride with its count clamped to N, and `GetTouchScreenState` copies the newest. An edge sits in exactly one sample. Fingers beyond a small reader's capacity go unseen there, as on hardware. Console-mode reads go through the same ring and ask for no touch points, so two within 4 ms share a number, as the console's LIFO keeps writing empty entries when docked; the ring is not cleared on a mode change. Gestures keep their own finger state. For every game.
+- **Check:** `dm/host/tests/t_hid.c` (`t_touch_sampling`): reads within 4 ms share the sample and the sampler runs once, 4 ms later is a new sample, the history is newest first and holds 16, a small reader's stride and count (a sample it makes still holds every finger), a Start found at index 1 by a later reader, mode changes within 4 ms, `n <= 0` makes no sample, and a read applies the `DM_KEYS` frame steps due in its frame first. Pokémon Friends on the Mac (a copy of a phone save, 30 fps): taps on Start Game and the room button work and a dragged object follows the finger. Mario Kart 8 Deluxe 4.0.1 on the Mac, handheld (`GetTouchScreenStates<16>`, given up to 16 states now where it had one): each tap on its menus (Single Player, Grand Prix, back) moves exactly one step. Animal Crossing was not run here: an iPhone check of the name-entry keyboard is still wanted. `DM_TOUCH_LOG` prints one line per sample, so its 500 lines are about 8 s of touch at 60 reads a second.
+- **Open:** a reader that looks only at the newest state misses an edge made more than 4 ms before its read, as on hardware; a reader polling faster than every 4 ms sees no new sample.
+- **Found on:** [Animal Crossing: New Horizons](../../dm/games/01006F8002326000/README.md) 3.0.3, the name entry in handheld mode ([issue #2567](https://github.com/seedds/nxnative/issues/2567), [PR #2603](https://github.com/seedds/nxnative/pull/2603)); Pokémon Friends 1.1.5, dragging furniture on the Mac and on an iPhone.
