+### IR camera reported unconnected
+
+- **Symptom:** undefined `irsensor` imports found from the import list ([Listing a game's unhooked imports before they are met](#listing-a-games-unhooked-imports-before-they-are-met)).
+- **Cause:** Bezel Engine's input layer makes one (an IR camera) per controller and asks its status (`main+0x497f84`).
+- **Fix:** the IR camera (`irsensor`) answers every camera unconnected, and processors cannot run.
+- **Found on:** [Big Brain Academy: Brain vs. Brain](../../dm/games/0100620012D6E000/README.md) (SDK 11.4.3, NintendoWare Bezel Engine).
+
+## Software keyboard and applets
+
+### System applets opened by `Show…` calls
+
+- **Symptom:** null read in `sdk` under `nn::applet::CreateLibraryApplet` (Engage: title menu → DLC → `nn::ec::ShowShopAddOnContentList`); `Show…` overloads found in the imports before they were met.
+- **Cause:** every `Show…` call that opens a system applet (eShop, friend lists, web pages, error screens) goes through the applet proxy that `oe::Initialize` would have set up, and the host has none.
+- **Fix:** hook each `Show…` overload the game imports: list `main`'s undefined symbols containing `Show` / `Applet` and grep the host sources for each mangled name; one overload missing is enough to crash. The shop calls return `void` and act as if the player closed the shop (`hle_misc.c`); the friends and web ones return failure (`hle_friends.c`, `hle_offline.c`). `ShowWebPage` remains cut; `web::ShowOfflineHtmlPage` now uses the [offline web view](#offline-html-pages-in-a-web-view) when its page is available. `err::ShowNetworkServiceError` and `err::ShowApplicationError` are logged. Also hooked, found in the imports before they were met: the `ShowShopAddOnContentList(UserHandle)` overload, and the `ec::ShowShop…` overloads without an `ApplicationId` (they show the calling application's own pages). `ec::ShowShopSubscriptionDetails` is under [Shop service accessors](#shop-service-accessors).
+- **Found on:** [Fire Emblem Engage](../../dm/games/0100A6301214E000/README.md) (the shop calls); [Octopath Traveler 0](../../dm/games/01005270232F2000/README.md) (`web::ShowOfflineHtmlPage`); [Pokémon Sword](../../dm/games/0100ABF008968000/README.md) (SDK 7, `err::ShowNetworkServiceError`); [Pokémon Legends: Z-A](../../dm/games/0100F43008C44000/README.md) (19.3.11: `ShowShopAddOnContentList(UserHandle)`, `err::ShowApplicationError`); [The Great Ace Attorney Chronicles](../../dm/games/010036E00FB20000/README.md) (SDK 10.4.1: the overloads without an `ApplicationId`); [Fire Emblem Fortune's Weave](../../dm/games/04007EF01F8AE000/README.md) (SDK 22.2.0: `hid::ShowControllerStrapGuide(const StrapGuideArg&)`, met at the title about 100 s in).
+
+### Library applets take the application's focus
+
+- **Symptom:** entering 2P stops the game on its 2P screen: the same frame repeats, the music plays, no controller does anything, until the window loses and regains focus (Mario Kart 8 Deluxe, every launch). With one controller, once that was fixed: the 2P character select ignores input while the log shows the controller applet opened again every few frames.
+- **Cause:** on Horizon a library applet takes the application's focus. am sends message 15 with OutOfFocus when it opens and 15 with InFocus when it closes, and the `Show…` call returns once it has closed. The host's stand-ins answered at once with no focus change. Mario Kart applies the controller applet's answer only after that InFocus notification: its notification thread (`main+0x8ea560`, blocking `PopNotificationMessage`) sets a flag on 15 with state 1, and the per-frame `main+0x87b5d0` takes the flag before it applies the answer (`main+0x87b300`). Second: with fewer controllers than the game's minimum, the host answered success with the connected count. A Switch never gives that answer (its applet waits for controllers until the player closes it), and the game, which asks for two in 2P, opened the applet again.
+- **Fix:** `hle_oe.c` counts open applets apart from the front end's focus. The game sees the front end's state, or 2 while an applet is open over a game in focus; each change of what it sees is one 15 (under focus modes 1 and 2, [Focus handling mode decides whether focus changes are notified](#focus-handling-mode-decides-whether-focus-changes-are-notified)). `DM_APPLET("name");` (`hle.h`) at the top of a stand-in holds the game out of focus until the stand-in returns: the controller applet (both overloads), strap guide (both overloads: SDK 22 adds one with a `StrapGuideArg`) and firmware update, `swkbd::ShowKeyboard`, the Mii editor, the user selector and creator, the error applets and the shop pages. Stand-ins that report the applet could not start (friends, web, the license notice, the album, amiibo) open none. The inline keyboard is left as it was: its applet draws into the game's own picture and is not believed to take focus (not checked). An instant open and close merges into one pending 15 that reads 1, which is all Mario Kart needs; a game that must see the 2 would need more. `ShowControllerSupport` with fewer players than the minimum (`ControllerSupportArg` +0) returns `ResultControllerSupportCanceled` (2202-3101), what the SDK's `ShowControllerSupportCore` (SDK 4.4.0 `sdk+0x24eeb0`) returns when the player closes the applet; Mario Kart goes back to its engine class menu, as on a Switch. `DM_OE_SCRIPT` now also starts for a game that only uses the blocking `PopNotificationMessage`.
+- **Found on:** [Mario Kart 8 Deluxe](../../dm/games/0100152000022000/README.md) (4.0.0 and 4.0.1; GitHub issue #550).
+- **Check:** `t_oe` and `t_hid` (`dm/host/tests`). On the Mac, from a save past the Mii pick: L+R at the title, Down, A (2P), A (Grand Prix), A (50cc). The log shows `oe library applet: controller support`, then `oe focus state → 2 (library applet open)` and `→ 1 (library applet closed)`, and the character select takes input. With one controller and no window (or under `DM_CTL`) the applet logs `too few, closed as the player would (canceled)` once and the engine class menu comes back; with a window its prompt waits for a second player ([The controller applet waits for players](#the-controller-applet-waits-for-players)).
+
+### The controller applet waits for players
+
+- **Symptom:** with [Press a button to join](../settings.md#press-a-button-to-join) on, a game's player screen closes when the game asks for more players than have pressed a button. The log shows `hid controller applet: 2 to 2 players wanted, 1 connected: too few, closed as the player would (canceled)` (Mario Kart World 1.8.0, pull request #1596).
+- **Cause:** the controller applet's stand-in (`ctrl_support`, `hle_hid.c`) answered at once, and below the game's minimum (`ControllerSupportArg` +0) it answered as the player closing the applet. A Switch's applet stays open until enough controllers are there or the player closes it. Before Press a button to join the host had nothing to wait for; with it, connected controllers that are no player yet are what the applet waits for.
+- **Fix:** with a window and no scripted input, `ctrl_support` asks the front end (`front_ctrl_support`, `front.h`). Its prompt is up over the game, holding the game's thread, while controllers join: a first button press with Press a button to join, a connection without it. The rules are in `ctrl_prompt.h`: B or Escape closes it (canceled); with at least the minimum in, it is done when a player presses A or Return, when the game's maximum has joined, or when no connected controller is left to join; the press that makes a controller join counts for neither; no time limit and no close on a focus loss, as on a Switch. The game gets no controller or keyboard input while it is up, nor until the A or B that closed it is let go. The host counts the players again once it closes (`dm_players_reported`) and answers canceled below the minimum. The Mac draws it on the Home prompt's card (`front_mac.m`, `home_quit_mac.h` `hq_card_view`), iOS as a card of its own with Done and Cancel buttons for touch (`DmGamepad.m`). A front end passes the texts in the player's language as `DM_CTRL_PROMPT_TEXT` ([Starting a game](../frontends.md#starting-a-game)). Without a window, or with `DM_KEYS` or `DM_CTL` (nobody at the controllers), the host answers at once as before.
+- **Found on:** [Mario Kart World](../../dm/games/0400C3F00006E000/README.md) (1.8.0, pull request #1596; GitHub issue #1640).
+- **Check:** `t_ctrl_prompt` and `t_hid` (`dm/host/tests`). On the Mac with a window, two controllers and Press a button to join on, Mario Kart 8 Deluxe from a save past the Mii pick: press a button on one controller, then L+R at the title, Down, A (2P), A (Grand Prix), A (50cc). The log shows `front end: controller prompt open: 2 to 2 players wanted, 1 in, 1 controllers waiting to join`; a press on the second controller closes it (`controller prompt closed: 2 players (done)`, then `the prompt waited, 2 now (OK)`) and the character select has two players. B instead closes it as canceled and the engine class menu comes back.
+
+### The controller applet leaves out the players past the maximum
+
+- **Symptom:** with more controllers than a game takes, the game gets no input and the log repeats `oe library applet: controller support`, `hid controller applet: 1 to 1 players wanted, 2 connected` and `focus state → 1 (library applet closed)` every few frames (1,671 times in one run). A game that suspends its music while an applet has focus plays none.
+- **Cause:** above the game's maximum (`ControllerSupportArg` +1) the stand-in (`ctrl_support`, `hle_hid.c`) answered with the count capped and left every npad connected. On a Switch the applet leaves no more controllers connected than the game takes, so the game saw the second npad again on its next frame and opened the applet again. Any second player caused it: a second controller, a receiver read as a pad ([A keyboard receiver that declares a gamepad takes player 1](#a-keyboard-receiver-that-declares-a-gamepad-takes-player-1)), or one controller listed twice ([One Pro Controller read as two players](#one-pro-controller-read-as-two-players)).
+- **Fix:** the players past the maximum, the highest first, are left out (`g_benched`): their npads read as not connected and their style events are signalled, as for a player on an npad the game does not support. With a maximum of one their controllers feed player 1 (`merged_players`), so whichever controller the player holds plays, and a device that never sends input does no harm. With a higher maximum they are not used. They count again when the applet opens for more players (the lowest first), when the game sets its supported npads again (`SetSupportedNpadIdType`), or when their controller goes away; a controller that connects later is a player until the applet opens again. The log names each one: `hid: player 2's controller feeds player 1: the controller applet left it out`. No prompt asks which controllers stay, as a Switch's applet does: with a maximum above one the lowest players stay.
+- **Open:** not run on a game (no second controller here): the tests cover the host's side, and that Ocarina of Time stops asking once No2 reads as not connected follows from its request for one player, not from a run. A game that opens the applet for one player and later expects a second controller without opening it again would not see that controller until it reconnects; none is known. The second HID entry behind the report no longer counts as a pad: [A HID entry that sends nothing counted as a second pad](#a-hid-entry-that-sends-nothing-counted-as-a-second-pad).
+- **Found on:** [The Legend of Zelda: Ocarina of Time](../../dm/games/0400486024DEC000/README.md) (v131072, one Pro Controller over Bluetooth on the Mac, read as players 1 and 2 after it reconnected; GitHub issue #2237).
+- **Check:** `t_hid` (`dm/host/tests`): one wanted with two and three players, the lowest players kept with a maximum of two, the applet opened for more, a controller that goes and comes back, the supported npads set again.
+
+### The software keyboard blocks only its caller
+
+- **Symptom:** the sound stutters and the input box lags while the player types a name (Octopath Traveler 0 on an iPhone: one 4.7 s frame, 5,035 wakeups a second instead of about 1,800, and no audio underruns).
+- **Cause:** the host froze the guest clock while `swkbd::ShowKeyboard` was up (d2r-mobile's choice, so that monsters did not attack while the player typed). Unreal calls it off its game thread, so the game kept running, and every thread that waits on the clock spun while its audio mixing starved. On the Switch the applet blocks only the caller while the clock runs.
+- **Fix:** the host now does the same (`hle_swkbd.c`). The iOS input box froze the clock a second time around its own wait (`front_ios.m`), so the first fix changed nothing on the phone (4,977 ms and 2,577 ms frames, 5,181 wakeups a second): look for every caller of `guest_clock_freeze_for`, the front ends included. The inline keyboard keeps the freeze: its game (Monster Hunter Rise) does not block on hardware at all, and the host's input box blocks it.
+- **Found on:** [Octopath Traveler 0](../../dm/games/01005270232F2000/README.md).
+- **Check:** on the Mac `DM_KBD=<text> DM_TEST_SWKBD_HOLD_MS=8000` holds the keyboard up: frozen, 8.8 fps and 7,936 wakeups a second in that window; running, 30 fps and 1,788.
+
+### The inline keyboard as a resident library applet
+
+- **Symptom:** null read in `applet::CreateLibraryApplet` under `swkbd::InlineKeyboard::Initialize` (3D World, entering the save select after the character select).
+- **Cause:** the inline keyboard is a resident library applet whose results arrive through `Calc`.
+- **Fix:** `hle_swkbd.c` takes over every entry that touches the applet: `Appear` arms a request, and the next `Calc` asks for the text the way `ShowKeyboard` does (`DM_KBD`, else the front end's input box) and hands it to the game's changed-string and enter callbacks, or calls its cancel callback; `GetImage` reports no picture. `GetImageMemoryRequirement` is not a pure computation: it asks vi, hence the `vi::GetIndirectImageMemoryRequirement` hook ([vi layer and display calls without vi service objects](#vi-layer-and-display-calls-without-vi-service-objects)).
+- **Found on:** [Super Mario 3D World + Bowser's Fury](../../dm/games/010028600EBDA000/README.md).
+
+### Inline keyboard window size, setters and callbacks
+
+- **Symptom:** texture creation returns null right after the inline keyboard's `Initialize`, then a null read at `main+0x7713044`.
+- **Cause:** the game makes three textures of `swkbd::InlineKeyboard::GetWindowSize`, and the host answered 0 × 0 (its creation function returns null for a size of 0, `main+0x77112b8`).
+- **Fix:** it is 1280 × 720 as on hardware now. SDK 11 adds keyboard setters (window mode, key top alpha / scale / position, touch, hardware keyboard, user words, dictionaries; ignored) and two callbacks the applet makes from `Calc`: finished-initialize after `Initialize` and released-user-words after `UnsetUserWordInfo`. The host makes them on the next `Calc`.
+- **Found on:** [Monster Hunter Rise](../../dm/games/0100B04011742000/README.md).
+
+### Inline keyboard text callbacks and `Calc` states
+
+- **Symptom:** the hunter's name box stays empty, then stays open with the name in it (Monster Hunter Rise, character creation).
+- **Cause:** the inline keyboard used to count every `Appear` as a cancel. The game also finishes the entry only when `Calc` returns 1 (ready / hidden) after a non-zero value (`main+0x6fb3e6c`); the hook returned nothing.
+- **Fix:** the text arrives through the enter callback, whose argument is the text inline (`char16_t[502]`, the length at +0x3EC; the changed-string argument adds three cursor positions at +0x3F0..+0x3F8: `ikb_str_arg`, read off the game's callbacks at `main+0x6fb3910` / `main+0x6fb3afc`). `Calc` now returns 0 until initialization finishes, 3 while shown and 1 otherwise. `SetFinishedKeyboardCallback` is called on the `Calc` after `Disappear`. AppearArg's `textMaxLength` (+0x20) limits the text.
+- **Found on:** [Monster Hunter Rise](../../dm/games/0100B04011742000/README.md).
+
+### The Mac input box keeps the game's focus
+
+- **Symptom:** the name typed in the Mac input box is dropped and the game asks again; the log has `oe focus state → 2` and `→ 1` between `inline keyboard Appear` and `→ OK` (Monster Hunter Rise, character creation).
+- **Cause:** the box (`front_text_input`, an `NSAlert`) takes the key window, and the game window resigning key was sent to the game as out of focus. On hardware the inline keyboard is drawn inside the game, which keeps its focus.
+- **Fix:** `front_mac.m` does not report the game window resigning key while the box is up (`g_text_box`). `ShowKeyboard`'s applet still sends its own out-of-focus / in-focus pair (`DM_APPLET`).
+- **Found on:** [Monster Hunter Rise](../../dm/games/0100B04011742000/README.md).
+
+### `nn::ngc::ProfanityFilter` vtable and by-name overloads
+
+- **Symptom:** `CRASH sig=10` at a heap address, called from `main+0x6bca160` right after the name was entered (Monster Hunter Rise); crash in `lmem::AllocateFromExpHeap` under `ngc::ProfanityFilter::MaskProfanityWordsInText` right after the character name is entered (DARK SOULS: REMASTERED); filter methods among the undefined imports with no hook, found from the import list before they were met (The Great Ace Attorney Chronicles).
+- **Cause:** `nn::ngc::ProfanityFilter` is polymorphic and Monster Hunter Rise masks the name through its vtable (slot 8, `MaskProfanityWordsInText(int *, char16_t *, uint32_t)`). The constructor hook did nothing, so the object kept what the heap held. Other games call the methods by name: DARK SOULS: REMASTERED calls the UTF-16 overload (`EPiPtj`) by name, and the host had taken it over only in the filter's vtable, so the SDK's version ran on a filter whose word list the host never loaded; The Great Ace Attorney Chronicles calls `ngc::ProfanityFilter::MaskProfanityWordsInText(int *, char *, uint32_t)`, `SetMaskMode` and `Finalize` by name rather than through the vtable.
+- **Fix:** the filter now gets a host vtable (`ngc_vtable` in `hle_misc.c`): each slot of the SDK's `_ZTVN2nn3ngc15ProfanityFilterE` is matched by address with the class's methods, and slots the loader already bound to forced take-overs keep them. The `unsigned short` and `char16_t` overloads are taken over by name too, as are the `char` overload, `SetMaskMode` and `Finalize` (`hle_misc.c`). Nothing is masked or reported.
+- **Found on:** [Monster Hunter Rise](../../dm/games/0100B04011742000/README.md) (the vtable); [DARK SOULS: REMASTERED](../../dm/games/01004AB00A260000/README.md) (SDK 4.5.0; one boot: the UTF-16 overload by name); [The Great Ace Attorney Chronicles](../../dm/games/010036E00FB20000/README.md) (SDK 10.4.1: the `char` overload, `SetMaskMode`, `Finalize`).
+
+### `nn::ngc` China-region profanity check and mask
+
+- **Symptom:** `[RED] sf: the game connects to service ngct:u itself, which the host does not serve`, then `Break#1 reason=0 Result=0xe15` from the SDK when the player name is entered after a race (Cruis'n Blast).
+- **Cause:** `nn::ngc::CheckProfanityWordsInTextForPlatformRegionChina` and `MaskProfanityWordsInTextForPlatformRegionChina` are free functions, not `ProfanityFilter` methods. They go through `nn::ngc::t::Match`, which opens the `ngct:u` service, and the host does not serve it.
+- **Fix:** both are taken over by name in `hle_offline.c`, next to the newer `ProfanityFilter` overloads: the check reports no profanity, and the mask leaves the text unchanged.
+- **Found on:** Cruis'n Blast (0100B41013C82000, SDK 10, version 1.0.7; no profile in this repository).
+
+## Display, window and operation mode
+
+### `vi::CreateLayer` with the layer's size
+
+- **Symptom:** SIGSEGV in `vi::CreateLayer(Layer**, Display*, int, int)` (`sdk+0x3f9bc4`, from SDL3's Switch video driver at `main+0x239304`).
+- **Cause:** the overload with the layer's size; the host had only the two-argument one.
+- **Fix:** it returns the static layer (`hle_vi.c`).
+- **Found on:** [Absolum](../../dm/games/0100A83022A22000/README.md) (SDK 18.3.2, FNA on .NET NativeAOT with SDL3; one boot each).
+
+### `vi::CreateLayer` with `LayerCreationSettings` (SDK 22)
+
+- **Symptom:** SIGSEGV at `sdk+0x44ea94` reading address 0, in `vi::CreateLayer`, right after `am::GetSelfController()`; the caller is the game's window setup (`main+0x978cb6c`).
+- **Cause:** SDK 22 adds `CreateLayer(Layer**, Display*, const LayerCreationSettings*)`; the game builds the settings with the SDK's `LayerCreationSettings(int w, int h)` (`{w, h, 2}`, `sdk+0x4526a0`), and the SDK's `CreateLayer` asks the applet's self controller, which the host never made.
+- **Fix:** the host takes it over like the sized overload (`hle_vi.c`): the static layer, and the size recorded for an EGL surface.
+- **Found on:** [EA SPORTS FC 27](../../dm/games/0100C49025D3E000/README.md) (SDK 22.2.0): 1920x1080.
+
+### vi layer and display calls without vi service objects
+
+- **Symptom:** SIGSEGV in `vi::SetLayerCrop` or `vi::OpenDisplay` when opening a display by name.
+- **Cause:** the SDK's version dereferences vi service objects the host never made.
+- **Fix:** `SetLayerCrop` only records the crop for the present ([The NVN present crops by the vi layer crop](rendering.md#the-nvn-present-crops-by-the-vi-layer-crop)); `DestroyLayer`, `CloseDisplay` and `vi::Finalize` are host no-ops. Also hooked: `vi::GetIndirectImageMemoryRequirement` (asked by the inline keyboard's `GetImageMemoryRequirement`, [The inline keyboard as a resident library applet](#the-inline-keyboard-as-a-resident-library-applet)); `vi::ListDisplays` (one display, "Default"; the game lists 10 into a stack buffer and does not read them, `main+0x808f08`), found from the import list before it was met. `vi::OpenDisplay("Default")` returns the same static display as `OpenDefaultDisplay`, so applications can open the name returned by `ListDisplays` without entering the SDK's absent service client.
+- **Found on:** [The Legend of Zelda: Link's Awakening](../../dm/games/01006BB00C6F0000/README.md) (SDK 8.2.0); [Super Mario 3D World + Bowser's Fury](../../dm/games/010028600EBDA000/README.md) (SDK 10: `vi::GetIndirectImageMemoryRequirement`); [The Great Ace Attorney Chronicles](../../dm/games/010036E00FB20000/README.md) (SDK 10.4.1: `vi::ListDisplays`); Alien: Isolation (SDK 8.2.0: `vi::OpenDisplay`).
+
+### `vi::InitializeWithVrrLfcEnabled`
+
+- **Symptom:** SIGSEGV reading address 0 in `SdkMutexType::Lock` (`sdk+0x171ef8`) right after graphics initialize, under `nn::vi2::InitializeWithVrrLfcEnabled`.
+- **Cause:** the Switch 2 SDKs' display initialize with low frame rate compensation for a variable refresh rate display. It does `nn::vi::Initialize`'s work through `vi2`, whose VRR hooks lock the mutex at the start of `nn::gsv::GetUserSession()`'s session, which the host never opens.
+- **Fix:** the host takes it over like `nn::vi::Initialize` (`hle_vi.c`): it has no vi service and no VRR display (`QueryDisplayVrrSupported` says so).
+- **Found on:** [Resident Evil 4](../../dm/games/0400569027BF4000/README.md) (v131072, SDK 21.4.3: `main+0xb180288` → `sdk+0x5d8cf0`).
+
+### Layer HDR metadata
+
+- **Symptom:** `CRASH sig=11 addr=0 pc=0` with the link register just after a call to a `vi::SetLayerNextHdr…` or `vi::ClearLayerNextHdr…` function.
+- **Cause:** the SDK forwards a layer's HDR metadata through a table its graphics system fills, which is null on the host.
+- **Fix:** every layer HDR metadata call of SDK 22's free functions succeeds with nothing to apply (`dm/gfx/hle_vi.c`): the content gamut, the HDR to SDR tone mapping, the standard and extended content luminance ranges, the adjustable Bezier tone curve, and the clears of the gamut, the tone mapping and the curve. The host presents SDR.
+- **Found on:** [Donkey Kong Bananza](../../dm/games/040074A01BF12000/README.md) (the set calls); the curve and the clears: [Mario Kart World](../../dm/games/0400C3F00006E000/README.md) 1.8.0 sets the curve every frame, from pull request #1116 (that build is not run here).
+
+### Layer frame numbers
+
+- **Symptom:** SIGSEGV in `android::Surface::getQueuedFrameNumber` under `nn::vi::GetLatestFrameNumber`.
+- **Cause:** Unreal paces frames by the layer's frame number (`main+0x10140e8`); the SDK reads the vi service's surface, which the host never made.
+- **Fix:** `hle_vi.c` answers with the frames presented so far; `ListFrameHistory` lists them, as the next entry says.
+- **Found on:** [Octopath Traveler 0](../../dm/games/01005270232F2000/README.md).
+
+### Layer frame history
+
+- **Symptom:** right after the opening logo movie nothing is presented again, with no red line; the watchdog reports `no progress … stage present frame`. The game thread is in Unreal's `FlushRenderingCommands`, waking each millisecond from a condition variable whose event is never triggered, while the render, RHI, frame-flip and interrupt threads all wait idle.
+- **Cause:** Unreal's Switch RHI learns which frames reached the screen only from `nn::vi::ListFrameHistory`: its "Viewport Thread" waits for each vsync, asks for the entry count (a null buffer: the SDK answers 8, the history's size), reads the 40-byte `FrameInfo` entries and takes the first one shown as the latest flip. Those flips complete the frame-end fences that wait for the swap chain (`r.GTSyncType` 2, `FFrameEndSync`), and the first full `FlushRenderingCommands` waits for every fence in that ring. The host returned no entries, so no flip ever came and the flush waited for ever.
+- **Fix:** `dm/gfx/hle_vi.c` keeps the last 8 presents (recorded at each NVN present, numbered as `GetLatestFrameNumber`, with the present queue's ticket) and answers as the SDK fills `FrameInfo` (`sdk+0x4510a0` in SDK 20.5.15): status (2 shown with a present time, 1 queued and not shown yet), frame number, queue tick and present tick twice, newest first. A frame is shown once the present queue has handed it on (`mtl_present_progress`), dropped by the Mac or not (next entry). Its present time is the drawable's presented time when Metal reports one, else (headless, before the handler runs, or dropped) the time it was first seen handed on.
+- **Found on:** [Minecraft Dungeons II](../../dm/games/0100A7C01B792000/README.md); [Octopath Traveler 0](../../dm/games/01005270232F2000/README.md) and [OCTOPATH TRAVELER II](../../dm/games/0100A3501946E000/README.md) call it from the same RHI code (`main+0x1014994`, `main+0x32b408c`) and got no entries before. [Luigi's Mansion 3](../../dm/games/0100DCA0064A6000/README.md) paces its presents by the history and the window's interval ([A game's own present pacing reads the interval the window presents at](frame-rate.md#a-games-own-present-pacing-reads-the-interval-the-window-presents-at)).
+
+### A frame the Mac dropped stays queued in the frame history
+
+- **Symptom:** the picture stops at a load (Orbitals: right after both players ready up at the character select) with no red line; minutes later `game called nn::diag abort: Result 0, call site main+0x10a0` under Unreal's low-level fatal error, whose format is `GameThread timed out waiting for %s after %.02f secs` (`RenderingThread.cpp`). Before the abort the watchdog reports `no progress … stage present frame`; the game thread wakes each millisecond in `FFrameEndSync`, and the render, RHI, frame-flip and interrupt threads wait idle, as in [Layer frame history](#layer-frame-history).
+- **Cause:** Metal reported the last two drawables as never put on screen (`presentedTime` 0, recorded as dropped), and `ListFrameHistory` listed a dropped frame as queued (status 1) for ever. A Switch's compositor shows every frame it is handed, in order, so the game can wait for a frame's flip before it presents another: Unreal's Viewport Thread (`main+0x1540460`) took the older shown frame as its latest flip, and the frame-end sync waited for the newer one.
+- **Fix:** `dm/gfx/hle_vi.c` counts a frame the present queue handed on as shown whether or not the Mac displayed it, with the time it was first seen handed on when Metal gives no presented time. `dm/host/tests/t_vi_history.c` covers it.
+- **Found on:** [Orbitals](../../dm/games/040032B023CA4000/README.md) (Unreal Engine 5.6.1, Switch 2), in a window on the Mac; which Mac condition dropped the drawables (the window's occlusion, the display link) is not known.
