+### `account::GetUserId` copies the Uid of any handle
+
+- **Symptom:** a second controller ends the game with `FATAL account::GetUserId: the handle was not issued by the host (0x2)`, at boot with both connected or when the second joins during play (EA SPORTS FC 27 1.8d.6523; the FC 26 patch reports `(0x8)`).
+- **Cause:** the host checked the handle's third word against the marker its own open calls write. The SDK's accessor never reads that word: FC 27's `nn::account::GetUserId` (`sdk+0x1c0040`) calls `detail::GetUserId` (`sdk+0x1bede0`, `ldp x8, x1, [x0]; mov x0, x8; ret`), stores the 16 bytes and returns 0. For a second controller's player FC 27 passes a handle it built and never filled (`main+0x1f638b0`; words `0x7012222b20 0x400000001 0x2`), so on a console the call succeeds and returns those bytes.
+- **Fix:** `GetUserId` copies the Uid and returns 0 for any handle, as the SDK does (`hle_account.c`); the open calls issue session tokens for closing their own sessions, but the accessor still does not inspect that word. `t_account` covers the host's handle and built handles with third words 2, 8, 0 and all ones.
+- **Found on:** [EA SPORTS FC 27](../../dm/games/0100C49025D3E000/README.md) (PR #1129; two DualSense controllers on a Mac and an iPad Air M4, reported in its review thread); first found on EA SPORTS FC 26 by the [FC 26 120 fps patch](https://github.com/imnotsharingmyname/nxnative-fc26-120fps) (MIT).
+
+### Local account selection, sessions and separate saves
+
+- **Symptom:** Minecraft Dungeons II's second local player repeatedly receives player 1's user, cannot join, and opens the user-selector/error applets on every attempt (PR #2846).
+- **Cause:** the host always returned its one fixed UID. The game leaves `invalidUidList` empty and expects the person at the selector to choose another account. The PR's carried implementation demonstrated about 40 seconds of two-player play, but guessed selection intent from controller counts, listed closed users as open, and forgot registered users at the next boot.
+- **Fix:** `hle_account.c` presents the eligible users and an explicit Add user action through `front_user_select` (native Mac/iOS dialogs). It checks exclusions for existing and new identities, supports cancellation and the optional Skip action, and only bypasses the UI for the single registered user when the game's `isSkipEnabled` permits it, with neither exclusions nor `additionalSelect`. It never reads a controller count. Network-required selections fail when unqualified users are forbidden; the host has no linked Nintendo Accounts. The settings layout and the distinction between skipping the UI and showing a Skip button follow [libnx's selector](https://github.com/switchbrew/libnx/blob/master/nx/source/applets/psel.c).
+- **Identity and sessions:** user 1 retains its original UID; users 2–8 use PR #2846's deterministic slots and `Player N` nicknames. `hle_account_users.c` atomically records registration in `<DM_SAVE>.users` before returning a new UID. An unreadable/corrupt registry fails closed without overwriting it. Selection does not open a user: each open owns a unique session token, `ListOpenUsers` lists only users with live sessions, `ListAllUsers` includes closed profiles, and `GetLastOpenedUser` follows opens. Closing a copied/stale token cannot close another session. A fresh process has registered profiles but no live sessions; the launch user remains user 1.
+- **Saves:** `hle_fs.c` retains `<DM_SAVE>` for user 1 and uses `<DM_SAVE>_userN` for each additional user, including read-only mounts. Unknown UIDs fail instead of falling back to user 1. Reusing an occupied mount name for a different backing directory fails. The existing transaction engine handles each directory independently; no transaction rewrite. Additional users and the registry are local to the app's save store and outside the current primary-account/device cloud-sync format. See the [front-end contract](../frontends.md#local-users-and-the-account-selector) for backup/move requirements.
+- **Checked:** `t_account` covers choices, exclusions (including uncreated UIDs), skip/cancel, registration failure, two sessions for one UID, stale closes, concurrent opens/closes, and fresh-process registry loading. `t_account_save` chooses and opens two users, mounts distinct saves with identical filenames, commits independently (including an alias and read-only views), injects a failure in each direction, and starts two fresh processes to verify registered identities and committed bytes survive while pending writes do not. Native Mac picker selection and Mac/iOS source builds were checked; actual Minecraft co-op/progress across boots and iOS device interaction remain untested on this implementation.
+- **Found on:** [Minecraft Dungeons II](../../dm/games/0100A7C01B792000/README.md), reported by papertradooor in PR #2846; save/session contracts reproduced with synthetic inputs.
+
+### An account AsyncContext needs its SDK constructor
+
+- **Symptom:** `CRASH sig=11` at `main+0x5b64f8`, `lr=main+0x3f1b8cc` (`nn.account.AsyncContext$$Finalize`), on an IL2CPP thread-pool worker a few seconds after the player confirms a name and birthday (Pokémon Friends 1.1.5). The object's first word, its vtable, holds stray bytes (`0xffffd256` in one run, `0x15600000128` in another). The game had started `account::EnsureNetworkServiceAccountIdTokenCacheAsync`.
+- **Cause:** `nn::account::AsyncContext` is polymorphic. The SDK's constructor (SDK 19.3.11: `sdk+0x1ad6c8`) writes the class's vtable and nulls the three words after it, the IPC object pointer at +0x18 among them, and sends nothing. The host took the constructor over with one that wrote no memory, so the vtable word kept what the allocator left there. The game's C# binding deletes the native context from the managed finalizer through the virtual deleting destructor, inlined in main (`main+0x5b64f0`: `if (p) p->vtable[1](p)`), which the host's `AsyncContextD1Ev` takeover never sees, and jumped through those bytes.
+- **Fix:** the constructor is no longer taken over (`hle_account.c`): the SDK's own runs. No host interface sets the IPC pointer, so the SDK's destructor, reached through the vtable, finds no session to close and frees the object. `friends::AsyncContext` and `account::NintendoAccountAuthorizationRequestContext` have no vtable (their SDK constructors write none), so their takeovers stay.
+- **Found on:** Pokémon Friends (`010008C01E742000`, update 1.1.5, SDK 19.3.11; no profile in the repository yet). The managed names come from Il2CppDumper's table ([IL2CPP method lookup](../development.md#il2cpp-method-lookup)).
+
+### Friends presence is accepted offline
+
+- **Symptom:** the loading screen stays at 22% for ever while the music plays (Twelve Minutes). No red line, no crash. `DM_FS=1` shows `level0` and the FMOD master bank read, then no other scene and no save mount; `DM_HLECOUNT` shows `account::TryOpenPreselectedUser`, `GetUserId`, `GetNickname` and `friends::UserPresence::Initialize` once each, and `fs::MountSaveData` never.
+- **Cause:** the host's `UserPresence::Initialize` returned `RES_FAIL` like the other friends calls. The game's start-up takes the failure as fatal and stops before it mounts its save, so the loading screen waits for a scene that is never asked for. A presence is the game's own object until the friends service publishes it, so failing to set one up is not what an offline console reports.
+- **Fix:** `UserPresence::Initialize`, `SetDescription` and `Commit` succeed and publish nothing (`hle_friends.c`). The calls that need the service (friend lists, notification queue, online play sessions, invitations) still fail.
+- **Check:** `make -C dm/host/tests GAME=<id> run` runs `t_friends`.
+- **Found on:** Twelve Minutes (`0100CD701434C000`, Unity 2019.4.31f1 IL2CPP, SDK 12.3.7; seedds/nxnative#217). Its profile and game note are pending in [PR #2904](https://github.com/seedds/nxnative/pull/2904).
+
+### Creating an ssl context needs no network
+
+- **Symptom:** the game ends right after `nnMain`, with a crash in its own cleanup on an object it had not created yet (DRAGON QUEST BUILDERS 2: `CRASH sig=11 addr=0x60db0` at `main+0x2676c8`, x0 = 0, the callers `main+0x85210` and `main+0x7fabf8`). No red line.
+- **Cause:** `ssl::Context::Create` failed in the host's offline stubs. A context is a session of the console's own ssl service, so it succeeds on Horizon with no network; only a connection needs one. The game's online library treats the failure as fatal, and its startup (`main+0x84a60`) returns false.
+- **Fix:** `Context::Create` and `Context::Destroy` succeed and connect to nothing (`hle_net.c`). `Connection::Create` and the rest still fail, as they do with no network.
+- **Found on:** [DRAGON QUEST BUILDERS 2](../../dm/games/010042000A986000/README.md) (SDK 5.4.0). 53 of 82 games surveyed import the call.
+
+### `EventFd` needs no network
+
+- **Symptom:** `abort` under `grpc_init` at startup, with no red line: `game called nn::diag abort: Result 0, call site sdk+0xb8fe8`, backtrace `sdk+0xba138 sdk+0x7bd670 sdk+0x777f30 main+0x21f0c80`, just after `fs mount _npln:` (Ocarina of Time).
+- **Cause:** gRPC, which `nn::npln` links, wakes its pollers with `nn::socket::EventFd`. The host failed that with ENETDOWN, so gRPC found no wakeup descriptor, its poll engine's factory (`main+0x21f1280`) returned null and the engine selection called `abort` (`main+0x21f0c7c`). An event descriptor is local to the bsd service, so on Horizon it works with no network; only a connection needs one.
+- **Fix:** `EventFd`, `EventFdRead` and `EventFdWrite` are host counters, `Poll` waits on them with a condition variable and `Close` frees them (`hle_net.c`). They share the BSD descriptor table with [local UDP sockets](#udp-loopback-works-offline). An endless wait comes back with 0 after 100 ms, as it does with nothing to wait on.
+- **Check:** `make -C dm/host/tests GAME=<id> run` runs `t_net_eventfd`.
+- **Found on:** [The Legend of Zelda: Ocarina of Time](../../dm/games/0400486024DEC000/README.md) (SDK 22.2.8).
+
+### Event descriptors in an `fd_set`
+
+- **Symptom:** `CRASH sig=10` (SIGBUS) in a network thread right after start (Minecraft's `NetherNet Network`, `main+0x18b2ae8`, `x0=0x4000`), at a load from its own stack.
+- **Cause:** the host numbered its event descriptors from `0x4000`. The bsd service gives out the lowest free number from 0, and games size their tables for such small numbers: NetherNet puts its event descriptor in a 1024-bit `fd_set` on the stack for `Select`, and `FD_SET(0x4000)` reads and writes 2 KB past the set. `Select` also failed whenever a descriptor was set, so it could never wait on one.
+- **Fix:** the event descriptors are numbered as the service numbers them, the lowest free one from 0 (`hle_net.c`). `Select` (`dm_sk_select`, both overloads) is `Poll` over the descriptors in its sets: it waits on the host's event descriptors, a descriptor the host never made has nothing to say, the sets come back with the ready ones, and a descriptor closed during the wait fails with EBADF. As a game's own stale or never-valid descriptors now fall in the same range, `Poll` reports a closed descriptor (`POLLNVAL`) only for one that was open when the wait began.
+- **Check:** `t_net_eventfd` (`make -C dm/host/tests GAME=<id> run`).
+- **Found on:** Minecraft (Bedrock Edition, 1.21.120).
+
+### UDP loopback works offline
+
+- **Symptom:** New Game stays on the loading artwork with an empty progress bar while rendering and loading tips continue (Divinity: Original Sin 2). Both `nn::socket::Socket(AF_INET, SOCK_DGRAM, 0)` calls fail, so RakNet never completes initialization.
+- **Cause:** the host treated every socket as remote networking and returned ENETDOWN. RakNet's startup tests a local UDP endpoint by sending a four-byte datagram to itself at `127.0.0.1`; this works on an offline console. The missing test leaves the game's single-player client and server waiting. The same cause was independently documented in [DroidPlay Core's Divinity note](https://github.com/enjoyerofslop/droidplay-core/blob/main/dm/games/010027400CDC6000/README.md).
+- **Fix:** `hle_net.c` serves IPv4 UDP endpoints inside the process, with BSD sockaddr/flags/errors, local binding and ephemeral ports, datagram queues, connected send/receive, nonblocking calls and `Poll` / `Select`. The enum, plain-argument and C entry points share this service with EventFd. Queues are bounded; close wakes blocked readers and pollers, and descriptor generations prevent waits from attaching to a reused slot. Remote traffic and name resolution remain unavailable. No game key or fixed address is involved: this is host binding, its loosest correct binding.
+- **Check:** `t_net_loopback` exercises the four-byte startup test, two-endpoint exchange, connected filtering, peek/truncation, nonblocking and timed receive, remote rejection, mixed EventFd readiness, and close/reuse during waits; `t_net_eventfd` retains the existing event-descriptor checks.
+- **Found on:** [Divinity: Original Sin 2 - Definitive Edition](../../dm/games/010027400CDC6000/README.md) (update 1.0.12, SDK 9.3.0).
+
+### The local user's profile image
+
+- **Symptom:** `Break#1 reason=0 Result=0` right after the first save is created, then exit 42; the stack has `nn::diag::detail::OnAssertionFailure` under `nn::image::JpegDecoder::Decode` (`sdk+0x394e4`), called from the game (Burnout Paradise Remastered, SDK 9.3).
+- **Cause:** `account::LoadProfileImage` failed with 0 bytes ("the host has none"). Every user on a console has a profile image, a 256x256 JPEG, and the game hands the buffer to `JpegDecoder` without checking the Result.
+- **Fix:** `hle_account.c` answers with the local user's image, a plain 256x256 baseline JPEG of 670 bytes built into the host: the size is the image's, the copy what the buffer holds.
+- **Found on:** Burnout Paradise Remastered (`0100DBF01000A000`, no profile; [issue #1118](https://github.com/seedds/nxnative/issues/1118)), from the reporter's symbolized stack; not run here.
+
+### Mii database answered by the host
+
+- **Symptom:** `[RED] sf: the game connects to service mii:e … mii:u itself`, then an abort.
+- **Cause:** `nn::mii::Database` keeps an `IDatabaseService` of the mii service at +0 (`Database::Initialize`, `sdk+0x1c5664`), and `AppendMii` / `ShowMiiEdit` start the MiiEdit applet.
+- **Fix:** `hle_mii.c` takes over the public methods: a console with no Mii of its own (an empty database: no index, nothing to update) that still has the six default Miis every console has. A CharInfo the host builds is the default Mii's parameters with Switch common colours, a create ID with version 4 and variant bits (fixed per default Mii, so a saved choice finds the same Mii; fresh for `BuildRandom`, which the title's demo race calls for 12 racers) and the nickname "no name"; every field is within `CharInfoRaw::Verify`'s ranges (`sdk+0x1b68c8`). `ShowMiiEdit` is answered as the editor closed with no change; `AppendMii` makes a Mii ([A Mii made in the editor's place](#a-mii-made-in-the-editors-place)).
+- **Found on:** [Mario Kart 8 Deluxe](../../dm/games/0100152000022000/README.md) (4.0.0, SDK 4.4.0; one boot). The game asks for the default Miis with `Get(…, 6, SourceFlag 2)` and never reads the count (`main+0x81e954`): its boot-time list of Mii icons (`main+0x419dc8`) builds a head model for each of the six, and zeroed entries failed the model's `CharInfoAccessor::IsValid` (`CharModel::Initialize` returned 0xd67e, `main+0x8f15b0`) and crashed at `main+0x8f152c`.
+- **Note:** the heads' model data is a stand-in with every part empty ([System data stand-in for the MiiModel archive](#system-data-stand-in-for-the-miimodel-archive)).
+
+### A Mii made in the editor's place
+
+- **Symptom:** `mii: AppendMii (the host has no Mii editor: cancelled)` at each press, and the game goes back to where it asked for a Mii (Mario Golf: Super Rush's Golf Adventure: its save slots).
+- **Cause:** `nn::mii::AppendMii` starts the MiiEdit library applet to make a new Mii, and a game that needs one (Golf Adventure is played as a Mii) asks for it when the database holds none. The host has no editor, and answered "cancelled".
+- **Fix:** the host answers as a player who saves the editor's starting Mii: the system's default Mii (male, "no name") with a create ID of its own, added to the database after the console's Miis, and `*index` is its place there (`hle_mii.c` `h_mii_Append`). `IsUpdated` is true once after it for a question that includes the database. Such Miis are kept for every game in `<DM_SYSTEM_DIR>/host/MiiDatabase.dat`, a file of the host's own format ("DMMI", version 1, the count, then the CharInfos), read at the next start beside the console's database, which is never written. A full database (100) cancels, as the editor cannot add one then.
+- **Found on:** [Mario Golf: Super Rush](../../dm/games/0100C9C00E25C000/README.md) (4.0.0): Golf Adventure shows its own "Select a Mii" with the new Mii, starts, and plays its opening in the village (Mac, M6). A Mii shows without a head unless the player supplies the head data ([A console's own Mii database and system data](#a-consoles-own-mii-database-and-system-data)). [Issue #2361](https://github.com/seedds/nxnative/issues/2361) found that the game needs the call to succeed. Also called by Super Smash Bros. Ultimate and Nintendo Switch Sports Resort's `main` (not run).
+- **Check:** `make -C dm/host/tests GAME=<id> build/t_mii && (cd dm/host/tests && build/t_mii)`: the Mii is added after the console's, listed, found by its create ID, kept in the host's file and read back at the next start; the console's file is not written; a damaged host file is refused; a full database cancels.
+
+### A console's own Mii database and system data
+
+- **Symptom:** the game shows only the six default Miis, and a Switch 2 game logs `[RED] fs mount system data MiiResource: the host has no 0400000000000849` (Nintendo Switch Sports Resort: Mii heads cannot be drawn).
+- **Cause:** both live on the console: the Miis in the mii service's system save, the head textures and shapes in a system data archive. The host ships neither.
+- **Fix:** a player can supply their own, under `DM_SYSTEM_DIR` (the Mac launcher sets `~/Library/Application Support/nxnative/system`, the iOS app `Documents/system`; elsewhere `$DM_ROOT/run/platform/system`):
+  - `sysdata/<system data ID, 16 hex digits>/`: an archive's RomFS files. `MountSystemData` mounts it read-only (`hle_fs.c` `sysdata_own`), ahead of the host's Mii model stand-in (MiiModel, and the Switch 2's MiiResource: [The Switch 2's Mii model archive](#the-switch-2s-mii-model-archive)).
+  - `save/8000000000000030/MiiDatabase.dat`: the mii service's database. The Switch 2's service (program `0400000000000039`) mounts system save `8000000000000030` as `mii` and reads `mii:/MiiDatabase.dat`, as the Switch does. The file is 0x1a98 bytes: `NFDB`, 100 StoreData of 0x44 bytes, the version (1) at +0x1a94, the count at +0x1a95, and a CRC-16/CCITT (polynomial 0x1021, initial 0) of the bytes before it at +0x1a96, big-endian. `hle_mii.c` reads it once at `Database::Initialize`. Each StoreData's seven words of bit fields, nickname and create ID become a CharInfo (`mii_from_store`; the stored eyebrow y is 3 below the CharInfo's). `Get` lists them before the six default Miis (source 0, then 1), `GetIndex` and `UpdateLatest` find them by create ID, and `IsFullDatabase` is true at 100. A file of another size, magic, version or CRC is logged and leaves the database empty. The file is never written: Miis a game adds go to a file of the host's own ([A Mii made in the editor's place](#a-mii-made-in-the-editors-place)).
+- **Found on:** [Nintendo Switch Sports Resort](../../dm/games/04008E001F098000/README.md) (v65536). The save's ID, mount name and file checks were read from the Switch 2's system program `0400000000000039`.
+  - **The archive it needs:** the game mounts system data `0400000000000849` as `MiiResource:` and reads `ShapeMid.dat` / `ShapeHigh.dat` and `OunceTexture{Low,Mid,High}{Linear,SRGB}.dat`. Its code also names the four `NXTexture*` files of the Switch's MiiModel (`0100000000000802`), but it picks the texture file from a table by a flag (`main+0x1cdeb50`) that its Mii setup (`main+0x320040`) only ever clears, so it always reads the `OunceTexture*` files: the Switch's archive, or the host's stand-in of it, cannot take its place. Without the archive the setup function's size query fails and it mounts nothing, and the game shows no Mii heads; the host now mounts its stand-in there, whose `OunceTexture*` files are blank, so the heads still draw nothing ([The Switch 2's Mii model archive](#the-switch-2s-mii-model-archive)).
+  - **Run:** with a console's `0400000000000849` (its eight files) and a database of one Mii in place, the game mounts the archive, lists the Mii first on Select User, and draws Mii heads there, on its Customize screen and in play (M4 Pro, macOS 27.2).
+- **Check:** `make -C dm/host/tests GAME=<id> build/t_mii && dm/host/tests/build/t_mii`: a database with known fields unpacks into them; a wrong CRC, size or version is refused; `Get`, `GetCount`, `GetIndex` and `UpdateLatest` answer from it.
